(window.__KSHC_DATA=window.__KSHC_DATA||{})["medBank"]={"questions":[{"id":"med-cardio-001","prompt":"A 59-year-old man with hypertension and type 2 diabetes presents with 50 minutes of crushing chest pressure radiating to the left arm. Blood pressure is 142/88 mm Hg and pulse is 96/min. Lungs are clear. ECG shows 2-mm ST-segment elevation in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6, with reciprocal ST depression in leads II, III, and aVF. Occlusion of which of the following arteries is most likely responsible?","choices":["Left anterior descending artery","Right coronary artery","Posterior descending artery","Left main coronary artery","Left circumflex artery"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"ST elevation in I, aVL, V5, and V6 localizes the infarct to the lateral wall, which is supplied mainly by the left circumflex artery (with contributions from diagonal branches of the LAD). LAD occlusion produces ST elevation across V1–V4 (anteroseptal/anterior wall), whereas RCA occlusion produces elevation in II, III, and aVF—the leads that are reciprocally depressed here. The posterior descending artery, usually arising from the RCA, supplies the inferior wall and posterior third of the septum. Left main occlusion typically causes diffuse ST depression with ST elevation in aVR and is often accompanied by cardiogenic shock.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["acute-coronary-syndrome","ecg-localization","coronary-anatomy"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Lateral Wall Myocardial Infarction (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537228/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-002","prompt":"A 66-year-old woman with ischemic cardiomyopathy (left ventricular ejection fraction 28%) has NYHA class II symptoms. She takes sacubitril-valsartan, metoprolol succinate at target dose, spironolactone, and furosemide as needed. She has not been hospitalized in the past year. Blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg and pulse is 62/min in sinus rhythm. Serum potassium is 4.6 mEq/L and eGFR is 52 mL/min/1.73 m². She does not have diabetes. Which of the following is the most appropriate addition to reduce her risk of death and heart failure hospitalization?","choices":["Ivabradine","Dapagliflozin","Vericiguat","Lisinopril","Hydralazine–isosorbide dinitrate"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin) are one of the four pillars of guideline-directed therapy for HFrEF, alongside an ARNI/ACE inhibitor/ARB, an evidence-based beta-blocker, and a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist; they reduce cardiovascular death and HF hospitalization regardless of diabetes status (DAPA-HF, EMPEROR-Reduced) and carry a class 1 recommendation in the 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA guideline. Ivabradine is reserved for patients in sinus rhythm with a resting heart rate ≥70/min despite maximally tolerated beta-blockade, and vericiguat is considered after a recent worsening-HF event. Adding lisinopril to sacubitril-valsartan is contraindicated because of angioedema risk. Hydralazine–isosorbide dinitrate is added for self-identified Black patients with persistent symptoms on optimized therapy or for patients who cannot take RAS inhibitors.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["heart-failure","hfref-gdmt","sglt2-inhibitors"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Sodium-Glucose Transport 2 (SGLT2) Inhibitors (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576405/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-003","prompt":"A 61-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction takes lisinopril 20 mg daily. His cardiologist plans to replace lisinopril with sacubitril-valsartan and instructs him to wait at least 36 hours after his last lisinopril dose before taking the first dose of the new drug. This washout period is primarily intended to prevent which of the following?","choices":["Hyperkalemia due to suppression of aldosterone","Angioedema due to accumulation of bradykinin","Rebound hypertension due to a renin surge","Acute kidney injury due to efferent dilation","Cough due to accumulation of substance P"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Both ACE (kininase II) and neprilysin degrade bradykinin, so overlapping exposure to an ACE inhibitor and sacubitril (a neprilysin inhibitor) markedly raises bradykinin levels and the risk of angioedema; the combination is contraindicated and a 36-hour washout is required when switching. For the same reason sacubitril is paired with an ARB (valsartan) rather than an ACE inhibitor. Hyperkalemia and a fall in GFR are risks of any RAS blockade but are not what the washout prevents. Cough is an ACE inhibitor effect that does not require any washout before switching.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["heart-failure","sacubitril-valsartan","angioedema","pharmacology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Sacubitril-Valsartan (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507904/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-004","prompt":"A 17-year-old boy collapses during a basketball game and regains consciousness within a minute. His uncle died suddenly at age 30. Examination shows a harsh crescendo–decrescendo systolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that becomes louder when he stands up from a squat and softer with sustained handgrip. There is no midsystolic click, and the carotid upstroke is brisk with a bifid contour. Which of the following is the most likely underlying abnormality?","choices":["Myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve leaflets","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy with outflow obstruction","Congenitally bicuspid aortic valve with stenosis","Defect in the membranous interventricular septum","Left coronary artery arising from the right sinus"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A systolic murmur that increases when preload falls (standing, Valsalva) and decreases when afterload or preload rises (handgrip, squatting) is the signature of hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy: asymmetric septal hypertrophy plus systolic anterior motion of the mitral leaflet creates dynamic outflow obstruction that worsens as the ventricle gets smaller. It is usually autosomal dominant (e.g., MYH7 or MYBPC3 sarcomere mutations), fitting his family history of sudden death. Mitral valve prolapse also gets louder with standing, but it is an apical late-systolic murmur preceded by a midsystolic click. Aortic stenosis softens with standing and causes a slow, weak carotid upstroke, and a VSD murmur is holosystolic and louder with handgrip.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["hypertrophic-cardiomyopathy","murmur-maneuvers","sudden-cardiac-death"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430788/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-005","prompt":"A 78-year-old man has had 3 months of exertional chest tightness and one episode of near-syncope while climbing stairs. He has a grade 3/6 late-peaking crescendo–decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to both carotids, a soft single S2, and a delayed, diminished carotid upstroke. Transthoracic echocardiography shows a calcified trileaflet aortic valve with a valve area of 0.7 cm², a mean gradient of 48 mm Hg, and a left ventricular ejection fraction of 60%. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Repeat echocardiography in 6–12 months","Balloon aortic valvuloplasty alone","Aortic valve replacement","Beta-blocker and sublingual nitroglycerin","ACE inhibitor titrated to reduce afterload"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Severe aortic stenosis (valve area ≤1.0 cm², mean gradient ≥40 mm Hg) with symptoms—angina and exertional presyncope—is a class 1 indication for aortic valve replacement in the 2020 ACC/AHA valve guideline; at 78, the heart team chooses between transcatheter and surgical replacement. Once symptoms appear, average survival without replacement is only about 2–3 years. Surveillance echocardiography is appropriate only for asymptomatic severe stenosis with preserved ejection fraction. Balloon valvuloplasty gives transient benefit and serves only as a bridge, and nitrates or aggressive afterload reduction can provoke hypotension and syncope because the fixed obstruction prevents a compensatory rise in stroke volume.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["aortic-stenosis","valvular-disease","tavr"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Aortic Stenosis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557628/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-006","prompt":"A 54-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer presents with dyspnea and lightheadedness. Blood pressure is 88/64 mm Hg, pulse is 124/min, and jugular venous pressure is 16 cm H2O with a blunted y descent. Heart sounds are distant. Her systolic blood pressure falls by 18 mm Hg during inspiration. Echocardiography shows a large circumferential pericardial effusion. Which of the following best explains the inspiratory fall in systolic pressure?","choices":["Fixed pericardium blocks inspiratory filling of the RV","Pericardial fluid compresses the left main coronary artery","Increased RV filling shifts the septum toward the LV","Inspiratory pulmonary vasoconstriction reduces LV preload","Inspiration triggers reflex vagal slowing of the heart"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In tamponade the tense effusion fixes total cardiac volume, so the two ventricles compete for space (ventricular interdependence). During inspiration venous return to the right heart rises, and the RV can expand only by bowing the interventricular septum into the LV, which reduces LV filling, stroke volume, and systolic pressure by more than 10 mm Hg (pulsus paradoxus). Failure of the RV to accept inspiratory venous return because of a rigid pericardium describes constrictive pericarditis, which produces Kussmaul sign and a prominent y descent rather than marked pulsus paradoxus. Inspiration normally speeds the heart rate (sinus arrhythmia) rather than slowing it.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["cardiac-tamponade","pulsus-paradoxus","pericardial-disease"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cardiac Tamponade (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431090/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-007","prompt":"A 68-year-old man is hospitalized with 3 weeks of fevers and fatigue. He has a new holosystolic apical murmur. Three of three blood culture sets grow Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis biotype I). Transesophageal echocardiography shows an 8-mm vegetation on the anterior mitral leaflet. Hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL with a mean corpuscular volume of 74 fL. Appropriate intravenous antibiotics are started. Which of the following additional evaluations is most appropriate?","choices":["Upper endoscopy","Panoramic dental radiograph","Colonoscopy","Bone marrow biopsy","Abdominal ultrasonography"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Streptococcus gallolyticus bacteremia or endocarditis is strongly associated with colorectal adenomas and carcinoma, which provide a portal of entry through the colonic mucosa, so every patient should undergo colonoscopy; the microcytic anemia here also points to occult colonic blood loss. Viridans group streptococci, not S. gallolyticus, suggest a dental source. Upper endoscopy may find a cause of iron deficiency but would miss the colonic lesion this organism specifically signals. By analogy, enterococcal endocarditis prompts evaluation of the genitourinary tract.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["infective-endocarditis","streptococcus-gallolyticus","colorectal-cancer"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Infective Endocarditis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557641/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-008","prompt":"A 29-year-old woman has exertional dyspnea. Examination shows a systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border and a widely split S2 that does not vary with respiration. Right heart catheterization oxygen saturations are: superior vena cava 68%, right atrium 83%, right ventricle 84%, pulmonary artery 84%, and left ventricle 97%. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Perimembranous ventricular septal defect","Ostium secundum atrial septal defect","Patent ductus arteriosus","Isolated pulmonary valve stenosis","Ventricular septal defect with shunt reversal"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An oxygen saturation step-up shows where oxygenated blood enters the right heart; here it occurs between the superior vena cava and the right atrium, localizing a left-to-right shunt to the atrial level. The fixed split S2 (increased RV volume delays pulmonic closure in both phases of respiration) and the pulmonic flow murmur fit an ostium secundum ASD. A VSD would produce the step-up in the right ventricle, and a PDA in the pulmonary artery. Pulmonary stenosis causes no step-up, and shunt reversal (Eisenmenger physiology) would lower the left ventricular saturation rather than leave it at 97%.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["congenital-heart-disease","atrial-septal-defect","cardiac-catheterization"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Atrial Septal Defect (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535440/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-009","prompt":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing hypertension develops sudden tearing pain between his shoulder blades. Blood pressure is 182/104 mm Hg in both arms and pulse is 98/min. Peripheral pulses are intact, and he has no neurologic deficits. Serum creatinine and lactate are normal. CT angiography shows an intimal flap beginning distal to the left subclavian artery and extending to the renal arteries; the ascending aorta and arch are normal, and all branch vessels fill normally. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["IV esmolol, then a vasodilator if pressure remains high","IV nitroprusside alone to lower pressure rapidly","Emergency open repair of the descending aorta","Emergency thoracic endovascular aortic repair","IV heparin infusion and admission to telemetry"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"This is an uncomplicated Stanford type B dissection (no ascending involvement, malperfusion, rupture, or refractory pain), which is managed medically: IV beta-blockade (esmolol or labetalol) to a heart rate of about 60/min and a systolic pressure of about 100–120 mm Hg lowers aortic wall stress (dP/dt), and a vasodilator such as nicardipine or nitroprusside is added only after beta-blockade. A vasodilator given first causes reflex tachycardia that increases shear force and can propagate the tear. Emergency surgery is required for type A dissection, and endovascular repair (TEVAR) is reserved for complicated type B dissection. Anticoagulation is contraindicated.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["aortic-dissection","type-b-dissection","hypertension"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Aortic Dissection (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441963/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-010","prompt":"A 46-year-old woman who grew up in Guatemala has palpitations. ECG shows atrial fibrillation with a ventricular rate of 88/min. She has an opening snap followed by a low-pitched mid-diastolic rumble at the apex. Echocardiography shows rheumatic mitral stenosis with a valve area of 1.2 cm². She has no hypertension, diabetes, heart failure, vascular disease, or prior stroke, and her kidney function is normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate long-term antithrombotic therapy?","choices":["Apixaban at the standard dose","Dabigatran at the standard dose","Aspirin 81 mg daily","Warfarin with a target INR of 2.0–3.0","None, since her score is only 1"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Atrial fibrillation with moderate-to-severe rheumatic mitral stenosis carries a very high thromboembolic risk, and anticoagulation with a vitamin K antagonist is indicated regardless of the CHA2DS2-VASc score (2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS AF guideline; 2020 ACC/AHA valve guideline). DOACs such as apixaban and dabigatran were excluded from the pivotal AF trials in this population, and in the INVICTUS trial rivaroxaban was inferior to warfarin for rheumatic heart disease–associated AF. Her CHA2DS2-VASc score of 1 (female sex only) would argue against anticoagulation only in AF without moderate-to-severe mitral stenosis or a mechanical valve. Aspirin does not adequately prevent cardioembolic stroke.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["atrial-fibrillation","mitral-stenosis","anticoagulation","cha2ds2-vasc"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Mitral Stenosis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430742/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-011","prompt":"An 81-year-old woman with heart failure and atrial fibrillation takes digoxin, furosemide, and metoprolol. She presents with nausea, anorexia, and yellow halos around lights. Serum potassium is 2.9 mEq/L, creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (her baseline), and the digoxin level is 2.4 ng/mL. ECG shows frequent premature ventricular complexes. Which of the following best explains how her potassium level contributes to digoxin toxicity?","choices":["Low K+ displaces digoxin from plasma albumin","Low K+ inhibits hepatic metabolism of digoxin","Low K+ increases vagal tone at the AV node","Low K+ directly opens cardiac L-type Ca2+ channels","Less K+ competes with digoxin for the Na+/K+-ATPase"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Digoxin binds the extracellular K+-binding site of the Na+/K+-ATPase, and K+ competes for that site, so hypokalemia (here from furosemide) increases digoxin binding and toxicity even at modestly elevated levels; conversely, acute digoxin overdose causes hyperkalemia by blocking the pump. Pump inhibition raises intracellular Na+, which reduces Na+/Ca2+ exchange and loads the myocyte with Ca2+, producing delayed afterdepolarizations and ventricular ectopy. Digoxin is excreted largely unchanged by the kidneys and is only about 25% protein bound, so albumin displacement and hepatic metabolism are not relevant. Increased vagal tone at the AV node is a therapeutic effect of digoxin, not the way hypokalemia worsens toxicity.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["digoxin-toxicity","hypokalemia","antiarrhythmics","pharmacology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cardiac Glycoside and Digoxin Toxicity (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459165/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-012","prompt":"A 52-year-old man who stopped his antihypertensive medications 2 months ago presents with severe headache, blurred vision, and confusion. Blood pressure is 232/138 mm Hg in both arms. Funduscopy shows bilateral papilledema and flame hemorrhages. Serum creatinine is 2.1 mg/dL (baseline 1.0 mg/dL), and urinalysis shows 2+ protein and red blood cells. CT of the head shows no hemorrhage, and there is no evidence of aortic dissection. Which of the following is the most appropriate blood pressure management?","choices":["IV labetalol to reach <140/90 mm Hg within 1 hour","IV nicardipine to lower MAP by ≤25% in the first hour","Oral amlodipine and clonidine with recheck in 24 hours","Sublingual nifedipine to lower pressure within minutes","IV hydralazine boluses to normalize pressure by 6 hours"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Severe hypertension with acute target-organ damage (encephalopathy, grade IV retinopathy, acute kidney injury) is a hypertensive emergency requiring ICU admission and a titratable IV agent such as nicardipine, clevidipine, or labetalol. The 2017 ACC/AHA guideline advises lowering MAP by no more than 25% in the first hour, then to about 160/100–110 mm Hg over the next 2–6 hours, and toward normal over 24–48 hours, because chronically shifted cerebral and renal autoregulation makes rapid normalization cause ischemia. Faster targets apply only to aortic dissection, severe preeclampsia or eclampsia, and pheochromocytoma crisis. Immediate-release nifedipine and hydralazine boluses cause unpredictable, precipitous drops, and oral therapy is reserved for severe hypertension without target-organ injury.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["hypertensive-emergency","antihypertensives","nicardipine"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Hypertensive Crisis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507701/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-013","prompt":"A 58-year-old woman is started on amlodipine 10 mg daily for hypertension. Six weeks later her blood pressure is controlled, but she has bilateral pitting ankle edema. She has no dyspnea, jugular venous distension, or proteinuria, and her serum albumin is 4.2 g/dL. Adding furosemide does not reduce the edema. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of her edema?","choices":["Reflex renal sodium retention from systemic vasodilation","Negative inotropy causing systemic venous congestion","Arteriolar > venular dilation raising capillary pressure","Increased capillary permeability from bradykinin","Lymphatic obstruction from drug-induced fibrosis"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers dilate precapillary arterioles much more than postcapillary venules, so hydrostatic pressure in dependent capillary beds rises and fluid filters into the interstitium. Because the edema is not caused by sodium retention or volume overload, diuretics are largely ineffective; lowering the dose or adding an ACE inhibitor or ARB, which dilates venules, works better. Negative inotropy that could cause venous congestion is a feature of the non-dihydropyridines verapamil and diltiazem. Bradykinin-mediated permeability underlies ACE inhibitor angioedema, which is nonpitting and typically involves the lips, tongue, and face.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["antihypertensives","calcium-channel-blockers","edema","pharmacology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Amlodipine (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519508/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-014","prompt":"A 64-year-old man had a non–ST-elevation myocardial infarction 1 year ago. He takes aspirin, metoprolol, lisinopril, and atorvastatin 80 mg daily, which he tolerates without adverse effects and takes consistently. Fasting lipid panel shows total cholesterol 160 mg/dL, LDL cholesterol 92 mg/dL, HDL cholesterol 46 mg/dL, and triglycerides 110 mg/dL. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to reduce his cardiovascular risk?","choices":["Add fenofibrate","Add extended-release niacin","Switch to rosuvastatin 10 mg","Add ezetimibe","Continue the current regimen"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"After an MI he has clinical ASCVD, and his LDL cholesterol of 92 mg/dL remains above goal despite adherence to maximal high-intensity statin therapy. The 2026 ACC/AHA dyslipidemia guideline sets an LDL cholesterol goal below 70 mg/dL for clinical ASCVD (below 55 mg/dL for patients at very high risk) and recommends adding a nonstatin agent when the goal is not met; the 2018 guideline used an LDL threshold of 70 mg/dL or higher for the same step. Ezetimibe is inexpensive, well tolerated, and reduced cardiovascular events when added to a statin in IMPROVE-IT. A PCSK9 monoclonal antibody is the other proven add-on, used when a larger LDL reduction is needed or the goal is still not reached. Adding niacin or fenofibrate to a statin did not reduce cardiovascular events (AIM-HIGH, ACCORD-Lipid). Rosuvastatin 10 mg is only moderate intensity and would be a step down from atorvastatin 80 mg.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["lipid-therapy","secondary-prevention","ezetimibe"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Ezetimibe (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532879/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-015","prompt":"A 76-year-old Black man has progressive exertional dyspnea and leg edema. He had bilateral carpal tunnel release 8 years ago and has lumbar spinal stenosis. ECG shows low QRS voltage in the limb leads. Echocardiography shows concentric left ventricular wall thickening (septum 17 mm) with preserved ejection fraction, biatrial enlargement, and apical sparing of longitudinal strain. Serum and urine immunofixation and the serum free light-chain ratio are normal. Technetium-99m pyrophosphate scintigraphy shows intense myocardial uptake. Which of the following is the most likely pathologic process in his myocardium?","choices":["Deposition of immunoglobulin light chains","Sarcomeric mutation with myocyte disarray","Noncaseating granulomas in the myocardium","Deposition of misfolded transthyretin","Iron accumulation within cardiomyocytes"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Low QRS voltage despite thick ventricular walls, apical sparing of strain, heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, prior bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome, and spinal stenosis indicate cardiac amyloidosis; intense technetium pyrophosphate uptake with no monoclonal protein establishes transthyretin (ATTR) cardiomyopathy without biopsy. The hereditary V122I (Val142Ile) TTR variant is common in older Black patients, and wild-type ATTR occurs in elderly men; TTR stabilizers such as tafamidis improve outcomes. AL amyloidosis from light chains must be excluded first, which the normal immunofixation and free light-chain ratio accomplish. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy from sarcomere mutations produces high, not low, QRS voltage.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["cardiomyopathy","cardiac-amyloidosis","restrictive-cardiomyopathy"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Transthyretin Amyloid Cardiomyopathy (ATTR-CM) (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574531/"}]},{"id":"med-cardio-016","prompt":"A 24-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after a stab wound to the thigh with substantial blood loss. Blood pressure is 92/60 mm Hg and pulse is 122/min; his skin is cool and pale. Arterial PaO2 is 96 mm Hg. Which of the following reflex changes best accounts for his tachycardia and vasoconstriction?","choices":["Decreased carotid sinus firing, increasing sympathetic outflow","Increased carotid sinus firing, increasing sympathetic outflow","Decreased carotid sinus firing, increasing vagal outflow","Increased aortic arch firing via the glossopharyngeal nerve","Activation of carotid body chemoreceptors by hypoxemia"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors are stretch receptors whose firing rate falls when arterial pressure drops. Reduced afferent traffic (carotid sinus via the glossopharyngeal nerve, aortic arch via the vagus nerve) to the nucleus tractus solitarius decreases parasympathetic and increases sympathetic outflow, producing tachycardia, increased contractility, and arteriolar and venous constriction. Increased vagal outflow would slow the heart. Carotid body chemoreceptors respond mainly to a low PaO2, which is normal here.","subject":"cardiovascular","tags":["baroreceptor-reflex","hemorrhagic-shock","cardiovascular-physiology"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Physiology, Baroreceptors (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538172/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-001","prompt":"A 34-year-old man is found unresponsive with pinpoint pupils and a respiratory rate of 6/min after injecting heroin. Arterial blood gas on room air at sea level shows pH 7.21, PaCO2 70 mm Hg, and PaO2 54 mm Hg. Assume a respiratory quotient of 0.8. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of his hypoxemia?","choices":["Ventilation–perfusion mismatch","Right-to-left intrapulmonary shunt","Impaired alveolar–capillary diffusion","Alveolar hypoventilation","Reduced inspired oxygen tension"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The alveolar gas equation gives PAO2 = 150 − (70/0.8) ≈ 62 mm Hg, so the A–a gradient is about 8 mm Hg, which is normal; hypoxemia with a normal A–a gradient means alveolar oxygen itself is low, here from opioid-induced hypoventilation with CO2 retention. V/Q mismatch, shunt, and diffusion limitation all widen the A–a gradient, and shunt is further distinguished by poor correction with supplemental oxygen. Low inspired oxygen tension (high altitude) also preserves the A–a gradient but is accompanied by a low, not high, PaCO2 because hypoxemia drives hyperventilation.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["a-a-gradient","hypoxemia","hypoventilation","opioid-overdose"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Physiology, Alveolar to Arterial Oxygen Gradient (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK545153/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-002","prompt":"A 69-year-old man who is a former smoker has 18 months of progressive exertional dyspnea and dry cough. He has no bird, mold, or occupational dust exposure and no joint or skin symptoms. He has digital clubbing and fine end-inspiratory 'Velcro' crackles at both lung bases. Pulmonary function tests show FEV1 68% predicted, FVC 62% predicted, FEV1/FVC 0.84, total lung capacity 60% predicted, and DLCO 38% predicted. High-resolution CT shows subpleural, basal-predominant reticulation with honeycombing. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia","Centrilobular emphysema","Pulmonary sarcoidosis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"A restrictive pattern (normal-to-high FEV1/FVC with reduced TLC) plus a markedly reduced DLCO indicates parenchymal interstitial disease rather than chest wall or neuromuscular restriction, in which DLCO is preserved. In an older former smoker with clubbing, basal Velcro crackles, a usual interstitial pneumonia pattern (subpleural, basal reticulation with honeycombing), and no exposure or connective tissue disease, the diagnosis is IPF; antifibrotics (nintedanib or pirfenidone) slow FVC decline. Chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis tends to be upper- or mid-lung predominant with centrilobular nodules and air trapping, and NSIP shows ground-glass opacities with little honeycombing. Emphysema produces an obstructive pattern with increased TLC.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["pulmonary-function-tests","interstitial-lung-disease","idiopathic-pulmonary-fibrosis"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448162/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-003","prompt":"A 64-year-old man with a 50-pack-year smoking history has 2 months of difficulty climbing stairs and rising from chairs, dry mouth, and erectile dysfunction. Proximal leg strength is 4/5 and improves transiently after 10 seconds of sustained maximal contraction; deep tendon reflexes are absent at rest but reappear after brief exercise. Serum calcium is normal. Chest CT shows a large central hilar mass with bulky mediastinal lymphadenopathy. Biopsy of the mass is most likely to show which of the following?","choices":["Mucin-producing glands with lepidic growth at the edge","Keratin pearls and intercellular bridges in cell nests","Large anaplastic cells with no glands or keratinization","Small cells with scant cytoplasm and nuclear molding","Uniform cells in organoid nests with rare mitoses"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Proximal weakness that improves with sustained effort, areflexia with post-exercise facilitation, and autonomic symptoms define Lambert–Eaton myasthenic syndrome, in which antibodies against presynaptic P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels reduce acetylcholine release. When it is paraneoplastic, the tumor is almost always small cell lung carcinoma, a central neuroendocrine tumor of heavy smokers with early bulky nodal spread: sheets of small cells with scant cytoplasm, finely granular chromatin, nuclear molding, frequent mitoses, and necrosis that stain for chromogranin and synaptophysin. Squamous cell carcinoma (keratin pearls, intercellular bridges) is also central and smoking-related, but its classic paraneoplastic syndrome is PTHrP-mediated hypercalcemia, and his calcium is normal. Adenocarcinoma (mucin-producing glands, lepidic growth) and large cell carcinoma are usually peripheral. A typical carcinoid (organoid nests, rare mitoses) is an indolent tumor that is not related to smoking and would not present with bulky nodal disease.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["lung-cancer","small-cell-lung-cancer","paraneoplastic-syndromes","lambert-eaton"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Small Cell Lung Cancer (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482458/"},{"title":"Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507891/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-004","prompt":"A 38-year-old woman has 3 months of dry cough, fatigue, and tender red nodules on both shins. Chest radiograph shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. Serum calcium is 11.6 mg/dL, phosphorus 4.1 mg/dL, PTH 9 pg/mL (normal 10–65), 25-hydroxyvitamin D 28 ng/mL, and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D 88 pg/mL (normal 18–72). Transbronchial biopsy shows noncaseating granulomas. Which of the following is the most likely cause of her hypercalcemia?","choices":["Secretion of PTH-related peptide by granulomas","Autonomous secretion by a parathyroid adenoma","Extrarenal 1α-hydroxylase activity in macrophages","Excess ingestion of vitamin D supplements","Osteoclast activation by granulomas in bone"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Activated macrophages in sarcoid granulomas express 1α-hydroxylase that is not regulated by PTH, so they convert 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol and increase intestinal calcium absorption, producing hypercalcemia with suppressed PTH and an elevated 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D despite a normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D. Glucocorticoids correct it by suppressing granulomatous 1α-hydroxylase. PTHrP-mediated hypercalcemia (e.g., squamous cell carcinoma) also suppresses PTH but lowers calcitriol, and a parathyroid adenoma would cause an elevated PTH. Vitamin D intoxication from supplements raises 25-hydroxyvitamin D.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["sarcoidosis","hypercalcemia","vitamin-d"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Sarcoidosis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430687/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-005","prompt":"A 47-year-old woman with severe acute pancreatitis develops worsening hypoxemia on hospital day 2. She is intubated; PaO2 is 82 mm Hg on FiO2 0.6 with PEEP of 10 cm H2O. Chest radiograph shows bilateral diffuse alveolar opacities, and echocardiography shows normal left ventricular function. Despite lung-protective ventilation she dies 4 days later. Lung histology would most likely show which of the following?","choices":["Hemosiderin-laden macrophages in the alveoli","Hyaline membranes lining the alveolar walls","Fibroblastic foci with honeycomb change","Granulation tissue plugs in alveolar ducts","Neutrophil-filled alveoli confined to one lobe"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Pancreatitis is a classic indirect trigger of ARDS: bilateral opacities with a PaO2/FiO2 ratio of about 137 on PEEP, not explained by cardiac failure and arising within 1 week of the insult, meet the Berlin definition of moderate ARDS. Neutrophil- and cytokine-mediated injury to alveolar epithelium and capillary endothelium causes protein-rich exudation, and the exudative phase of diffuse alveolar damage is marked by hyaline membranes composed of fibrin and necrotic cell debris. Hemosiderin-laden macrophages suggest chronic pulmonary venous congestion, which the normal echocardiogram argues against. Fibroblastic foci with honeycombing describe usual interstitial pneumonia, and granulation tissue plugs describe organizing pneumonia.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["ards","diffuse-alveolar-damage","pathology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK436002/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-006","prompt":"A 66-year-old man who smokes and has type 2 diabetes develops fever to 39.6°C, nonproductive cough, watery diarrhea, and confusion 5 days after staying at a hotel with a large whirlpool spa. Pulse is 88/min. Serum sodium is 128 mEq/L, and AST and ALT are mildly elevated. Chest radiograph shows patchy right lower lobe consolidation. Sputum Gram stain shows many neutrophils but no organisms. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?","choices":["Legionella pneumophila","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Chlamydophila pneumoniae"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Legionnaires' disease is acquired by inhaling aerosolized water from contaminated sources such as whirlpool spas, cooling towers, and plumbing, and classically affects older smokers or immunocompromised patients with high fever, relative bradycardia, GI symptoms, confusion, hyponatremia, and elevated transaminases. Legionella is a gram-negative intracellular rod that stains poorly, so sputum shows neutrophils without organisms; diagnosis is by urinary antigen (serogroup 1) or culture on buffered charcoal yeast extract with cysteine and iron. Mycoplasma also is invisible on Gram stain (no cell wall) but usually causes milder 'walking pneumonia' in younger adults, without hyponatremia and diarrhea. S. pneumoniae and Klebsiella would appear as gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci and encapsulated gram-negative rods, respectively.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["pneumonia","legionella","microbiology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Legionnaires' Disease (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430807/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-007","prompt":"A 57-year-old man with a BMI of 41 kg/m² has loud snoring, witnessed apneas, and daytime sleepiness that have never been evaluated. He now has progressive exertional dyspnea and leg swelling. Examination shows jugular venous distension, a loud P2, a left parasternal heave, and pitting edema. Echocardiography shows right ventricular hypertrophy and dilation with normal left ventricular size, ejection fraction, and diastolic function. Spirometry and chest CT are normal, and a ventilation-perfusion scan shows no mismatched defects. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of his right heart failure?","choices":["Elevated left atrial pressure transmitted to the lungs","Organized thromboemboli obstructing pulmonary arteries","Chronic hypoxic pulmonary arteriolar vasoconstriction","BMPR2-related proliferation of arterial endothelium","Loss of capillary bed from alveolar wall destruction"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Untreated obstructive sleep apnea, often with obesity hypoventilation, causes repetitive hypoxemia and hypercapnia; alveolar hypoxia constricts pulmonary arterioles (the opposite of the systemic response), and sustained vasoconstriction leads to medial hypertrophy and remodeling, producing WHO group 3 pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale. Normal left ventricular systolic and diastolic function argues against group 2 (left heart) disease, and normal spirometry and CT exclude emphysema or interstitial lung disease. A V/Q scan without mismatched defects excludes chronic thromboembolic disease, and heritable (BMPR2) pulmonary arterial hypertension is diagnosed only after the more common group 2–4 causes are excluded.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["obstructive-sleep-apnea","pulmonary-hypertension","cor-pulmonale"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Pulmonary Hypertension Due to Lung Disease or Hypoxia (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK587358/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-008","prompt":"A 24-year-old woman with asthma uses an albuterol inhaler about 3 times per week for wheeze and chest tightness. She has not awakened at night with symptoms and has had no exacerbations. Spirometry shows FEV1 88% predicted with a significant bronchodilator response. She takes no controller medication. According to GINA recommendations, which of the following is the preferred treatment?","choices":["As-needed low-dose budesonide-formoterol","Continue as-needed albuterol alone","Daily montelukast with as-needed albuterol","Daily medium-dose budesonide-formoterol","Daily tiotropium with as-needed albuterol"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"GINA no longer recommends SABA-only treatment for adults and adolescents because it increases the risk of severe exacerbations and asthma death; every patient should receive an inhaled corticosteroid–containing regimen. For symptoms on fewer than 4–5 days a week without night waking or exacerbations (Steps 1–2), the preferred Track 1 regimen is as-needed low-dose budesonide-formoterol, which reduces severe exacerbations compared with SABA alone and performs similarly to daily low-dose ICS. Montelukast is a less effective alternative with a boxed warning for neuropsychiatric effects, and maintenance medium-dose ICS-LABA or add-on LAMA belong to higher steps. The U.S. NAEPP 2020 update differs slightly, also allowing daily low-dose ICS or as-needed concomitant ICS and SABA at Step 2.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["asthma","gina","ics-formoterol"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Asthma Medications (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK531455/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-009","prompt":"A 68-year-old woman with COPD (post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC 0.56, FEV1 48% predicted) uses a LAMA-LABA inhaler daily with correct technique. In the past year she had two exacerbations treated with oral prednisone and antibiotics, one of which required hospitalization. Her blood eosinophil count is 380 cells/µL. She quit smoking 5 years ago and is up to date on vaccines. She has a chronic cough with daily sputum. According to GOLD recommendations, which of the following is the most appropriate change in pharmacotherapy?","choices":["Add roflumilast to LAMA-LABA","Add azithromycin to LAMA-LABA","Escalate to LAMA-LABA-ICS","Add dupilumab to LAMA-LABA","Switch to ICS-LABA"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"GOLD recommends escalating a patient who continues to exacerbate on LAMA-LABA to triple therapy (LAMA-LABA-ICS) when blood eosinophils are ≥100 cells/µL, with the greatest benefit at ≥300 cells/µL; triple therapy reduced exacerbations and, in IMPACT and ETHOS, all-cause mortality. Roflumilast (for FEV1 <50% with chronic bronchitis) and azithromycin (preferentially in former smokers) are the escalation options when eosinophils are <100 cells/µL. Dupilumab is added for patients who still exacerbate on triple therapy with eosinophils ≥300 cells/µL and chronic bronchitis, so it is premature here. ICS-LABA without a LAMA is not encouraged in COPD; if an ICS is indicated, LAMA-LABA-ICS is preferred.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["copd","gold","triple-therapy","eosinophils"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559281/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-010","prompt":"A 51-year-old woman develops sudden pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea 6 days after a total knee arthroplasty. Blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg, pulse 114/min, respiratory rate 24/min, and oxygen saturation 90% on room air. Her left calf is swollen and tender. Serum creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL, and she has no contrast allergy. ECG shows sinus tachycardia. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in diagnosis?","choices":["Quantitative D-dimer assay","Ventilation-perfusion scan","Transthoracic echocardiography","CT pulmonary angiography","Arterial blood gas analysis"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Her Wells score is about 9 (clinical signs of DVT, PE as the most likely diagnosis, heart rate >100/min, surgery within 4 weeks), so PE is 'likely' and the next step is definitive imaging with CT pulmonary angiography; normal kidney function and no allergy permit contrast. A D-dimer is used only to exclude PE when pretest probability is low or 'unlikely', and it is expected to be elevated after surgery, so it adds nothing here. V/Q scanning is the alternative when iodinated contrast is contraindicated. Echocardiography may show RV strain but cannot exclude PE, and an arterial blood gas is nonspecific.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["pulmonary-embolism","wells-score","ct-pulmonary-angiography"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Acute Pulmonary Embolism (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560551/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-011","prompt":"A 62-year-old man is admitted with a CT-confirmed saddle pulmonary embolism, and a heparin infusion is started. Two hours later he becomes diaphoretic and confused. Blood pressure is 76/48 mm Hg despite a 500-mL saline bolus, pulse is 128/min, and oxygen saturation is 86% on high-flow oxygen. Bedside echocardiography shows a severely dilated, hypokinetic right ventricle. He has no history of stroke, recent surgery, bleeding, or intracranial disease. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Inferior vena cava filter placement","Switch to subcutaneous enoxaparin","Surgical pulmonary embolectomy","Aggressive IV fluids, 3 L of saline","Systemic thrombolysis with alteplase"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Pulmonary embolism with sustained hypotension (systolic <90 mm Hg) and RV failure is high-risk ('massive') PE, category E (cardiopulmonary failure) in the 2026 AHA/ACC classification, and anticoagulation alone is not enough. In a patient without contraindications, systemic thrombolysis (e.g., alteplase 100 mg over 2 hours) is the most rapidly available reperfusion therapy and quickly reduces clot burden and RV afterload. Catheter-based thrombectomy or thrombolysis and surgical embolectomy are alternative reperfusion strategies that depend on immediately available expertise; they are favored when thrombolysis is contraindicated or has failed, and an operation requiring cardiopulmonary bypass is not the first choice in a patient who can receive a thrombolytic at the bedside. An IVC filter does nothing for existing pulmonary obstruction and is indicated mainly when anticoagulation is contraindicated. Large-volume fluid loading overdistends the failing RV, shifts the septum, and further reduces LV filling, so vasopressors such as norepinephrine are preferred after a cautious small bolus.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["pulmonary-embolism","massive-pe","thrombolysis"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Acute Pulmonary Embolism (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560551/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-012","prompt":"A 34-year-old man presents with 3 days of fever, productive cough, and right-sided pleuritic chest pain. He has no medical problems, takes no medications, and has not used antibiotics recently. Temperature is 38.6°C, blood pressure 124/76 mm Hg, pulse 98/min, respiratory rate 20/min, and oxygen saturation 96% on room air. He is alert and oriented. BUN is 12 mg/dL. Chest radiograph shows right lower lobe consolidation. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral levofloxacin as an outpatient","Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate plus azithromycin","Oral amoxicillin as an outpatient","Admission for IV ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Admission for IV vancomycin and cefepime"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"With a CURB-65 score of 0 (no confusion, BUN ≤19 mg/dL, respiratory rate <30/min, normal blood pressure, age <65 years), he can be treated as an outpatient. For healthy adults without comorbidities or recent antibiotic use, the 2019 ATS/IDSA guideline recommends amoxicillin 1 g three times daily, with doxycycline or (where pneumococcal macrolide resistance is <25%) a macrolide as alternatives. Amoxicillin-clavulanate plus a macrolide, or a respiratory fluoroquinolone such as levofloxacin, is reserved for outpatients with comorbidities such as chronic heart, lung, liver, or kidney disease, diabetes, alcohol use disorder, or asplenia. Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin is for hospitalized patients, and anti-MRSA and antipseudomonal coverage requires specific risk factors.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["community-acquired-pneumonia","curb-65","antibiotics"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Community-Acquired Pneumonia (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430749/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-013","prompt":"A 27-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after a motorcycle collision. He is agitated and dyspneic. Blood pressure is 74/40 mm Hg, pulse 138/min, and oxygen saturation 82% on a non-rebreather mask. The trachea is deviated to the left, the right hemithorax is hyperresonant with absent breath sounds, and the neck veins are distended. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Portable chest radiograph to confirm","Immediate right-sided pleural decompression","CT of the chest with IV contrast","Pericardiocentesis for presumed tamponade","Rapid sequence intubation before any procedure"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Hypotension, distended neck veins, tracheal deviation away from the affected side, hyperresonance, and absent breath sounds establish tension pneumothorax, a clinical diagnosis treated immediately by decompression (needle decompression at the 4th–5th intercostal space in the anterior/midaxillary line or the 2nd intercostal space in the midclavicular line, or finger thoracostomy), followed by tube thoracostomy. Waiting for a radiograph or CT delays treatment of an obstructive shock that can rapidly progress to cardiac arrest. Cardiac tamponade also causes hypotension and jugular venous distension but not tracheal deviation or unilateral hyperresonance. Positive-pressure ventilation before decompression can enlarge the pneumothorax and precipitate arrest.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["pneumothorax","tension-pneumothorax","trauma"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Tension Pneumothorax (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559090/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-014","prompt":"A 58-year-old woman comes for a routine health maintenance visit. She smoked 1 pack of cigarettes per day from age 18 to 48 and has not smoked since. She has no respiratory symptoms, and her physical examination is normal. She is in good health and would be willing to undergo lung surgery if needed. Which of the following is the most appropriate lung cancer screening recommendation?","choices":["No screening, since she quit smoking","Annual posteroanterior chest radiograph","One-time low-dose CT of the chest","Sputum cytology every 2 years","Annual low-dose CT of the chest"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"The USPSTF (2021) recommends annual low-dose CT screening for adults aged 50–80 years with at least a 20-pack-year history who currently smoke or quit within the past 15 years; she has 30 pack-years and quit 10 years ago, so she qualifies. Screening stops once a person has not smoked for 15 years or develops a health problem that substantially limits life expectancy or the ability or willingness to have curative surgery. Chest radiography and sputum cytology did not reduce lung cancer mortality in trials, and the mortality benefit in NLST and NELSON came from repeated, not single, low-dose CT screening.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["lung-cancer-screening","uspstf","low-dose-ct"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Lung Cancer: Screening (USPSTF Recommendation)","url":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org/uspstf/recommendation/lung-cancer-screening"}]},{"id":"med-resp-015","prompt":"A 45-year-old man has 2 months of cough, night sweats, and weight loss. He had latent tuberculosis infection diagnosed 10 years ago that was never treated. Chest radiograph shows a cavitary lesion in the apical segment of the right upper lobe. In an upright person, which of the following characteristics of the lung apex best explains why reactivation tuberculosis favors this region?","choices":["High V/Q ratio producing a high alveolar PO2","Low V/Q ratio producing a high alveolar PCO2","Greater blood flow per alveolus than at the base","Greater ventilation per alveolus than at the base","Less negative pleural pressure collapsing apical alveoli"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In the upright lung, gravity makes both ventilation and perfusion greatest at the base, but perfusion falls off much more steeply toward the apex, so the apex has the highest V/Q ratio (about 3) and therefore the highest alveolar PO2 and lowest PCO2. Mycobacterium tuberculosis is an obligate aerobe, so reactivation disease favors the oxygen-rich apical and posterior segments of the upper lobes. The apex actually receives less ventilation and much less blood flow per alveolus than the base. Pleural pressure is more negative at the apex, which holds apical alveoli more distended, not collapsed, at FRC.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["ventilation-perfusion","tuberculosis","pulmonary-physiology"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Physiology, Pulmonary Ventilation and Perfusion (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539907/"}]},{"id":"med-resp-016","prompt":"A family is brought to the emergency department after waking with headache, nausea, and confusion; a gas furnace had been running in their closed home. In the 38-year-old father, pulse oximetry reads 98% on room air. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.32, PaCO2 34 mm Hg, PaO2 96 mm Hg, and a carboxyhemoglobin level of 28%. Serum lactate is elevated. Which of the following best describes his oxygen transport?","choices":["Reduced PaO2 with a right-shifted dissociation curve","Normal O2 content because the PaO2 is normal","Reduced O2 content with a right-shifted dissociation curve","Reduced O2 content with a left-shifted dissociation curve","Normal O2 saturation, as the oximeter reading shows"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin with roughly 200–250 times the affinity of oxygen, so carboxyhemoglobin occupies binding sites and lowers oxygen content even though dissolved oxygen (PaO2) is normal. CO also increases the oxygen affinity of the remaining heme sites, shifting the dissociation curve to the left and impairing unloading to tissues, which explains the lactic acidosis. Standard two-wavelength pulse oximeters read carboxyhemoglobin as oxyhemoglobin, giving a falsely normal SpO2, so co-oximetry is required. Treatment is 100% oxygen, which shortens the CO half-life, with hyperbaric oxygen considered for severe poisoning.","subject":"respiratory","tags":["carbon-monoxide-poisoning","oxygen-dissociation-curve","pulse-oximetry"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Carbon Monoxide Poisoning (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557888/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-001","prompt":"A 29-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after a prolonged generalized seizure. He had injected heroin before the seizure and is now somnolent with a respiratory rate of 8/min. Serum studies show Na+ 140 mEq/L, Cl− 100 mEq/L, HCO3− 12 mEq/L, and lactate 9 mmol/L. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.08 and PaCO2 42 mm Hg. Which of the following best describes his acid–base status?","choices":["Anion gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate compensation","Anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis","Combined anion gap and non-gap metabolic acidosis","Primary respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation","Anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"The anion gap is 140 − (100 + 12) = 28 mEq/L, indicating an anion gap (lactic) metabolic acidosis from the seizure. Winter's formula predicts the PaCO2 expected with appropriate respiratory compensation: 1.5 × 12 + 8 = 26 ± 2 mm Hg; his PaCO2 of 42 mm Hg is far higher, so opioid-induced hypoventilation has added a concurrent respiratory acidosis, explaining the severe acidemia. A PaCO2 below 24 mm Hg would have indicated a concurrent respiratory alkalosis. The delta ratio (ΔAG 16 / ΔHCO3− 12 ≈ 1.3) lies between 1 and 2, so there is no coexisting non-gap acidosis or metabolic alkalosis.","subject":"renal","tags":["acid-base","anion-gap","winters-formula","mixed-disorders"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Anion Gap and Non-Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448090/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-002","prompt":"A 42-year-old man is found confused in his garage. Serum studies show Na+ 139 mEq/L, Cl− 101 mEq/L, HCO3− 8 mEq/L, glucose 90 mg/dL, BUN 14 mg/dL, and creatinine 2.4 mg/dL; measured serum osmolality is 330 mOsm/kg. Ethanol is undetectable. Urinalysis shows numerous envelope-shaped crystals. The most appropriate antidote acts by inhibiting which of the following enzymes?","choices":["Alcohol dehydrogenase","Aldehyde dehydrogenase","Glycolate oxidase","Lactate dehydrogenase","Xanthine oxidase"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"An anion gap metabolic acidosis (gap 30) with a large osmolal gap (measured 330 vs calculated about 288 mOsm/kg), acute kidney injury, and envelope-shaped calcium oxalate dihydrate crystals indicates ethylene glycol poisoning, as from antifreeze. Ethylene glycol itself is relatively nontoxic; alcohol dehydrogenase converts it to glycoaldehyde, which is oxidized to glycolic acid (the main cause of the acidosis) and ultimately oxalic acid, which precipitates with calcium in the renal tubules. Fomepizole competitively inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase and prevents formation of these metabolites, and hemodialysis removes the parent compound and acids in severe cases. Disulfiram, not fomepizole, inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase.","subject":"renal","tags":["toxic-alcohols","ethylene-glycol","osmolal-gap","fomepizole"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Ethylene Glycol Toxicity (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537009/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-003","prompt":"A 46-year-old man has had three episodes of calcium oxalate nephrolithiasis. A 24-hour urine collection on his usual diet shows calcium of 380 mg/day (normal <250 mg/day) with normal oxalate, citrate, and uric acid. Serum calcium and PTH are normal. In addition to increased fluid intake and sodium restriction, his physician prescribes a diuretic to lower his urinary calcium excretion. Which of the following is the primary site and mechanism of action of the most appropriate drug?","choices":["Na+-K+-2Cl− cotransporter blockade in the thick ascending limb","Carbonic anhydrase inhibition in the proximal convoluted tubule","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonism in the collecting duct","Osmotic retention of water in the proximal tubule and loop","Na+-Cl− cotransporter blockade in the distal convoluted tubule"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Thiazide and thiazide-like diuretics (chlorthalidone, hydrochlorothiazide, indapamide) are the diuretics that lower urinary calcium, so they are the class used for idiopathic hypercalciuria with recurrent calcium stones; they block the Na+-Cl− cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule. The resulting fall in intracellular Na+ enhances basolateral Na+/Ca2+ exchange and apical Ca2+ entry in the DCT, and mild volume contraction increases proximal Na+ and Ca2+ reabsorption, which is also why thiazides can cause mild hypercalcemia. Loop diuretics do the opposite: blocking NKCC2 abolishes the lumen-positive potential that drives paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb, causing hypercalciuria. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors alkalinize the urine and promote calcium phosphate stones. Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists and osmotic diuretics are not used to lower urinary calcium or to prevent stones.","subject":"renal","tags":["diuretics","thiazides","nephrolithiasis","hypercalciuria"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Thiazide Diuretics (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532918/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-004","prompt":"A 41-year-old woman with Sjögren syndrome has fatigue and muscle weakness. Serum studies show Na+ 139 mEq/L, K+ 2.8 mEq/L, Cl− 115 mEq/L, HCO3− 15 mEq/L, and creatinine 0.8 mg/dL. Urine pH is 6.4 despite the acidemia, and the urine anion gap is positive. Abdominal radiograph shows bilateral nephrocalcinosis. Which of the following is the most likely underlying defect?","choices":["Impaired HCO3− reabsorption in the proximal tubule","Impaired aldosterone effect in the collecting duct","Gastrointestinal loss of bicarbonate from diarrhea","Impaired H+ secretion by α-intercalated cells","Impaired Na+-K+-2Cl− transport in the loop of Henle"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A normal anion gap (9 mEq/L) metabolic acidosis with hypokalemia, an inappropriately alkaline urine (pH >5.5), a positive urine anion gap reflecting poor NH4+ excretion, and nephrocalcinosis indicates distal (type 1) RTA, in which α-intercalated cells cannot secrete H+, often from autoimmune injury in Sjögren syndrome. Chronic acidosis releases calcium phosphate from bone, increases urinary calcium, and lowers urinary citrate, so calcium phosphate stones form in the alkaline urine. Proximal (type 2) RTA can acidify the urine below pH 5.5 once serum bicarbonate falls below the lowered reabsorptive threshold, and it does not cause stones. Type 4 RTA causes hyperkalemia, and diarrhea produces a negative urine anion gap because ammonium excretion is intact.","subject":"renal","tags":["renal-tubular-acidosis","distal-rta","non-anion-gap-acidosis"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Renal Tubular Acidosis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519044/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-005","prompt":"A 54-year-old man has 6 weeks of progressive leg and scrotal edema. Blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein, oval fat bodies, and no red cell casts. Urine protein is 9.2 g/24 h, serum albumin 2.1 g/dL, and LDL cholesterol 268 mg/dL. Serum complement levels are normal, and anti–phospholipase A2 receptor (PLA2R) antibodies are strongly positive. If a kidney biopsy were performed, which of the following would it most likely show?","choices":["Diffuse foot-process effacement with no immune deposits","Segmental sclerosis and hyalinosis of some glomeruli","Subepithelial deposits with GBM 'spikes' on silver stain","Subendothelial deposits with a 'tram-track' GBM","Mesangial IgA deposits with mesangial proliferation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Nephrotic syndrome in a middle-aged adult with positive anti-PLA2R antibodies indicates primary membranous nephropathy, a leading cause of nephrotic syndrome in nondiabetic adults. Autoantibodies bind the podocyte antigen PLA2R and form immune complexes in situ, producing granular IgG and C3 subepithelial deposits; new basement membrane laid down between them appears as 'spikes' on silver stain, and complement (C5b-9) injures the podocytes. Effacement without deposits is minimal change disease, and subendothelial deposits with GBM duplication describe membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, which usually lowers complement. Patients with membranous nephropathy are at particular risk of venous thrombosis, including renal vein thrombosis.","subject":"renal","tags":["nephrotic-syndrome","membranous-nephropathy","renal-pathology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Membranous Nephropathy (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559169/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-006","prompt":"A 21-year-old man notices cola-colored urine 2 days after the onset of a sore throat and fever. He had a similar episode a year ago that resolved on its own. Blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Urinalysis shows dysmorphic red blood cells, red cell casts, and 1+ protein. Serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL, and C3 and C4 levels are normal. Which of the following is most likely to be seen on kidney biopsy?","choices":["Subepithelial 'humps' containing IgG and C3","Linear IgG staining along the basement membrane","Thin basement membranes with no immune deposits","Mesangial IgA deposits on immunofluorescence","Split, laminated GBM on electron microscopy"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Episodic gross hematuria beginning within 1–2 days of an upper respiratory infection (synpharyngitic hematuria) with normal complement levels is classic for IgA nephropathy, the most common primary glomerulonephritis worldwide; immune complexes containing galactose-deficient IgA1 deposit in the mesangium. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is the key confounder: it follows pharyngitis by 1–3 weeks, lowers C3, and shows subepithelial humps. Linear IgG along the GBM indicates anti-GBM disease. Thin or split basement membranes indicate thin basement membrane disease or Alport syndrome, which usually cause persistent microscopic hematuria, often with a family history and, in Alport syndrome, hearing loss.","subject":"renal","tags":["nephritic-syndrome","iga-nephropathy","glomerulonephritis"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"IgA Nephropathy (Berger Disease) (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538214/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-007","prompt":"A 34-year-old woman has hypertension and intermittent flank discomfort. Her father began dialysis at age 52. Ultrasonography shows bilaterally enlarged kidneys with more than 10 cysts in each kidney. Her total kidney volume places her in a high-risk category, and her eGFR has fallen from 92 to 74 mL/min/1.73 m² over 3 years. Her blood pressure is controlled with lisinopril. Her nephrologist recommends adding the drug specifically approved to slow kidney function decline in this disease. Which of the following is the mechanism of that drug?","choices":["Vasopressin V2 receptor antagonism","mTOR inhibition in cyst epithelial cells","Somatostatin receptor activation","Angiotensin II type 1 receptor blockade","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibition"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (most often PKD1 mutations affecting polycystin-1), cyst epithelial proliferation and fluid secretion are driven by cAMP, which vasopressin raises through V2 receptors in the collecting duct. Tolvaptan, a selective V2 receptor antagonist, is FDA-approved for adults at risk of rapidly progressing ADPKD because it slows growth of total kidney volume and eGFR decline (TEMPO 3:4, REPRISE); its drawbacks are aquaresis with polyuria and thirst and idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity requiring regular liver enzyme monitoring. mTOR inhibitors and somatostatin analogs reduced cyst growth in some trials but did not preserve kidney function and are not approved for this purpose. RAS blockade is the preferred antihypertensive in ADPKD (she already takes lisinopril) but is not a disease-specific therapy, and adding an ARB to an ACE inhibitor is not recommended.","subject":"renal","tags":["polycystic-kidney-disease","adpkd","tolvaptan","pharmacology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532934/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-008","prompt":"A 72-year-old woman is hospitalized with urosepsis and has a blood pressure of 78/44 mm Hg for several hours before responding to fluids and norepinephrine. Over the next 48 hours her urine output falls to 15 mL/h and her creatinine rises from 0.9 to 3.0 mg/dL. She has not received diuretics or contrast. Urine sodium is 52 mEq/L, urine creatinine 40 mg/dL, and serum sodium 138 mEq/L. Kidney ultrasonography shows no hydronephrosis. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Prerenal azotemia","Acute interstitial nephritis","Acute tubular necrosis","Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","Postrenal obstruction"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"FENa = (UNa × PCr)/(PNa × UCr) × 100 = (52 × 3.0)/(138 × 40) × 100 ≈ 2.8%. A FENa above 2% in oliguric AKI after prolonged hypotension means the tubules can no longer reabsorb sodium, consistent with ischemic acute tubular necrosis, which typically shows muddy brown granular casts. In prerenal azotemia, intact tubules avidly retain sodium, giving a FENa below 1% and a BUN-to-creatinine ratio above 20; the fractional excretion of urea (<35%) is preferred when diuretics have been given. Acute interstitial nephritis from her antibiotics would usually appear 1–2 weeks after exposure, often with rash, fever, eosinophilia, or white cell casts, and the absence of hydronephrosis argues against obstruction.","subject":"renal","tags":["acute-kidney-injury","fena","acute-tubular-necrosis"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Acute Kidney Injury (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441896/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-009","prompt":"A 67-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease who takes lisinopril and spironolactone presents with generalized weakness. Serum potassium is 7.6 mEq/L on a nonhemolyzed sample. ECG shows peaked T waves, a prolonged PR interval, and a QRS duration of 150 ms. Blood pressure is 136/80 mm Hg. Which of the following is the most appropriate first step in management?","choices":["IV regular insulin with dextrose","Nebulized high-dose albuterol","Oral sodium zirconium cyclosilicate","IV calcium gluconate","Urgent hemodialysis"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Hyperkalemia with ECG changes (peaked T waves, PR prolongation, QRS widening) is an emergency, and the first step is IV calcium (gluconate or chloride), which stabilizes the cardiac membrane within minutes by restoring the gap between resting and threshold potential, although it does not lower serum potassium. Insulin with dextrose and β2-agonists come next to shift potassium into cells, followed by measures that remove potassium from the body: binders, loop diuretics, or hemodialysis. Starting with shifting or removal therapy leaves the myocardium unprotected for the 15–30 minutes or more those treatments take to work. Lisinopril and spironolactone should be held.","subject":"renal","tags":["hyperkalemia","electrolyte-emergency","calcium-gluconate"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Hyperkalemia (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470284/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-010","prompt":"A 58-year-old man with alcohol use disorder and poor oral intake has had 1 week of worsening confusion and today had a brief generalized seizure. Serum sodium is 108 mEq/L, potassium 2.9 mEq/L, and plasma osmolality 228 mOsm/kg. He receives a 100-mL bolus of 3% saline, and the seizure does not recur. Which of the following is the most appropriate goal for correction of his serum sodium?","choices":["Rise of 10–12 mEq/L in 24 hours, then 18 in 48 hours","Rise to 125 mEq/L within the first 24 hours","Rise of 4–6 mEq/L now, and no more than 8 in 24 hours","Rise of only 2 mEq/L in 24 hours to avoid any risk","Rise to 135 mEq/L within 24 hours using 3% saline"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Severe symptomatic hyponatremia (seizure) warrants hypertonic saline to raise sodium by 4–6 mEq/L quickly, which is usually enough to reverse cerebral edema. Because his hyponatremia is chronic (>48 hours) and he has high-risk features for osmotic demyelination—sodium below 120 mEq/L, alcohol use disorder, malnutrition, and hypokalemia—correction should not exceed 8 mEq/L in any 24-hour period. The 10–12 mEq/L per 24-hour limit applies only to patients at average risk. Overly rapid correction shrinks brain cells that have shed organic osmolytes, causing demyelination of the pons and other regions days later, and potassium repletion also raises serum sodium and must be counted.","subject":"renal","tags":["hyponatremia","osmotic-demyelination","hypertonic-saline"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Hyponatremia (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470386/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-011","prompt":"A 71-year-old man with a 60-pack-year smoking history has mild fatigue. He takes no medications and is euvolemic on examination. Serum sodium is 127 mEq/L, plasma osmolality 262 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality 480 mOsm/kg, and urine sodium 54 mEq/L. TSH and morning cortisol are normal. Chest CT shows a central hilar mass. He is alert and neurologically intact. In addition to evaluating the mass, which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment of his hyponatremia?","choices":["Fluid restriction","0.9% saline infusion","3% saline infusion","Oral tolvaptan","Demeclocycline"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Euvolemic hypotonic hyponatremia with inappropriately concentrated urine (>100 mOsm/kg), urine sodium >30 mEq/L, normal thyroid and adrenal function, and no diuretic use meets criteria for SIADH, here probably from ectopic ADH secretion by small cell lung cancer. For mild-to-moderate, minimally symptomatic SIADH, first-line therapy is fluid restriction (typically <800–1,000 mL/day); oral urea or a vasopressin antagonist such as tolvaptan is added if restriction fails. Isotonic saline often worsens SIADH because the kidney excretes the salt in concentrated urine and retains the free water. Hypertonic saline is reserved for severe or acutely worsening neurologic symptoms.","subject":"renal","tags":["siadh","hyponatremia","small-cell-lung-cancer"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone Secretion (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507777/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-012","prompt":"A 39-year-old woman has 2 days of left flank pain and 1 day of fever and rigors. Temperature is 39.4°C, blood pressure 96/58 mm Hg, and pulse 116/min. WBC count is 19,000/mm³, creatinine 1.4 mg/dL, and urinalysis shows pyuria and bacteriuria. Noncontrast CT shows a 7-mm stone at the left ureterovesical junction with moderate hydronephrosis. Blood and urine cultures are drawn, and IV ceftriaxone and fluids are started. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Tamsulosin and observation for stone passage","Urgent ureteral stent or percutaneous nephrostomy","Ureteroscopic laser lithotripsy today","Shock wave lithotripsy within 24 hours","Continued IV antibiotics alone for 48 hours"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An obstructing ureteral stone with infection (fever, sepsis physiology, pyuria) is a urologic emergency because infected urine trapped behind an obstruction cannot be cleared by antibiotics alone; urgent decompression with a retrograde ureteral stent or percutaneous nephrostomy is required along with broad-spectrum antibiotics (AUA guideline). Definitive stone treatment with ureteroscopy or shock wave lithotripsy is deferred until the infection has been treated, because manipulating an infected system can worsen sepsis. Medical expulsive therapy with tamsulosin is appropriate only for uncomplicated distal stones up to about 10 mm without infection, intractable pain, or significant AKI.","subject":"renal","tags":["nephrolithiasis","obstructive-pyelonephritis","urologic-emergency"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Renal Calculi, Nephrolithiasis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK442014/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-013","prompt":"A 61-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and hypertension has an eGFR of 48 mL/min/1.73 m² and a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio of 640 mg/g on two occasions 3 months apart. He takes metformin and the maximum dose of losartan. Blood pressure is 126/76 mm Hg, HbA1c 7.2%, and potassium 4.7 mEq/L. Which of the following is the most appropriate addition to slow progression of his kidney disease?","choices":["Lisinopril","Amlodipine","Empagliflozin","Hydrochlorothiazide","Aliskiren"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"KDIGO 2022 diabetes-in-CKD guidance and the ADA Standards of Care recommend an SGLT2 inhibitor for patients with type 2 diabetes, CKD, and eGFR ≥20 mL/min/1.73 m², in addition to maximally tolerated RAS blockade; by restoring tubuloglomerular feedback and afferent arteriolar tone, SGLT2 inhibitors lower intraglomerular pressure and reduced kidney failure and death in CREDENCE, DAPA-CKD, and EMPA-KIDNEY. Adding an ACE inhibitor or the direct renin inhibitor aliskiren to an ARB (dual RAS blockade) increases hyperkalemia and AKI without renal benefit. His blood pressure is already below 130/80 mm Hg, so another antihypertensive such as amlodipine or a thiazide is not needed for renoprotection. The nonsteroidal mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist finerenone is a further option if albuminuria persists.","subject":"renal","tags":["chronic-kidney-disease","diabetic-kidney-disease","sglt2-inhibitors","kdigo"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Diabetic Nephropathy (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534200/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-014","prompt":"A 4-year-old boy has 1 week of puffy eyes in the morning and swelling of his legs. Blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein and no blood. Serum albumin is 1.7 g/dL, cholesterol 340 mg/dL, creatinine 0.3 mg/dL, and C3 normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Oral prednisone without biopsy","Percutaneous kidney biopsy","Oral cyclophosphamide","Lisinopril as sole therapy","IV albumin with furosemide"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A young child with nephrotic syndrome, normal blood pressure, no hematuria, normal kidney function, and normal complement most likely has minimal change disease, which responds to glucocorticoids so reliably that empiric oral prednisone is given without biopsy (KDIGO). Biopsy is reserved for steroid-resistant disease or atypical features (age >12 years, hematuria, hypertension, low complement, or reduced GFR), and it is the usual first step in adults, in whom other causes are more common. Cyclophosphamide and other steroid-sparing agents are used for frequently relapsing or steroid-dependent disease. Albumin with furosemide treats refractory edema but does not induce remission.","subject":"renal","tags":["nephrotic-syndrome","minimal-change-disease","pediatrics"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Minimal Change Disease (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560639/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-015","prompt":"A 70-year-old woman with heart failure takes lisinopril and furosemide. She begins taking ibuprofen 800 mg three times daily for knee pain. One week later her serum creatinine has risen from 1.1 to 2.0 mg/dL. Which of the following changes in glomerular hemodynamics best explains how ibuprofen contributed to her decline in GFR?","choices":["Efferent dilation from loss of angiotensin II","Efferent constriction from excess angiotensin II","Afferent dilation from loss of thromboxane A2","Afferent constriction from loss of prostaglandins","Raised Bowman space pressure from tubular blockage"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"When effective circulating volume is reduced (heart failure, diuretics), GFR depends on prostaglandin-mediated afferent arteriolar dilation and angiotensin II–mediated efferent arteriolar constriction. NSAIDs block prostaglandin synthesis, leaving afferent vasoconstriction unopposed and lowering glomerular plasma flow and capillary hydrostatic pressure. ACE inhibitors remove the efferent tone, so an NSAID, an ACE inhibitor, and a diuretic together (the 'triple whammy') predictably reduce GFR. Efferent dilation from loss of angiotensin II is the lisinopril component of the injury, not the effect of ibuprofen.","subject":"renal","tags":["glomerular-filtration","nsaids","renal-physiology","triple-whammy"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Physiology, Glomerular Filtration Rate (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500032/"}]},{"id":"med-renal-016","prompt":"A 26-year-old man runs a marathon on a hot day and drinks little water. That evening his urine is scant and dark, with an osmolality of 1,050 mOsm/kg. Serum sodium is 147 mEq/L. Which of the following cellular events in the kidney is primarily responsible for his concentrated urine?","choices":["Insertion of aquaporin-1 into thin descending limb membranes","Activation of ENaC in principal cells by aldosterone","Increased proximal water reabsorption by angiotensin II","Insertion of aquaporin-2 into collecting duct apical membranes","Opening of urea transporters in the proximal tubule"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Hyperosmolality and volume depletion stimulate ADH release from the posterior pituitary; ADH binds V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells, raising cAMP and causing protein kinase A–dependent insertion of aquaporin-2 channels into the apical membrane, so water moves into the hypertonic medullary interstitium and the urine becomes concentrated (up to about 1,200 mOsm/kg). Aquaporin-1 in the proximal tubule and thin descending limb is constitutive and not regulated by ADH. Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption through ENaC but does not by itself produce maximally concentrated urine. ADH increases urea permeability in the inner medullary collecting duct, not the proximal tubule, which helps build the medullary gradient.","subject":"renal","tags":["antidiuretic-hormone","aquaporin-2","urine-concentration"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Physiology, Vasopressin (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526069/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-001","prompt":"A 74-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes treated with metformin is brought in after 5 days of increasing thirst, polyuria, and confusion during a urinary tract infection. Blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg, and her mucous membranes are dry. Glucose is 1,020 mg/dL, sodium 138 mEq/L, bicarbonate 22 mEq/L, anion gap 13 mEq/L, and serum osmolality 352 mOsm/kg; β-hydroxybutyrate is only mildly elevated. Which of the following best explains the absence of significant ketoacidosis?","choices":["Hyperglycemia suppresses glucagon secretion","Residual insulin secretion suppresses lipolysis","Osmotic diuresis clears ketones as they form","Metformin blocks hepatic ketogenesis","Infection-related cytokines inhibit ketogenesis"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In the hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state, patients with type 2 diabetes still secrete some insulin, and the amount needed to suppress adipose lipolysis—and therefore hepatic ketogenesis from free fatty acids—is far lower than the amount needed to drive peripheral glucose uptake, so profound hyperglycemia and hyperosmolality develop without significant ketoacidosis. In DKA, near-absolute insulin deficiency together with elevated glucagon unleashes lipolysis and hepatic ketone production. Glucagon and other counterregulatory hormones are elevated, not suppressed, in both disorders. Osmotic diuresis does excrete ketones, but ketonuria cannot prevent ketoacidosis when production is unchecked, as DKA demonstrates.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["hyperosmolar-hyperglycemic-state","diabetes-pathophysiology","ketogenesis"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic Syndrome (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482142/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-002","prompt":"A 56-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes undergoes elective cholecystectomy. She fasted before surgery but continued all of her usual diabetes medications. On postoperative day 1 she has nausea, vomiting, and rapid deep breathing. Glucose is 188 mg/dL, sodium 137 mEq/L, chloride 102 mEq/L, bicarbonate 11 mEq/L, β-hydroxybutyrate 6.1 mmol/L (normal <0.6), lactate 1.3 mmol/L, and creatinine 0.9 mg/dL. Which of her medications most likely contributed to this presentation?","choices":["Metformin extended-release","Semaglutide","Glimepiride","Pioglitazone","Canagliflozin"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"An anion gap (24 mEq/L) ketoacidosis with glucose below 250 mg/dL and a normal lactate is euglycemic DKA, a recognized complication of SGLT2 inhibitors precipitated by fasting, surgery, acute illness, or insulin dose reduction. By causing glycosuria, SGLT2 inhibitors lower plasma glucose (masking the usual warning sign) and thereby reduce insulin secretion while raising glucagon, which promotes lipolysis and ketogenesis. The FDA advises stopping canagliflozin, dapagliflozin, and empagliflozin at least 3 days (ertugliflozin 4 days) before scheduled surgery. Metformin-associated acidosis is lactic acidosis, which the normal lactate excludes, and semaglutide's main perioperative concern is delayed gastric emptying with aspiration risk.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["euglycemic-dka","sglt2-inhibitors","diabetes-drugs","perioperative"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Euglycemic Diabetic Ketoacidosis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554570/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-003","prompt":"A 36-year-old woman has palpitations, heat intolerance, and weight loss. She has a diffuse, nontender goiter with a bruit, bilateral proptosis with periorbital edema, and nonpitting thickening of the skin over both shins. TSH is <0.01 µU/mL and free T4 is elevated. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of her proptosis?","choices":["Sympathetic overstimulation of the levator muscle","Lymphocytic infiltration of the lacrimal glands","Thyroid peroxidase antibodies injuring the orbit","TSH-receptor antibodies activating orbital fibroblasts","Thyroid hormone excess stimulating orbital adipocytes"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In Graves disease, stimulating autoantibodies against the TSH receptor (with IGF-1 receptor cross-talk) activate orbital fibroblasts and preadipocytes, which express the TSH receptor; together with T-cell cytokines they secrete hydrophilic glycosaminoglycans such as hyaluronan and differentiate into fat cells, enlarging the extraocular muscles and retro-orbital fat and pushing the globe forward. The same process in dermal fibroblasts causes pretibial myxedema. Sympathetic overactivity explains lid retraction and lid lag in any thyrotoxic state but not true proptosis. Thyroid hormone levels themselves do not drive orbital tissue expansion, which is why orbitopathy can persist or worsen after euthyroidism and is treated with glucocorticoids or teprotumumab rather than antithyroid drugs.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["graves-disease","thyroid-eye-disease","autoimmunity"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Graves Orbitopathy (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549889/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-004","prompt":"A 31-year-old man has a 2-cm firm thyroid nodule and intermittent watery diarrhea. His mother underwent thyroidectomy for cancer and later had an adrenal tumor that caused episodic headaches and palpitations. Serum calcitonin is markedly elevated, serum calcium is 10.9 mg/dL, and PTH is elevated. Fine-needle aspiration shows spindle-shaped cells in an amyloid-rich stroma. A germline mutation in which of the following genes is most likely?","choices":["MEN1 tumor suppressor","BRAF proto-oncogene","VHL tumor suppressor","NF1 tumor suppressor","RET proto-oncogene"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma (calcitonin-secreting parafollicular C cells with calcitonin-derived amyloid stroma), primary hyperparathyroidism, and pheochromocytoma constitute MEN2A, caused by autosomal dominant gain-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene, which encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase. Carriers are offered prophylactic thyroidectomy timed by codon risk, and pheochromocytoma must be excluded before any thyroid surgery. MEN1 (menin, a tumor suppressor) causes parathyroid, pituitary, and pancreatic islet tumors but not medullary thyroid cancer. Somatic BRAF V600E mutations are typical of papillary thyroid cancer, and VHL and NF1 predispose to pheochromocytoma but not to medullary carcinoma or hyperparathyroidism.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["medullary-thyroid-cancer","men2a","ret","genetics"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Medullary Thyroid Cancer (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459354/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-005","prompt":"A 9-day-old infant is brought in with vomiting, poor feeding, and lethargy. She was born at term with clitoromegaly and partially fused labioscrotal folds; karyotype is 46,XX. Blood pressure is 58/30 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 124 mEq/L, potassium 7.1 mEq/L, and glucose 48 mg/dL. Serum 17-hydroxyprogesterone is markedly elevated. Deficiency of which of the following enzymes is most likely?","choices":["11β-Hydroxylase","21-Hydroxylase","17α-Hydroxylase","3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","Aldosterone synthase"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A salt-wasting crisis (hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypotension, hypoglycemia) in the second week of life with virilized genitalia in a 46,XX infant and a markedly elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone is classic 21-hydroxylase deficiency, the cause of about 95% of congenital adrenal hyperplasia. The block prevents synthesis of both cortisol and aldosterone, precursors are shunted toward adrenal androgens, and low cortisol drives ACTH-mediated adrenal hyperplasia. 11β-Hydroxylase deficiency also virilizes but causes hypertension and hypokalemia because the mineralocorticoid 11-deoxycorticosterone accumulates. 3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency causes salt wasting with only mild virilization of 46,XX infants, and aldosterone synthase deficiency causes salt wasting without ambiguous genitalia.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["congenital-adrenal-hyperplasia","21-hydroxylase-deficiency","adrenal-steroidogenesis"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"21-Hydroxylase Deficiency (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493164/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-006","prompt":"A 24-year-old woman with schizophrenia has taken risperidone for 6 months. She now reports 4 months without menses and a milky nipple discharge. A pregnancy test is negative, TSH is normal, and serum prolactin is 92 ng/mL. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of her symptoms?","choices":["D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway","D2 blockade in the mesolimbic pathway","Elevated TRH from drug-induced hypothyroidism","Prolactin secretion by a lactotroph adenoma","D2 blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Dopamine released from hypothalamic tuberoinfundibular neurons into the hypophyseal portal circulation tonically inhibits prolactin secretion through D2 receptors on lactotrophs; risperidone, a potent D2 antagonist, removes this inhibition, causing hyperprolactinemia, galactorrhea, and amenorrhea through suppression of GnRH. Blockade of the nigrostriatal pathway causes extrapyramidal effects, and mesolimbic blockade produces the antipsychotic effect. Primary hypothyroidism can raise prolactin through elevated TRH, but her TSH is normal. A prolactinoma is less likely with a modest elevation that began on risperidone, and switching to a prolactin-sparing agent such as aripiprazole usually resolves it.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["hyperprolactinemia","antipsychotics","dopamine-pathways"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Hyperprolactinemia (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537331/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-007","prompt":"A 37-year-old man is found to have a serum calcium of 10.9 mg/dL on routine testing. He has no history of kidney stones or fractures. His father and sister have also been told they have 'high calcium,' and his father's neck exploration years ago did not lower his calcium. Serum phosphorus is 2.8 mg/dL, PTH 58 pg/mL (normal 10–65), and 25-hydroxyvitamin D 32 ng/mL. A 24-hour urine calcium is 62 mg, and the calcium-to-creatinine clearance ratio is 0.006. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his hypercalcemia?","choices":["Single adenoma of a parathyroid gland","Inactivating mutation of the calcium-sensing receptor","Germline mutation in the MEN1 tumor suppressor","Activating mutation of the calcium-sensing receptor","Ectopic secretion of PTH-related peptide"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Mild, asymptomatic, familial hypercalcemia with a normal or mildly elevated PTH and low urinary calcium excretion (calcium-to-creatinine clearance ratio <0.01) is familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia, usually caused by heterozygous inactivating CASR mutations that raise the calcium set point for PTH suppression and increase renal calcium reabsorption. It is benign, does not respond to parathyroidectomy (as his father's failed neck exploration illustrates), and needs no treatment. Primary hyperparathyroidism, whether from an adenoma or MEN1-associated hyperplasia, typically gives a clearance ratio above 0.02. Activating CASR mutations cause the opposite phenotype, autosomal dominant hypocalcemia with relative hypercalciuria.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["hypercalcemia","familial-hypocalciuric-hypercalcemia","calcium-sensing-receptor"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Familial Hypocalciuric Hypercalcemia (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459190/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-008","prompt":"A 22-year-old man with type 1 diabetes has 2 days of vomiting and abdominal pain after running out of insulin. Blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg and pulse 118/min. Glucose is 486 mg/dL, sodium 131 mEq/L, potassium 3.0 mEq/L, bicarbonate 9 mEq/L, anion gap 28 mEq/L, and venous pH 7.12; serum β-hydroxybutyrate is markedly elevated. A 1-L bolus of 0.9% saline has been given. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["IV regular insulin infusion at 0.1 units/kg/h","IV sodium bicarbonate to raise the pH above 7.2","Subcutaneous insulin glargine plus lispro","IV potassium, delaying insulin until K+ is repleted","IV insulin bolus, then potassium if K+ falls"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Insulin drives potassium into cells, so starting insulin when serum potassium is already below 3.3 mEq/L can precipitate life-threatening hypokalemia, arrhythmias, and respiratory muscle weakness; the ADA-led consensus on hyperglycemic crises recommends giving IV potassium (about 10–20 mEq/h) and delaying insulin until potassium is repleted (to at least 3.3–3.5 mEq/L, depending on the guideline version) while fluids continue. Total-body potassium is depleted in DKA even when the serum level is normal or high, because acidosis and insulin deficiency shift potassium out of cells. Once potassium is adequate, a continuous IV insulin infusion is begun. Bicarbonate is not recommended when the pH is 7.0 or higher, and a basal-bolus subcutaneous regimen is not appropriate initial therapy for severe DKA.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["diabetic-ketoacidosis","hypokalemia","insulin-therapy"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Adult Diabetic Ketoacidosis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560723/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-009","prompt":"A 57-year-old man with type 2 diabetes had a myocardial infarction 2 years ago. He takes metformin 1,000 mg twice daily, atorvastatin, aspirin, and lisinopril. BMI is 36 kg/m², HbA1c 7.9%, eGFR 82 mL/min/1.73 m², and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio normal. Left ventricular ejection fraction is normal. He has no personal or family history of thyroid cancer and no history of pancreatitis. Which of the following is the most appropriate addition to his regimen?","choices":["Sitagliptin","Glipizide","Pioglitazone","Insulin glargine","Semaglutide"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"The ADA Standards of Care recommend that adults with type 2 diabetes and established ASCVD receive a GLP-1 receptor agonist and/or an SGLT2 inhibitor with proven cardiovascular benefit, independent of HbA1c and of metformin use; with obesity, a GLP-1 RA such as semaglutide is particularly favored because it lowers major adverse cardiovascular events (SUSTAIN-6, SELECT) and produces substantial weight loss. DPP-4 inhibitors such as sitagliptin are weight neutral and cardiovascularly neutral. Sulfonylureas and basal insulin lower glucose but cause weight gain and hypoglycemia without cardiovascular benefit. Pioglitazone causes weight gain and fluid retention and is not a first-choice agent for cardiovascular risk reduction.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["type-2-diabetes","glp-1-receptor-agonists","ascvd","ada-standards"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Glucagon-Like Peptide-1 Receptor Agonists (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551568/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-010","prompt":"A 34-year-old woman with known but untreated Graves disease presents 2 days after an appendectomy with a temperature of 40.1°C, agitation, vomiting, and diarrhea. Pulse is 162/min and irregularly irregular; blood pressure is 148/62 mm Hg. Free T4 is markedly elevated, and TSH is undetectable. Propranolol, high-dose propylthiouracil, IV hydrocortisone, and cooling measures are ordered. Which of the following is the most appropriate use of iodine in her treatment?","choices":["Potassium iodide at least 1 hour after the thionamide","Potassium iodide now, before the first thionamide dose","Radioactive iodine ablation during this admission","No iodine, because it worsens thyrotoxicosis","Potassium iodide in place of propylthiouracil"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Thyroid storm is treated with a beta-blocker (propranolol), a thionamide to block new hormone synthesis (PTU, which also inhibits peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion, or methimazole), glucocorticoids, and inorganic iodine (SSKI or Lugol solution) to block release of preformed hormone. Iodine must be given at least 1 hour after the thionamide; given first, it supplies substrate for new hormone synthesis and can worsen thyrotoxicosis (the Jod-Basedow effect). Radioactive iodine is a later definitive therapy once she is euthyroid, not acute management, and iodine is an adjunct to, not a replacement for, thionamide therapy.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["thyroid-storm","thionamides","iodine"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Thyroid Storm (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448095/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-011","prompt":"A 52-year-old woman has a 2.5-cm nodule in the right thyroid lobe found on physical examination. She reports mild heat intolerance. TSH is 0.08 µU/mL (normal 0.4–4.0), and free T4 is at the upper limit of normal. Ultrasonography confirms a solitary 2.5-cm solid nodule. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Fine-needle aspiration biopsy","Repeat ultrasound in 12 months","Thyroid lobectomy","Levothyroxine suppression","Radioiodine uptake scan"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"When a thyroid nodule is found with a low TSH, the ATA guideline recommends radionuclide scintigraphy as the next step: a hyperfunctioning ('hot') nodule is very rarely malignant and does not need FNA, and treatment options include radioactive iodine or surgery. If the TSH were normal or high, the next step would be ultrasound-based risk stratification with FNA for nodules that meet size and sonographic criteria. Levothyroxine suppression is no longer recommended for benign nodules, and in a patient who is already subclinically hyperthyroid it would worsen thyrotoxicosis.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["thyroid-nodule","radioiodine-scan","hyperthyroidism"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Thyroid Nodule (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535422/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-012","prompt":"A 44-year-old woman has weight gain, easy bruising, proximal muscle weakness, and new hypertension. Late-night salivary cortisol is elevated on two occasions, and a 1-mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test shows a morning cortisol of 12 µg/dL. She takes no exogenous glucocorticoids. Plasma ACTH is 4 pg/mL (normal 10–60). Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["CT of the adrenal glands","MRI of the pituitary gland","High-dose dexamethasone suppression test","Inferior petrosal sinus sampling","CT of the chest"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hypercortisolism has been confirmed by two abnormal screening tests, and the next step is plasma ACTH to separate ACTH-dependent from ACTH-independent disease. A suppressed ACTH (<5 pg/mL) indicates autonomous adrenal cortisol production from an adenoma, carcinoma, or bilateral macronodular hyperplasia, so adrenal CT is next. Pituitary MRI, high-dose dexamethasone testing, and inferior petrosal sinus sampling are used only when ACTH is normal or elevated, to distinguish Cushing disease from ectopic ACTH secretion. Chest imaging looks for an ectopic ACTH source such as a bronchial carcinoid, which would raise, not suppress, ACTH.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["cushing-syndrome","acth","adrenal-adenoma"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Hypercortisolism (Cushing Syndrome) (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551526/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-013","prompt":"A 49-year-old man with primary adrenal insufficiency who takes hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone develops gastroenteritis and cannot keep his pills down for 2 days. He is brought in confused with abdominal pain. Blood pressure is 74/42 mm Hg and pulse 124/min. Sodium is 126 mEq/L, potassium 5.9 mEq/L, and glucose 62 mg/dL. Blood is drawn for laboratory studies. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["IV hydrocortisone 100 mg and IV saline","Cosyntropin stimulation test before steroids","Double his usual oral hydrocortisone dose","IV norepinephrine infusion and fluids","IV dexamethasone only, to preserve cortisol testing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Adrenal crisis is life-threatening and is treated immediately with hydrocortisone 100 mg IV (followed by 200 mg/day by infusion or divided doses) plus rapid infusion of isotonic saline, with dextrose for hypoglycemia, without waiting for laboratory results. At this dose hydrocortisone has enough mineralocorticoid activity that fludrocortisone is unnecessary acutely. He already carries the diagnosis, so a cosyntropin test is pointless, and dexamethasone—chosen only when diagnostic testing is still pending—lacks mineralocorticoid activity. Vasopressors without glucocorticoids work poorly because vascular responsiveness to catecholamines requires cortisol, and oral therapy fails in a vomiting patient.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["adrenal-insufficiency","adrenal-crisis","hydrocortisone"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Adrenal Crisis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499968/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-014","prompt":"A 39-year-old woman has episodic headaches, palpitations, and diaphoresis, with blood pressures up to 210/120 mm Hg during episodes. Plasma free metanephrines are 6 times the upper limit of normal, and CT shows a 4-cm left adrenal mass with high unenhanced attenuation. Laparoscopic adrenalectomy is planned. Which of the following is the most appropriate preoperative management?","choices":["Beta-blockade first, then alpha-blockade before surgery","Alpha-blockade for 7–14 days, then beta-blockade if needed","Beta-blockade alone, titrated to a heart rate below 80/min","Hydrochlorothiazide with sodium and fluid restriction","Short-acting nifedipine at the onset of each episode"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Before resection of a pheochromocytoma, alpha-adrenergic blockade (phenoxybenzamine, or a selective α1 blocker such as doxazosin) is started 7–14 days ahead together with a high-sodium diet and liberal fluids to re-expand the contracted plasma volume; a beta-blocker is added only after adequate alpha-blockade to control reflex tachycardia (Endocrine Society guideline). Starting a beta-blocker first removes β2-mediated vasodilation and leaves α1-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed, which can precipitate hypertensive crisis. Diuretics and salt restriction worsen the volume contraction that causes severe hypotension after the tumor is removed. Calcium channel blockers can supplement alpha-blockade but are not used alone as episodic rescue therapy.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["pheochromocytoma","alpha-blockade","perioperative"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Perioperative Management of Pheochromocytoma (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589634/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-015","prompt":"A 31-year-old man has had excessive thirst and urinated about 8 L per day since a head injury in a motorcycle accident 3 weeks ago. Serum sodium is 146 mEq/L and urine osmolality 110 mOsm/kg. During a supervised water deprivation test, urine osmolality rises only to 140 mOsm/kg while plasma osmolality reaches 305 mOsm/kg. After desmopressin, urine osmolality increases to 520 mOsm/kg. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Arginine vasopressin resistance (nephrogenic DI)","Primary polydipsia","Arginine vasopressin deficiency (central DI)","Osmotic diuresis from hyperglycemia","Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Dilute urine that fails to concentrate during water deprivation despite rising plasma osmolality, followed by a large (>50%) rise in urine osmolality after desmopressin, indicates a lack of endogenous vasopressin—central diabetes insipidus, now termed arginine vasopressin deficiency—a known complication of head trauma involving the hypothalamus or posterior pituitary. In nephrogenic DI (AVP resistance), urine osmolality changes little after desmopressin because the collecting duct cannot respond. In primary polydipsia, serum sodium is low-normal and the urine concentrates during water deprivation. SIADH causes hyponatremia with concentrated urine, the opposite pattern.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["diabetes-insipidus","water-deprivation-test","pituitary"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Arginine Vasopressin Disorder (Diabetes Insipidus) (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470458/"}]},{"id":"med-endo-016","prompt":"A 46-year-old man has a blood pressure of 168/100 mm Hg despite three antihypertensive medications. Serum potassium is 3.1 mEq/L and bicarbonate 31 mEq/L. Plasma aldosterone is 28 ng/dL with suppressed plasma renin activity, and an intravenous saline infusion fails to suppress aldosterone. He has no peripheral edema. Which of the following best explains the absence of edema despite ongoing aldosterone excess?","choices":["Pressure natriuresis and ANP offset sodium retention","Hypokalemia blocks aldosterone action on ENaC","Aldosterone receptors downregulate after a few days","Aldosterone drives K+ secretion but not Na+ uptake","Edema occurs only with low plasma oncotic pressure"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Primary aldosteronism causes an initial period of sodium and water retention, but after a few days of extracellular volume expansion sodium excretion rises back to match intake—'aldosterone escape.' Higher arterial pressure and volume increase renal pressure natriuresis, atrial natriuretic peptide is released, and distal sodium transporter expression falls, so total body sodium stabilizes at a modestly expanded level that causes hypertension but not edema. Aldosterone's effects on potassium and hydrogen ion secretion do not escape, which is why hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis persist. Mineralocorticoid receptor signaling is not downregulated; blocking it with spironolactone or eplerenone corrects the hypokalemia and lowers blood pressure.","subject":"endocrine","tags":["primary-aldosteronism","aldosterone-escape","renal-physiology"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Hyperaldosteronism (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499983/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-001","prompt":"A 63-year-old man who takes naproxen daily for osteoarthritis presents with hematemesis and melena. Blood pressure is 88/56 mm Hg and pulse 122/min. After resuscitation, upper endoscopy shows a 1.5-cm ulcer on the posterior wall of the duodenal bulb with an actively bleeding visible vessel. Which of the following arteries is most likely the source of bleeding?","choices":["Left gastric artery","Splenic artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Right gastroepiploic artery","Short gastric arteries"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The gastroduodenal artery, a branch of the common hepatic artery, runs directly behind the first part of the duodenum, so a posterior duodenal bulb ulcer can erode into it and cause brisk hemorrhage; anterior duodenal ulcers more often perforate into the peritoneal cavity. Ulcers on the lesser curvature of the stomach erode the left gastric artery, and the splenic and short gastric arteries lie along the greater curvature and fundus. The right gastroepiploic artery is a terminal branch of the gastroduodenal artery that runs along the greater curvature rather than behind the duodenal bulb.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["peptic-ulcer-disease","upper-gi-bleeding","gi-anatomy"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Peptic Ulcer Disease (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534792/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-002","prompt":"A 27-year-old woman has 1 year of bloating, loose stools, and fatigue. She has an itchy vesicular rash on her elbows and knees. Hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL with a low ferritin. IgA anti–tissue transglutaminase antibodies are markedly elevated, and total IgA is normal. Duodenal biopsy is performed while she is still eating gluten. Which of the following findings is most likely?","choices":["PAS-positive foamy macrophages in the lamina propria","Villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia and IELs","Transmural inflammation with noncaseating granulomas","Absent plasma cells with nodular lymphoid hyperplasia","Lipid-laden enterocytes with normal villous architecture"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Positive IgA anti-tTG with normal total IgA, iron deficiency from impaired duodenal absorption, and dermatitis herpetiformis indicate celiac disease; biopsy while on gluten shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes (IELs), most marked in the proximal small bowel. Gliadin peptides deamidated by tissue transglutaminase bind HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 and activate T cells that damage the mucosa. PAS-positive foamy macrophages indicate Whipple disease, and transmural inflammation with granulomas indicates Crohn disease. Absent plasma cells suggest common variable immunodeficiency, and lipid-laden enterocytes are seen in abetalipoproteinemia.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["celiac-disease","malabsorption","dermatitis-herpetiformis","pathology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Celiac Disease (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441900/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-003","prompt":"A 35-year-old woman with Crohn disease underwent resection of 100 cm of terminal ileum 2 years ago; her colon is intact. She has chronic greasy, foul-smelling diarrhea and now presents with renal colic. The passed stone is composed of calcium oxalate. A 24-hour urine collection shows markedly elevated oxalate and low citrate. Which of the following best explains her stone formation?","choices":["Hepatic overproduction of oxalate from glyoxylate","Acidic urine from bicarbonate loss in an ileostomy","Fat binds gut calcium, freeing oxalate for colonic absorption","Vitamin D malabsorption leading to hypercalciuria","Excess vitamin C intake converted to oxalate"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"After ileal resection, bile acids are lost and fat is malabsorbed; unabsorbed fatty acids bind (saponify) luminal calcium that would normally complex dietary oxalate, so free soluble oxalate is absorbed in the colon, a process enhanced because bile acids and fatty acids increase colonic permeability. This enteric hyperoxaluria requires an intact colon and produces calcium oxalate stones, with low urine citrate and volume from diarrhea adding to the risk. Uric acid stones are favored by an ileostomy, which causes bicarbonate and water losses and a small volume of acidic urine. Primary hyperoxaluria is an inherited hepatic enzyme defect usually presenting in childhood, and fat malabsorption lowers rather than raises urinary calcium.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["crohn-disease","ileal-resection","enteric-hyperoxaluria","nephrolithiasis"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Hyperoxaluria (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558987/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-004","prompt":"A 41-year-old man is found to have a poorly differentiated adenocarcinoma of the cecum on colonoscopy, along with only two other small adenomas. His mother had endometrial cancer at 46, and his maternal uncle had colon cancer at 50. Immunohistochemistry of the tumor shows loss of MSH2 and MSH6 expression. Which of the following processes is most likely defective in his cells?","choices":["Excision of oxidized bases by base excision repair","Excision of bulky lesions by nucleotide excision repair","Repair of double-strand breaks by homologous recombination","Repair of base mismatches after DNA replication","APC-mediated degradation of cytoplasmic β-catenin"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Early-onset right-sided colon cancer with few polyps, colorectal and endometrial cancer across generations, and loss of MSH2/MSH6 staining indicate Lynch syndrome, an autosomal dominant defect in DNA mismatch repair (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2, or EPCAM deletion). Unrepaired replication errors accumulate in repetitive sequences, producing microsatellite instability and an accelerated adenoma–carcinoma sequence, so carriers need colonoscopy every 1–2 years beginning at age 20–25. Loss of APC causes familial adenomatous polyposis with hundreds to thousands of polyps, and MUTYH-associated polyposis is a recessive base excision repair defect that also produces many adenomas. Homologous recombination defects (BRCA1/2) chiefly predispose to breast, ovarian, prostate, and pancreatic cancer.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["lynch-syndrome","hereditary-colorectal-cancer","dna-repair"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Hereditary Nonpolyposis Colon Cancer (Lynch Syndrome) (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564511/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-005","prompt":"A 29-year-old man has 3 weeks of fatigue, dark urine, and jaundice after unprotected sex with a new partner. His ALT, which was 1,850 U/L two weeks ago, is now 210 U/L. Hepatitis serologies show HBsAg negative, anti-HBs negative, IgM anti-HBc positive, HBeAg negative, IgM anti-HAV negative, and anti-HCV negative. Which of the following best describes his hepatitis B status?","choices":["Window period of acute infection","Resolved infection with lasting immunity","Inactive chronic carrier state","Immunity from prior vaccination","HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis B"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"After acute hepatitis B, HBsAg disappears before anti-HBs becomes detectable; during this window period the only marker of infection is IgM antibody to hepatitis B core antigen, so a patient recovering from acute hepatitis can be negative for both HBsAg and anti-HBs. Resolved infection shows anti-HBs with IgG anti-HBc, and vaccination produces anti-HBs alone because the vaccine contains only surface antigen. Every form of chronic infection, including the inactive carrier state and HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis, requires HBsAg to persist for more than 6 months.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["hepatitis-b","serology","window-period","microbiology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Hepatitis B (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555945/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-006","prompt":"A 19-year-old man has 6 months of hand tremor, slurred speech, declining school performance, and episodes of irritability. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL with an elevated reticulocyte count and a negative direct Coombs test; ALT is 96 U/L and alkaline phosphatase is low. Slit-lamp examination shows golden-brown rings at the corneal periphery, and serum ceruloplasmin is 8 mg/dL (normal 20–40). Which of the following is the most likely underlying defect?","choices":["Impaired biliary copper excretion from ATP7B mutation","Impaired intestinal copper absorption from ATP7A mutation","Increased intestinal iron absorption from HFE mutation","Misfolded α1-antitrypsin retained in hepatocytes","Defective bilirubin conjugation from UGT1A1 mutation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Wilson disease is an autosomal recessive defect in ATP7B, a hepatocyte copper-transporting ATPase needed to load copper onto ceruloplasmin and excrete it into bile. Copper accumulates first in the liver and then deposits in the basal ganglia (tremor, dysarthria, psychiatric change), the cornea (Kayser–Fleischer rings), and red cells, causing Coombs-negative hemolysis; a low ceruloplasmin and low alkaline phosphatase are clues. ATP7A mutations impair intestinal copper absorption and cause Menkes disease, a copper deficiency of infancy. HFE hemochromatosis causes iron overload with cirrhosis, diabetes, and skin bronzing but not neuropsychiatric disease or corneal rings.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["wilson-disease","copper-metabolism","liver-disease","genetics"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Wilson Disease (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441990/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-007","prompt":"A 61-year-old man with alcohol-associated cirrhosis is brought in by his wife for 2 days of confusion and day-night reversal after several days of constipation. He has asterixis. He is afebrile, and diagnostic paracentesis shows 60 neutrophils/mm³. He is started on lactulose titrated to 2–3 soft bowel movements daily. Which of the following best describes how lactulose improves his mental status?","choices":["Eradication of urease-producing colonic bacteria","Increased hepatic urea cycle conversion of ammonia","Colonic acidification traps ammonia as ammonium","Blockade of benzodiazepine-like ligands at GABA-A","Supply of branched-chain amino acids to the brain"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Lactulose is a nonabsorbable disaccharide that colonic bacteria ferment to lactic and acetic acids; the acidic lumen converts diffusible ammonia (NH3) to nonabsorbable ammonium (NH4+), which is trapped and excreted, while the osmotic laxative effect speeds removal of nitrogenous material. It also favors non-urease-producing lactobacilli. Rifaximin, a minimally absorbed antibiotic added for recurrent hepatic encephalopathy, is the drug that works by reducing ammonia-producing gut flora. Constipation is a common precipitant, and infection such as spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (excluded here by an ascitic neutrophil count below 250/mm³) and GI bleeding should always be sought.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["hepatic-encephalopathy","cirrhosis","lactulose","pharmacology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Lactulose (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536930/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-008","prompt":"A 56-year-old man with known cirrhosis presents with large-volume hematemesis. Blood pressure is 86/52 mm Hg and pulse 128/min. Two large-bore IV lines are placed, and he is intubated for airway protection. Hemoglobin is 6.6 g/dL, platelet count 72,000/mm³, and INR 1.8. Packed red blood cells and an octreotide infusion are started, and urgent endoscopy is planned. Which of the following should also be given now?","choices":["IV propranolol","IV ceftriaxone","IV tranexamic acid","Fresh frozen plasma","Platelet transfusion"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Patients with cirrhosis and GI bleeding have a high rate of bacterial infection, and short-term antibiotic prophylaxis (ceftriaxone 1 g/day for up to 7 days) reduces infection, rebleeding, and death, so it is part of initial management together with a vasoactive agent (octreotide), restrictive transfusion to a hemoglobin of about 7–8 g/dL, and endoscopic band ligation within 12 hours (AASLD guidance). Nonselective beta-blockers lower blood pressure and blunt compensatory tachycardia, so they are held during acute hemorrhage and started later for secondary prophylaxis. The INR does not reflect the rebalanced hemostasis of cirrhosis, and FFP expands volume and can raise portal pressure; platelets are not needed at 72,000/mm³. Tranexamic acid did not reduce death from GI bleeding in the HALT-IT trial and increased venous thrombosis.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["variceal-bleeding","cirrhosis","antibiotic-prophylaxis"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Esophageal Varices (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448078/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-009","prompt":"A 44-year-old woman has 3 months of epigastric burning. A urea breath test is positive for Helicobacter pylori. She has no alarm features. She took azithromycin for bronchitis last year and has no drug allergies. Antibiotic susceptibility testing is not available. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["PPI, clarithromycin, amoxicillin, and metronidazole for 14 days","PPI, clarithromycin, and amoxicillin for 14 days","PPI, levofloxacin, and amoxicillin for 14 days","Twice-daily PPI for 8 weeks without antibiotics","PPI, bismuth, tetracycline, and metronidazole for 14 days"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"The 2024 ACG guideline recommends optimized bismuth quadruple therapy (PPI, bismuth, tetracycline, and metronidazole for 14 days) as the preferred first-line regimen when antibiotic susceptibility is unknown, particularly after prior macrolide exposure, because U.S. clarithromycin resistance is high. Clarithromycin-based triple or concomitant therapy is appropriate only when the strain is known to be clarithromycin-susceptible. Levofloxacin triple therapy is not recommended empirically because of high fluoroquinolone resistance. Rifabutin-based triple therapy and vonoprazan–amoxicillin dual therapy are alternatives in patients without penicillin allergy, and eradication should be confirmed with a urea breath test or stool antigen at least 4 weeks after treatment.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["helicobacter-pylori","peptic-ulcer-disease","bismuth-quadruple-therapy"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Helicobacter Pylori (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534233/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-010","prompt":"A 48-year-old woman is admitted with acute gallstone pancreatitis; lipase is 4,200 U/L. She has no organ failure, and her BISAP score is 1. She receives moderate goal-directed lactated Ringer's solution and IV analgesia. Twenty-four hours later her pain has improved substantially, she has no nausea, and she asks to eat. Which of the following is the most appropriate nutritional plan?","choices":["Start an oral low-fat solid diet now","Keep NPO until lipase normalizes","Begin total parenteral nutrition","Nasojejunal tube feeding for 7 days","Clear liquids only for 72 hours"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In mild acute pancreatitis, ACG and AGA guidance supports early oral feeding (within 24–48 hours) as tolerated, and a low-fat solid diet is safe and shortens hospital stay compared with starting with clear liquids; lipase does not need to normalize first. Prolonged NPO status promotes gut barrier dysfunction and infectious complications. When oral intake is not tolerated, as in severe pancreatitis, enteral feeding by nasogastric or nasojejunal tube is preferred over parenteral nutrition, which carries more infections. Because her pancreatitis is from gallstones, she should also undergo cholecystectomy during the same admission.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["acute-pancreatitis","bisap","nutrition"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Acute Pancreatitis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482468/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-011","prompt":"A 76-year-old woman has 1 day of fever, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice. Temperature is 39.3°C, blood pressure 92/56 mm Hg, and pulse 116/min. Total bilirubin is 5.8 mg/dL, alkaline phosphatase 410 U/L, and WBC count 18,500/mm³. Ultrasonography shows gallstones and a common bile duct dilated to 12 mm. Blood cultures are drawn, and IV fluids and piperacillin-tazobactam are started. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent laparoscopic cholecystectomy","ERCP with biliary decompression","MRCP to confirm a duct stone","Cholescintigraphy (HIDA scan)","Endoscopic ultrasonography"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Fever, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice (Charcot triad) with hypotension, cholestatic labs, and a dilated common bile duct indicate acute cholangitis from choledocholithiasis. After antibiotics and resuscitation, the definitive step is biliary drainage, preferably by ERCP with sphincterotomy and stone extraction or stenting, performed urgently in severe disease (Tokyo Guidelines); percutaneous transhepatic drainage is used if ERCP fails. MRCP and endoscopic ultrasonography are for patients with an intermediate probability of a duct stone, whereas cholangitis with a dilated duct already establishes the need for drainage. Cholecystectomy is done later, after the duct has been cleared and the patient has recovered.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["acute-cholangitis","choledocholithiasis","ercp"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cholangitis (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558946/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-012","prompt":"A 58-year-old man with alcohol-associated cirrhosis and ascites presents with diffuse abdominal discomfort and new confusion. Temperature is 37.9°C and blood pressure 104/62 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 1.6 mg/dL (baseline 0.9 mg/dL), and total bilirubin is 4.8 mg/dL. Diagnostic paracentesis shows an ascitic fluid neutrophil count of 620/mm³, total protein 0.9 g/dL, glucose 110 mg/dL, and LDH below the serum upper limit of normal; Gram stain shows no organisms. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["IV ceftriaxone alone, pending culture results","Large-volume paracentesis with albumin replacement","Exploratory laparotomy for secondary peritonitis","IV ceftriaxone plus IV albumin on days 1 and 3","Withhold antibiotics until ascitic culture is positive"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"An ascitic neutrophil count ≥250/mm³ in cirrhosis establishes spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, which is treated empirically with a third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone or cefotaxime without waiting for culture results. Because his creatinine is above 1 mg/dL and bilirubin above 4 mg/dL, IV albumin (1.5 g/kg on day 1 and 1 g/kg on day 3) should be added, which reduces kidney failure and mortality. Large-volume paracentesis is avoided during SBP with acute kidney injury. Secondary peritonitis from perforation is suggested by Runyon criteria (two of: total protein >1 g/dL, glucose <50 mg/dL, LDH above the serum upper limit of normal) or a polymicrobial Gram stain, none of which are present.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["spontaneous-bacterial-peritonitis","cirrhosis","albumin","ascites"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Ascites (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470482/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-013","prompt":"A 38-year-old man asks about colorectal cancer screening. His father was diagnosed with colon adenocarcinoma at age 46; no other relatives are affected, and genetic evaluation of the family found no hereditary syndrome. He has no gastrointestinal symptoms. According to the U.S. Multi-Society Task Force on Colorectal Cancer, which of the following is the most appropriate screening plan?","choices":["Colonoscopy at age 40, then every 10 years","Colonoscopy now, then every 5 years","Colonoscopy at age 45, then every 10 years","Annual fecal immunochemical test from age 40","Colonoscopy now, then every 10 years"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For a person with one first-degree relative diagnosed with colorectal cancer or an advanced adenoma before age 60 (or two first-degree relatives at any age), the U.S. Multi-Society Task Force recommends colonoscopy every 5 years beginning at age 40 or 10 years before the youngest affected relative's diagnosis, whichever is earlier. His father was diagnosed at 46, so screening should have started at 36, and he should have colonoscopy now. Starting at 45 with any recommended test is the average-risk approach (USPSTF 2021), and stool-based tests are not recommended as primary screening for this higher-risk group. A 10-year interval applies to average-risk patients or to those whose relative was diagnosed at 60 or older.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["colorectal-cancer-screening","family-history","colonoscopy"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Colorectal Cancer Screening (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559064/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-014","prompt":"A 27-year-old woman at 22 weeks' gestation has 18 hours of periumbilical pain that has moved to the right flank and right lower quadrant, with nausea and anorexia. Temperature is 38.1°C. She has right-sided abdominal tenderness with guarding. WBC count is 15,800/mm³ with a left shift. Graded-compression ultrasonography does not visualize the appendix. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["CT of the abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast","Repeat ultrasonography in 24 hours","IV antibiotics alone without further imaging","Transvaginal ultrasonography of the adnexa","MRI of the abdomen and pelvis without gadolinium"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"When appendicitis is suspected in pregnancy and ultrasonography is nondiagnostic, the American College of Radiology recommends noncontrast MRI, which has high sensitivity and specificity, avoids ionizing radiation, and can locate an appendix displaced upward by the gravid uterus. Gadolinium is avoided in pregnancy. CT is reserved for situations in which MRI is unavailable, because it exposes the fetus to radiation. Delaying the diagnosis with repeat imaging or treating with antibiotics alone risks perforation, which markedly increases fetal loss.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["appendicitis","pregnancy","diagnostic-imaging"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Appendicitis in Pregnancy (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551642/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-015","prompt":"A 22-year-old college student notices that his eyes look yellow after several days of fasting during final exams while recovering from a viral illness. He otherwise feels well. Total bilirubin is 3.4 mg/dL with a direct (conjugated) fraction of 0.3 mg/dL. Hemoglobin, reticulocyte count, haptoglobin, LDH, AST, ALT, and alkaline phosphatase are normal. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","choices":["Absent UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity","Defective canalicular secretion of conjugated bilirubin","Hemolysis increasing bilirubin production","Impaired hepatic storage of conjugated bilirubin","Reduced UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Mild, fluctuating unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia triggered by fasting, illness, or stress, with normal liver enzymes and no hemolysis, is Gilbert syndrome, caused by reduced UGT1A1 activity (often a promoter polymorphism) that lowers hepatic bilirubin glucuronidation to roughly 30% of normal; it is benign and needs no treatment. Absent UGT1A1 activity (Crigler–Najjar type I) causes severe neonatal unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and kernicterus. Defects in canalicular excretion (Dubin–Johnson) or hepatic storage (Rotor) cause conjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Hemolysis would raise the reticulocyte count and LDH and lower haptoglobin.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["bilirubin-metabolism","gilbert-syndrome","hyperbilirubinemia"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Gilbert Syndrome (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470200/"}]},{"id":"med-gi-016","prompt":"A 42-year-old woman has recurrent episodes of right upper quadrant pain that begin about 30 minutes after fatty meals and last 1–2 hours. Ultrasonography shows multiple gallstones without gallbladder wall thickening or pericholecystic fluid. The hormone whose release after these meals most directly triggers her pain also has which of the following actions?","choices":["Stimulates bicarbonate secretion by pancreatic ducts","Stimulates acid secretion by gastric parietal cells","Inhibits secretion of insulin, glucagon, and gastrin","Initiates migrating motor complexes during fasting","Stimulates enzyme secretion by pancreatic acini"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Fatty acids and amino acids entering the duodenum stimulate I cells of the duodenum and jejunum to release cholecystokinin, which contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi; gallbladder contraction against a stone transiently obstructing the cystic duct produces postprandial biliary colic. Cholecystokinin's other major action is to stimulate pancreatic acinar cells to secrete digestive enzymes, and it also slows gastric emptying. Bicarbonate-rich ductal secretion is the action of secretin, released from S cells in response to duodenal acid, and parietal cell acid secretion is stimulated by gastrin from antral G cells. Somatostatin from D cells broadly inhibits gastrointestinal and pancreatic hormone release and also inhibits gallbladder contraction, and motilin drives the migrating motor complex during fasting.","subject":"gastrointestinal","tags":["gi-hormones","cholecystokinin","biliary-colic"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Biochemistry, Cholecystokinin (StatPearls)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534204/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-001","prompt":"A 64-year-old man with hypertension and type 2 diabetes develops sudden vertigo, vomiting, and hoarseness. Examination shows horizontal nystagmus, left-sided ptosis and miosis, a diminished gag reflex with the uvula deviating to the right, left arm and leg ataxia, loss of pain and temperature sensation over the left face, and loss of pain and temperature sensation over the right arm, trunk, and leg. Strength and facial movement are normal. Occlusion of which artery best explains these findings?","choices":["Left posterior inferior cerebellar artery","Left anterior inferior cerebellar artery","Left anterior spinal artery","Right posterior inferior cerebellar artery","Proximal basilar artery"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"This is lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome from left PICA (or distal vertebral) occlusion: the nucleus ambiguus lesion causes hoarseness, dysphagia, and a weak palate with the uvula pulled away from the lesion; the spinal trigeminal nucleus causes ipsilateral facial pain/temperature loss; the spinothalamic tract causes contralateral body loss; descending sympathetic fibers cause ipsilateral Horner syndrome. Signs are ipsilateral to the lesion, so this is the left side. AICA occlusion (lateral pons) also causes vertigo and crossed sensory loss but adds ipsilateral facial paralysis and hearing loss rather than nucleus ambiguus signs. Anterior spinal artery occlusion produces medial medullary syndrome with contralateral hemiparesis and tongue deviation toward the lesion. Normal strength argues against basilar occlusion.","subject":"neurology","tags":["stroke","brainstem-syndromes","neuroanatomy","pica"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Lateral Medullary Syndrome (Wallenberg Syndrome)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551670/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-002","prompt":"A 63-year-old woman with a 45-pack-year smoking history reports 4 months of difficulty climbing stairs and rising from chairs, along with a dry mouth and constipation. Examination shows symmetric proximal leg weakness, mild ptosis, and absent patellar reflexes; after 10 seconds of maximal voluntary contraction, quadriceps strength and the patellar reflex transiently improve. Chest CT shows a central hilar mass. On repetitive nerve stimulation, the baseline compound muscle action potential is low and increases by more than 100% after brief exercise. Antibodies directed against which target are most likely responsible?","choices":["Postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors","Presynaptic P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels","Muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase (MuSK)","Presynaptic voltage-gated potassium channel complex","Ganglionic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, often paraneoplastic with small cell lung cancer, is caused by antibodies to presynaptic P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels, which reduce acetylcholine release. The triad is proximal weakness, hyporeflexia, and autonomic dysfunction (dry mouth, constipation), with facilitation of strength and reflexes after brief exercise and an incremental response on high-frequency or post-exercise stimulation. Myasthenia gravis (anti-AChR or anti-MuSK) causes fatigable ocular and bulbar weakness with preserved reflexes and a decremental response on low-frequency stimulation. Antibodies to the potassium channel complex (LGI1/CASPR2) cause neuromyotonia or limbic encephalitis, and ganglionic AChR antibodies cause isolated autonomic failure without limb weakness.","subject":"neurology","tags":["lambert-eaton","myasthenia-gravis","neuromuscular-junction","paraneoplastic"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507891/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-003","prompt":"A 68-year-old man with newly diagnosed Parkinson disease is started on a combination tablet containing levodopa and a second agent. Compared with levodopa given alone, the combination allows about a 75% lower levodopa dose and causes far less nausea, vomiting, and orthostatic hypotension. The second agent achieves this by which mechanism?","choices":["Inhibiting peripheral catechol-O-methyltransferase","Inhibiting monoamine oxidase type B in the striatum","Inhibiting peripheral DOPA decarboxylase","Blocking dopamine D2 receptors in the area postrema","Antagonizing NMDA glutamate receptors in the striatum"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Carbidopa inhibits peripheral aromatic L-amino acid (DOPA) decarboxylase and does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so less levodopa is converted to dopamine in the periphery. More levodopa reaches the brain (allowing lower doses), and peripheral dopamine effects such as nausea (via the area postrema, which lies outside the barrier) and orthostatic hypotension decrease. COMT inhibitors (entacapone) also act peripherally but are used to prolong levodopa's effect and reduce wearing-off, not to cut nausea. Peripheral D2 blockade (domperidone) reduces nausea but does not lower the levodopa dose requirement, and MAO-B inhibitors and amantadine act centrally as separate adjuncts.","subject":"neurology","tags":["parkinson-disease","carbidopa-levodopa","pharmacology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Carbidopa","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554552/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-004","prompt":"A 27-year-old woman with focal epilepsy and frequent migraines is switched to a single antiseizure medication chosen to also provide migraine prophylaxis. Over 4 months her seizures and headaches improve, but she loses 6 kg, notes tingling in her fingers and trouble finding words, and presents with right flank pain radiating to the groin. Serum bicarbonate is 17 mEq/L, chloride 112 mEq/L, and the anion gap is 10. CT shows a 5-mm right ureteral stone. Which medication is she most likely taking?","choices":["Valproate","Lamotrigine","Levetiracetam","Topiramate","Carbamazepine"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Topiramate is approved for focal and generalized epilepsy and for migraine prophylaxis. Its carbonic anhydrase inhibition causes a non-anion gap metabolic acidosis, paresthesias, and calcium phosphate kidney stones, and it commonly causes weight loss and cognitive slowing with word-finding difficulty. Valproate also prevents migraine and treats seizures but causes weight gain, tremor, and hepatotoxicity rather than stones or acidosis. Lamotrigine, levetiracetam, and carbamazepine are not standard migraine preventives, and none causes this acidosis-and-stone pattern.","subject":"neurology","tags":["epilepsy","antiseizure-drugs","adverse-effects","migraine-prophylaxis"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Topiramate","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554530/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-005","prompt":"A 61-year-old woman with hypothyroidism and vitiligo reports 6 months of tingling in her feet and unsteadiness in the dark. Examination shows absent vibration and joint-position sense in the toes, a positive Romberg sign, spastic leg weakness, bilateral extensor plantar responses, and absent ankle reflexes. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL and MCV is 112 fL. Which spinal cord regions are primarily affected?","choices":["Anterior horns and lateral corticospinal tracts","Crossing spinothalamic fibers in the anterior commissure","Dorsal roots and dorsal columns only","Anterior two-thirds of the cord, sparing the dorsal columns","Dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Vitamin B12 deficiency (here likely pernicious anemia, given her other autoimmune conditions) causes subacute combined degeneration: demyelination of the dorsal columns (lost vibration and proprioception, positive Romberg) and lateral corticospinal tracts (spasticity, extensor plantar responses). A coexisting peripheral neuropathy explains the absent ankle reflexes despite upper motor neuron signs. ALS affects anterior horns and corticospinal tracts but spares sensation. Tabes dorsalis (neurosyphilis) affects dorsal roots and columns without corticospinal signs, syringomyelia damages crossing spinothalamic fibers in a cape distribution, and anterior spinal artery infarction spares the dorsal columns.","subject":"neurology","tags":["spinal-cord-syndromes","vitamin-b12","subacute-combined-degeneration"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Spinal Cord Subacute Combined Degeneration","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560728/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-006","prompt":"A 54-year-old woman has a sudden, severe headache, followed hours later by double vision and a drooping right eyelid. She is alert, with no limb weakness or ataxia. The right eye rests down and out, with complete ptosis and a 7-mm pupil that does not react to light; abduction is full. Corneal and facial sensation are normal, and the left eye is normal. Which lesion is most likely?","choices":["Posterior communicating artery aneurysm","Microvascular ischemia of the oculomotor nerve","Right cavernous sinus thrombosis","Right midbrain infarct involving the cerebral peduncle","Uncal herniation from a temporal hematoma"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A painful, pupil-involving third nerve palsy is a compressive lesion, classically a posterior communicating artery aneurysm, until proven otherwise; the sudden severe headache raises concern for aneurysmal expansion or rupture and warrants urgent CTA. Pupillomotor parasympathetic fibers run superficially on the nerve and are compressed early. Diabetic or hypertensive microvascular ischemia affects the nerve's core and usually spares the pupil. Cavernous sinus disease typically also involves CN IV, VI, and V1 (here abduction and corneal sensation are normal), a Weber midbrain infarct adds contralateral hemiparesis, and uncal herniation causes a declining level of consciousness.","subject":"neurology","tags":["cranial-nerves","oculomotor-palsy","aneurysm","neuroanatomy"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Cranial Nerve III Palsy (Oculomotor Palsy)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526112/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-007","prompt":"A 29-year-old woman has 2 weeks of horizontal double vision. On attempted gaze to the left, her right eye does not move past the midline while the left eye abducts with nystagmus. On gaze to the right, both eyes move normally. Convergence is intact. MRI shows several periventricular T2-hyperintense lesions oriented perpendicular to the corpus callosum and a small lesion in the dorsal pons. Damage to which structure explains her eye findings?","choices":["Left medial longitudinal fasciculus","Right medial longitudinal fasciculus","Left paramedian pontine reticular formation","Right abducens nucleus","Right oculomotor nerve fascicle"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia results from a lesion of the medial longitudinal fasciculus, which carries signals from the contralateral abducens nucleus to the ipsilateral medial rectus subnucleus. The INO is named for the eye that fails to adduct, so a right MLF lesion impairs right-eye adduction on left gaze, with nystagmus of the abducting left eye; intact convergence shows the medial rectus and CN III are working. In a young woman, MS is the classic cause, and bilateral INO is especially suggestive. A left MLF lesion would instead impair left-eye adduction on right gaze, which is normal here. A left PPRF lesion would cause a conjugate gaze palsy to the left in which neither eye moves left, whereas her left eye abducts; a right abducens nucleus lesion would cause a conjugate gaze palsy to the right, which is full here. A CN III fascicle lesion would also impair convergence, vertical gaze, and lid elevation.","subject":"neurology","tags":["multiple-sclerosis","internuclear-ophthalmoplegia","neuroanatomy"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441970/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-008","prompt":"A 66-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 2 hours after his wife saw him suddenly develop right-sided weakness and difficulty speaking; he was normal 15 minutes before that. He takes metformin and atorvastatin and no anticoagulants. BP is 176/94 mm Hg, pulse 88/min and irregular, and glucose 142 mg/dL. He has global aphasia, left gaze deviation, and right face and arm plegia; NIHSS score is 19. Noncontrast CT shows no hemorrhage and no large established infarct (ASPECTS 9). CT angiography shows occlusion of the proximal left middle cerebral artery (M1). Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Mechanical thrombectomy alone, withholding thrombolysis","IV thrombolysis alone, then repeat CT in 24 hours","IV thrombolysis and mechanical thrombectomy","Lower BP below 140/90 mm Hg before reperfusion","Aspirin 325 mg and an IV heparin infusion"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Within 4.5 hours of last known well, an eligible patient should receive IV thrombolysis (alteplase or tenecteplase), and a large-vessel anterior circulation occlusion with NIHSS 6 or more and a favorable ASPECTS also warrants mechanical thrombectomy (recommended within 6 hours and, with imaging selection, up to 24 hours). Per AHA/ASA guidance, thrombolysis should not be withheld or delayed because thrombectomy is planned. His BP is already below the 185/110 mm Hg threshold required before thrombolysis, so aggressive lowering to 140/90 is unnecessary and could worsen ischemia. Thrombolysis alone is inadequate for a proximal M1 occlusion, and acute heparin has no role here.","subject":"neurology","tags":["ischemic-stroke","thrombolysis","thrombectomy","aha-asa-guidelines"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Acute Stroke: Evaluation and Management","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535369/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-009","prompt":"A 72-year-old woman had a 25-minute episode of right hand weakness and word-finding difficulty yesterday that resolved completely. She has hypertension and hyperlipidemia. Examination today is normal; ECG shows sinus rhythm, and 48 hours of telemetry show no arrhythmia. MRI of the brain shows no infarct. Carotid duplex ultrasonography, confirmed by CT angiography, shows 80% stenosis of the left internal carotid artery and 40% stenosis of the right. She is started on high-intensity statin and antiplatelet therapy. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Medical therapy alone because symptoms resolved","Warfarin with a target INR of 2 to 3","Elective left carotid stenting after 3 months","Left carotid endarterectomy within 2 weeks","Repeat carotid duplex imaging in 6 months"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A TIA referable to a 70%–99% ipsilateral (symptomatic) internal carotid stenosis is an indication for carotid endarterectomy, ideally within 2 weeks, because stroke risk is highest in the days after the event. Complete resolution of symptoms does not reduce this early risk; the event defines the stenosis as symptomatic. In patients over about 70, endarterectomy has lower periprocedural stroke risk than stenting, and delaying any revascularization for months forfeits most of the benefit. Warfarin is indicated for cardioembolic sources such as atrial fibrillation, which telemetry did not show.","subject":"neurology","tags":["tia","carotid-stenosis","endarterectomy","stroke-prevention"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Carotid Endarterectomy","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470582/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-010","prompt":"A 34-year-old man with epilepsy is brought in by ambulance during a generalized tonic-clonic seizure that began 12 minutes ago. Paramedics gave midazolam 10 mg IM 8 minutes ago without effect. In the emergency department, IV access is obtained, fingerstick glucose is 104 mg/dL, and he receives lorazepam 4 mg IV; 5 minutes later he is still convulsing. Oxygen saturation is 94% on a nonrebreather mask. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Another 4-mg dose of IV lorazepam","Intubation and continuous propofol infusion","IV thiamine followed by 50% dextrose","Emergent EEG before further medication","IV levetiracetam loading dose"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"He is in established status epilepticus after two adequate benzodiazepine doses (IM midazolam, then IV lorazepam). The next step is an IV second-line antiseizure loading dose: levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, or valproate, which performed similarly in the ESETT trial. Further benzodiazepine doses add respiratory depression with little benefit. Anesthetic infusions such as propofol or midazolam with intubation are reserved for refractory status after a second-line agent fails. Glucose is normal, so dextrose is not indicated, and treatment should never wait for an EEG.","subject":"neurology","tags":["status-epilepticus","seizures","emergency-management"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Status Epilepticus","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430686/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-011","prompt":"A 46-year-old woman develops a sudden, severe occipital headache while lifting a box; it reaches maximal intensity within seconds, and she vomits once. She presents 9 hours later. She is alert; BP is 148/86 mm Hg and temperature 37.0°C. The neurologic examination is nonfocal, and there is mild neck stiffness. Noncontrast head CT is normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Lumbar puncture with CSF analysis","IV prochlorperazine and discharge if relieved","MRI of the brain with gadolinium","Nimodipine and repeat CT in 24 hours","Sumatriptan and outpatient neurology follow-up"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A thunderclap headache requires exclusion of subarachnoid hemorrhage. Noncontrast CT is nearly 100% sensitive within 6 hours of onset, but sensitivity falls after that, so a normal CT at 9 hours must be followed by lumbar puncture looking for xanthochromia and red cells that do not clear (CT angiography is an accepted alternative in some pathways). Treating the pain with an antiemetic or triptan and discharging would miss a sentinel aneurysmal bleed, and triptans are vasoconstrictors that should be avoided until SAH and reversible vasoconstriction syndrome are excluded. Nimodipine is given only after SAH is confirmed, to improve outcomes related to delayed cerebral ischemia.","subject":"neurology","tags":["headache","subarachnoid-hemorrhage","thunderclap-headache","lumbar-puncture"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Subarachnoid Hemorrhage","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441958/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-012","prompt":"A 26-year-old woman (BMI 38 kg/m2) has 3 months of daily headaches that are worse when lying down, brief episodes of visual dimming when standing, and a whooshing sound in her ears. She takes no medications. Fundoscopy shows bilateral papilledema; visual acuity is 20/20, and perimetry shows only enlarged blind spots. MRI with MR venography shows a partially empty sella and no mass or venous sinus thrombosis. Lumbar puncture shows an opening pressure of 34 cm H2O with normal CSF composition. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Optic nerve sheath fenestration","Acetazolamide plus weight loss","High-dose oral prednisone","Ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement","Sumatriptan at headache onset"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is idiopathic intracranial hypertension: papilledema, raised opening pressure with normal CSF, and normal imaging after excluding venous sinus thrombosis. With mild visual loss, first-line treatment is weight loss plus acetazolamide, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that reduces CSF production; serial perimetry is essential. Optic nerve sheath fenestration and CSF shunting are reserved for severe or rapidly progressive vision loss or failure of medical therapy. Glucocorticoids are not used because of weight gain and rebound, and overuse of abortive analgesics can add medication-overuse headache.","subject":"neurology","tags":["headache","idiopathic-intracranial-hypertension","papilledema"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536924/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-013","prompt":"A 70-year-old man is brought in with 1 day of fever, headache, and confusion. Temperature is 39.4°C, BP 112/70 mm Hg, and pulse 112/min. He has nuchal rigidity and is disoriented without focal deficits. He has had no recent neurosurgery or head trauma, is not immunosuppressed, and has no drug allergies. Blood cultures are drawn and dexamethasone is given. Which empiric antimicrobial regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Vancomycin and ceftriaxone","Ampicillin, gentamicin, and cefotaxime","Vancomycin, ceftriaxone, and ampicillin","Vancomycin, cefepime, and metronidazole","Ceftriaxone and acyclovir"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"For community-acquired bacterial meningitis in adults older than 50, IDSA recommends vancomycin plus a third-generation cephalosporin (for Streptococcus pneumoniae, including resistant strains, and Neisseria meningitidis) plus ampicillin to cover Listeria monocytogenes, which is intrinsically resistant to cephalosporins. Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone alone is appropriate for immunocompetent patients aged 2 to 50 years. Ampicillin with gentamicin or cefotaxime is the neonatal regimen, and cefepime- or meropenem-based regimens are for post-neurosurgical or penetrating-trauma meningitis. Dexamethasone should precede or accompany the first antibiotic dose.","subject":"neurology","tags":["meningitis","empiric-antibiotics","listeria","infectious-neurology"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Bacterial Meningitis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470351/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-014","prompt":"A 38-year-old man has 5 days of tingling in his feet followed by progressive leg weakness that now involves his arms. Three weeks ago he had bloody diarrhea that resolved. He has symmetric weakness (3/5 in the legs, 4/5 in the arms), absent reflexes throughout, and mild distal sensory loss; pupils react normally. Forced vital capacity is 2.1 L (predicted 4.8 L) and falling on serial measurements. CSF shows protein 210 mg/dL and 2 cells/μL. In addition to ICU admission for respiratory monitoring, which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["High-dose IV methylprednisolone","Pyridostigmine","Equine botulinum antitoxin","Intravenous immune globulin","Oral prednisone taper"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Ascending symmetric weakness with areflexia after Campylobacter jejuni enteritis and albuminocytologic dissociation in CSF is Guillain-Barré syndrome (acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy). IVIG or plasma exchange, which are equally effective, hasten recovery in patients who cannot walk independently or have respiratory compromise. Glucocorticoids, oral or IV, do not improve outcomes in GBS, a common misconception because they help other inflammatory neuropathies such as CIDP. Pyridostigmine treats myasthenia gravis, and botulinum antitoxin treats botulism, which causes descending paralysis with dilated, poorly reactive pupils.","subject":"neurology","tags":["guillain-barre-syndrome","neuromuscular","ivig"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Guillain-Barre Syndrome","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532254/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-015","prompt":"A 74-year-old man has 18 months of worsening attention and difficulty judging distances, with day-to-day swings between near-normal alertness and marked confusion. He describes detailed, recurring visions of small children in his living room. His wife says he has acted out his dreams for years. Examination shows mild bradykinesia and rigidity without rest tremor. After a single dose of haloperidol for agitation, he became severely rigid and immobile. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":["Parkinson disease dementia","Alzheimer disease","Behavioral-variant frontotemporal dementia","Vascular dementia","Dementia with Lewy bodies"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Dementia with Lewy bodies is characterized by fluctuating cognition with early attention and visuospatial deficits, well-formed visual hallucinations, REM sleep behavior disorder, and parkinsonism, plus severe sensitivity to antipsychotics. Its pathology is cortical alpha-synuclein Lewy bodies. Parkinson disease dementia shares the pathology, but by convention it is diagnosed when parkinsonian motor signs precede dementia by more than 1 year; here cognition came first. Alzheimer disease begins with episodic memory loss and lacks early hallucinations and fluctuations, and frontotemporal dementia presents with behavioral or language change.","subject":"neurology","tags":["dementia","lewy-body-dementia","neuroleptic-sensitivity"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Lewy Body Dementia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482441/"}]},{"id":"med-neuro-016","prompt":"A 9-year-old boy has 2 months of morning headaches with vomiting and progressive clumsiness of his left hand. Examination shows papilledema and left-sided dysmetria. MRI shows a well-circumscribed cyst in the left cerebellar hemisphere with a brightly enhancing mural nodule. He has no family history of tumors, and abdominal imaging is normal. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":["Pilocytic astrocytoma","Medulloblastoma","Diffuse midline glioma","Hemangioblastoma","Craniopharyngioma"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Pilocytic astrocytoma is the most common primary brain tumor in children and classically appears as a cystic cerebellar hemisphere mass with an enhancing mural nodule; histology shows bipolar astrocytes with Rosenthal fibers, and prognosis after resection is excellent. Hemangioblastoma has a similar cyst-and-nodule appearance but occurs mainly in adults, often with von Hippel-Lindau disease. Medulloblastoma is a solid midline vermian tumor of small round blue cells that often obstructs the fourth ventricle, and diffuse midline glioma (H3 K27-altered) is an infiltrative, noncystic tumor of the pons or thalamus. Craniopharyngioma is suprasellar and causes visual-field and endocrine problems.","subject":"neurology","tags":["brain-tumors","pediatric-neuro-oncology","pilocytic-astrocytoma"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Pilocytic Astrocytoma","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560614/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-001","prompt":"A 52-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder has been stable for 6 years on lithium, with levels of 0.6–0.8 mEq/L. Three weeks ago her primary care physician started hydrochlorothiazide for hypertension. She now has nausea, diarrhea, a coarse tremor, slurred speech, and an unsteady gait. Lithium level is 2.1 mEq/L, sodium 134 mEq/L, and creatinine 1.0 mg/dL (baseline 0.9 mg/dL). The new drug raised her lithium level mainly by which mechanism?","choices":["Inhibition of hepatic lithium metabolism","Increased proximal tubular lithium reabsorption","Displacement of lithium from plasma albumin","Loss of prostaglandin-mediated afferent dilation","Blocked lithium entry through collecting duct ENaC"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Lithium is not metabolized or protein bound; it is filtered and handled like sodium, with most reabsorbed in the proximal tubule. Thiazides cause mild volume and sodium depletion, which triggers compensatory proximal sodium (and therefore lithium) reabsorption, raising levels by roughly 25%–40%. Hepatic metabolism and protein-binding interactions do not apply to lithium. Reduced prostaglandin-mediated afferent arteriolar dilation is how NSAIDs lower GFR and raise lithium. Blocking ENaC entry is the action of amiloride, which is used to treat lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and does not raise lithium levels.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["lithium-toxicity","drug-interactions","bipolar-disorder","renal-pharmacology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Lithium Toxicity","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499992/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-002","prompt":"A 31-year-old man with schizophrenia was hospitalized for agitation and received IM haloperidol several times over 2 days. He is now confused and diaphoretic, with temperature 40.1°C, pulse 128/min, and BP fluctuating between 150/100 and 96/60 mm Hg. He has diffuse lead-pipe rigidity, normal-sized pupils, and hypoactive reflexes without clonus. Creatine kinase is 21,000 U/L. Which mechanism best explains this condition?","choices":["Excess serotonin 5-HT2A receptor stimulation","Defective ryanodine receptor calcium release","Dopamine D2 receptor blockade","Central muscarinic receptor blockade","Excess α1-adrenergic receptor stimulation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome results from dopamine D2 blockade in the hypothalamus (impaired thermoregulation) and nigrostriatal pathway (severe rigidity), producing fever, lead-pipe rigidity, altered mental status, autonomic instability, and markedly elevated CK. It typically develops over days after starting or increasing a high-potency antipsychotic. Serotonin syndrome develops within hours and features hyperreflexia, clonus, mydriasis, and diarrhea rather than bradyreflexia. Malignant hyperthermia (a ryanodine receptor defect) occurs with volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine, and anticholinergic toxicity causes dry skin, mydriasis, and urinary retention without rigidity.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["neuroleptic-malignant-syndrome","antipsychotics","adverse-effects"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482282/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-003","prompt":"A 58-year-old woman with schizoaffective disorder has taken haloperidol for 9 years. Over several months she has developed repetitive lip smacking, tongue protrusion, and writhing finger movements that worsened after benztropine was added. Her psychiatric symptoms are well controlled. Benztropine is stopped, and she is started on a drug approved specifically for this movement disorder. That drug acts by which mechanism?","choices":["Central muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonism","Nonselective β-adrenergic receptor antagonism","Dopamine D2 receptor partial agonism","Inhibition of vesicular monoamine transporter 2","Serotonin 5-HT2A receptor antagonism"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Tardive dyskinesia is a late complication of chronic D2 blockade, thought to reflect upregulated, supersensitive striatal dopamine receptors. Valbenazine and deutetrabenazine, the FDA-approved treatments, inhibit VMAT2, reducing dopamine loading into presynaptic vesicles. Anticholinergics such as benztropine treat acute dystonia and drug-induced parkinsonism but can worsen tardive dyskinesia. Propranolol treats akathisia. Partial D2 agonists and 5-HT2A antagonism describe antipsychotics; switching to clozapine may help some patients, but these mechanisms are not the specific therapy for TD.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["tardive-dyskinesia","antipsychotics","vmat2-inhibitors"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Tardive Dyskinesia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448207/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-004","prompt":"A 45-year-old man taking sertraline for depression is prescribed tramadol for back pain. Twelve hours later he is agitated and diaphoretic, with temperature 39.0°C, pulse 124/min, dilated pupils, hyperactive bowel sounds, hyperreflexia, and inducible clonus most prominent in the legs. Both drugs are stopped, and he receives IV fluids, external cooling, and IV lorazepam, but agitation, tachycardia, and fever persist. The most appropriate next drug acts by which mechanism?","choices":["Dopamine D2 receptor agonism","Skeletal muscle ryanodine receptor antagonism","Muscarinic receptor antagonism","Central α2-adrenergic agonism","Serotonin 5-HT2A receptor antagonism"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Serotonin syndrome follows the combination of serotonergic drugs (here an SSRI and tramadol) and features rapid onset, mental status change, autonomic instability, and neuromuscular hyperactivity with clonus that is greatest in the lower extremities. After stopping the drugs and giving benzodiazepines and supportive care, cyproheptadine, a 5-HT2A (and H1) antagonist, is used for persistent symptoms. Bromocriptine (a D2 agonist) treats neuroleptic malignant syndrome and could worsen serotonin toxicity. Dantrolene targets malignant hyperthermia, and antimuscarinics or clonidine do not address serotonin excess.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["serotonin-syndrome","ssri","cyproheptadine","toxicology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Serotonin Syndrome","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482377/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-005","prompt":"A 29-year-old man with opioid use disorder who uses illicit fentanyl daily wants treatment. He last used 3 hours ago and has no withdrawal signs. In clinic he takes sublingual buprenorphine-naloxone 8 mg/2 mg; within 40 minutes he develops severe anxiety, yawning, rhinorrhea, piloerection, vomiting, and diffuse muscle aches. Which property best explains this reaction?","choices":["High-affinity partial μ-opioid receptor agonism","Sublingual absorption of the coformulated naloxone","Full agonism at κ-opioid receptors","Competitive full antagonism at μ-opioid receptors","Induction of CYP3A4 clearance of fentanyl"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Buprenorphine binds μ-opioid receptors with very high affinity but low intrinsic activity, so it displaces a full agonist such as fentanyl and abruptly lowers net receptor stimulation, causing precipitated withdrawal. That is why induction usually waits until the patient is in moderate withdrawal (and longer after fentanyl, which accumulates in tissue). Naloxone has poor sublingual bioavailability and is included only to deter injection, so it is not the cause. Buprenorphine is a κ antagonist rather than an agonist, and it is a partial agonist, not an antagonist, at μ receptors.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["opioid-use-disorder","buprenorphine","precipitated-withdrawal","pharmacology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Buprenorphine","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459126/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-006","prompt":"A 24-year-old woman with schizophrenia has been treated with risperidone for 5 months, with full remission of her hallucinations after two other antipsychotics failed. She reports no menstrual periods for 3 months and a milky discharge from both nipples. Pregnancy test is negative, TSH is normal, and serum prolactin is 96 ng/mL. Because she has responded so well, her psychiatrist continues risperidone and adds a low dose of a second antipsychotic. Eight weeks later her prolactin is 21 ng/mL and her menses have resumed. Which property of the added drug best explains the fall in prolactin?","choices":["Antagonism at 5-HT2A receptors in the hypothalamus","Partial agonism at D2 receptors on lactotrophs","Antagonism at D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway","Inhibition of CYP2D6 metabolism of risperidone","Agonism at TRH receptors on pituitary lactotrophs"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Dopamine released by tuberoinfundibular neurons into the pituitary portal circulation tonically inhibits prolactin secretion through D2 receptors on lactotrophs; risperidone blocks these receptors, and the resulting hyperprolactinemia suppresses GnRH, causing amenorrhea and galactorrhea. Aripiprazole binds D2 receptors with very high affinity but is a partial agonist, so on lactotrophs it displaces risperidone and restores enough D2 signaling to suppress prolactin; adding low-dose aripiprazole (or switching to it) corrects antipsychotic-induced hyperprolactinemia. Risperidone is already a potent 5-HT2A antagonist, so that property does not protect against prolactin elevation. Further D2 antagonism in the mesolimbic pathway treats psychosis but would not lower prolactin. Inhibiting CYP2D6 would raise risperidone exposure, and TRH stimulates rather than suppresses prolactin release.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["antipsychotics","hyperprolactinemia","dopamine-pathways"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Hyperprolactinemia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537331/"},{"title":"StatPearls: Aripiprazole","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547739/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-007","prompt":"A 17-year-old girl with anorexia nervosa (BMI 13.8 kg/m2) is admitted for medical stabilization, and nasogastric feeding is begun at a high caloric rate. On hospital day 3 she develops confusion, generalized weakness, dyspnea, leg edema, and dark urine; creatine kinase is 4,800 U/L. Which laboratory abnormality most likely underlies these complications?","choices":["Hypoalbuminemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypophosphatemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypoglycemia"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Refeeding syndrome occurs when carbohydrate after prolonged starvation triggers insulin release, driving phosphate (along with potassium and magnesium) into cells for glycolysis and ATP synthesis. Severe hypophosphatemia depletes ATP and 2,3-BPG, causing rhabdomyolysis, respiratory muscle weakness, heart failure, hemolysis, seizures, and delirium, typically within the first few days. Prevention involves cautious caloric advancement, thiamine, and close monitoring and replacement of phosphate, potassium, and magnesium. Hypokalemia, not hyperkalemia, is the expected potassium shift, and hypoalbuminemia or the other abnormalities do not explain rhabdomyolysis and hemolysis.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["eating-disorders","anorexia-nervosa","refeeding-syndrome","electrolytes"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Refeeding Syndrome","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564513/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-008","prompt":"A 36-year-old man has 2 months of low mood, poor concentration, fatigue, and hypersomnia, with no history of mania. Two years ago he stopped escitalopram because of delayed ejaculation and decreased libido. He smokes a pack of cigarettes daily and would like to quit. He has no history of seizures, eating disorders, or heavy alcohol use. Which medication is most appropriate?","choices":["Paroxetine","Mirtazapine","Venlafaxine","Bupropion","Sertraline"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Bupropion, a norepinephrine-dopamine reuptake inhibitor, is an effective antidepressant with minimal sexual side effects and no weight gain, and it is FDA-approved for smoking cessation. It is activating, which suits hypersomnia and fatigue. It lowers the seizure threshold, so it is contraindicated with seizure disorders, bulimia or anorexia, and abrupt alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal, none of which apply here. Paroxetine, sertraline, and venlafaxine carry high rates of sexual dysfunction, and mirtazapine, although sexually sparing, causes sedation and weight gain and does not aid smoking cessation.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["major-depressive-disorder","antidepressants","bupropion","smoking-cessation"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Bupropion","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470212/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-009","prompt":"A 60-year-old man with bipolar disorder who takes lithium is brought in after 4 days of vomiting and diarrhea. He is confused, with a coarse tremor and myoclonus, and has a generalized seizure in the emergency department. Lithium level is 4.3 mEq/L, and creatinine is 2.6 mg/dL (baseline 1.1 mg/dL). IV normal saline is started. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Activated charcoal","Whole-bowel irrigation","Sodium bicarbonate infusion","Forced diuresis with furosemide","Hemodialysis"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Lithium is a small, water-soluble, non-protein-bound ion that is efficiently removed by hemodialysis. Consensus (EXTRIP) recommendations support dialysis for severe toxicity, including a decreased level of consciousness, seizures, or dysrhythmias, or a level above 4.0 mEq/L with impaired kidney function, all present here. Activated charcoal does not bind lithium, and whole-bowel irrigation is used only for large acute ingestions of sustained-release tablets, not chronic toxicity. Loop diuretics and bicarbonate do not meaningfully enhance elimination and diuretics can worsen volume depletion.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["lithium-toxicity","hemodialysis","toxicology","bipolar-disorder"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Lithium Toxicity","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499992/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-010","prompt":"A 21-year-old college student is brought in by her roommate. For the past 3 months she has believed that her professors are inserting thoughts into her mind, hears voices commenting on her actions, and speaks in loosely connected phrases. She has stopped attending classes. She has had no prolonged mood episodes. Urine toxicology is negative, and the medical evaluation is unremarkable. Before these 3 months she functioned well. Which diagnosis is most appropriate?","choices":["Schizophreniform disorder","Schizophrenia","Brief psychotic disorder","Schizoaffective disorder","Delusional disorder, mixed type"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"DSM-5-TR distinguishes the primary psychotic disorders largely by duration: brief psychotic disorder lasts from 1 day to under 1 month, schizophreniform disorder from 1 month to under 6 months, and schizophrenia requires continuous signs for at least 6 months, including prodromal or residual periods. Her 3 months of delusions, hallucinations, and disorganized speech fit schizophreniform disorder, which does not require functional decline. Schizoaffective disorder requires a major mood episode concurrent with psychosis plus at least 2 weeks of psychosis without mood symptoms. Delusional disorder excludes prominent hallucinations and disorganized speech.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["schizophrenia-spectrum","psychosis","dsm-5-tr-criteria"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Schizophrenia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539864/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-011","prompt":"A 52-year-old man is hospitalized after a hip fracture. On hospital day 3 he becomes agitated and disoriented, picks at the air, and reports insects crawling on the walls. Temperature is 38.3°C, pulse 128/min, and BP 172/102 mm Hg; he is diaphoretic with a coarse tremor. His wife reports he drinks about a fifth of vodka daily. Glucose is 96 mg/dL, and he has already received IV thiamine. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["IM haloperidol as needed","IV lorazepam titrated to symptoms","Transdermal clonidine","Oral gabapentin taper","IV dexmedetomidine as monotherapy"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Delirium, hallucinations, fever, and marked autonomic hyperactivity 2–4 days after the last drink indicate delirium tremens, a medical emergency. Benzodiazepines, given IV and titrated to calm alertness (lorazepam, diazepam), are first-line because they treat the underlying GABA-A deficit and prevent seizures; phenobarbital is used for benzodiazepine-refractory cases. Haloperidol lowers the seizure threshold and does not treat withdrawal, so it is only an adjunct for agitation. Clonidine and dexmedetomidine blunt autonomic signs but do not prevent seizures or DTs and should not be used alone, and gabapentin is reserved for mild outpatient withdrawal.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["alcohol-withdrawal","delirium-tremens","benzodiazepines","substance-use"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Alcohol Withdrawal Syndrome","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441882/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-012","prompt":"A 34-year-old woman with opioid use disorder who uses heroin daily requests treatment at a primary care clinic. Her last use was 20 hours ago. She has dilated pupils, rhinorrhea, yawning, gooseflesh, and abdominal cramping; her Clinical Opiate Withdrawal Scale score is 15. Pregnancy test is negative, and liver enzymes are normal. Which is the most appropriate treatment to start today?","choices":["Extended-release naltrexone injection","Methadone prescribed for pharmacy pickup","Buprenorphine-naloxone induction","Clonidine taper without maintenance therapy","Intranasal naloxone kit alone"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"She is in moderate withdrawal (COWS above about 12), so buprenorphine can be started without precipitating withdrawal. Since 2023, any clinician with a DEA Schedule III registration can prescribe buprenorphine for opioid use disorder without a separate waiver, and medication for OUD substantially reduces overdose deaths. Naltrexone requires about 7–10 days of opioid abstinence or it will precipitate severe withdrawal. Methadone for OUD must be dispensed through a certified opioid treatment program, not a retail pharmacy prescription. Withdrawal management without maintenance has high relapse and overdose rates; a naloxone kit should be added but is not treatment on its own.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["opioid-use-disorder","buprenorphine","substance-use","moud"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Opioid Use Disorder: Evaluation and Management","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553166/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-013","prompt":"A 78-year-old man with mild Alzheimer disease (MoCA 21/30) has a gangrenous foot with spreading infection, and vascular surgery recommends below-knee amputation. He declines. He accurately describes his condition, states that refusing could lead to sepsis and death, explains that he values staying mobile at home more than prolonging his life, and has given the same answer in three separate conversations. His daughter, his designated health care proxy, insists that the surgery be done. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Obtain surgical consent from his daughter as proxy","Petition the court for a temporary guardian","Proceed, since dementia precludes capacity","Honor his refusal and continue other treatment","Delay a decision until psychiatry evaluates him"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Decision-making capacity is decision-specific and is assessed by the treating physician: the patient must communicate a consistent choice, understand the relevant information, appreciate how it applies to him, and reason about options in light of his values. He meets all four criteria, so his informed refusal must be respected even though he has mild dementia and his choice may lead to death. A health care proxy's authority is activated only when the patient lacks capacity, and a court guardianship is unnecessary when capacity is intact. Psychiatry consultation can help in unclear cases but is not required, and delaying care here is not justified.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["decision-making-capacity","informed-refusal","ethics","dementia"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Competency and Capacity","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-014","prompt":"A 20-year-old man spends about 3 hours a day washing his hands and showering because of intrusive fears of contamination. He recognizes that the fears are excessive and is distressed by them. He has no depressive symptoms and would prefer to avoid medication if possible. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Long-term psychodynamic psychotherapy","Cognitive processing therapy","Alprazolam as needed","Habit reversal training","Exposure and response prevention"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"First-line treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder is cognitive behavioral therapy with exposure and response prevention, an SSRI (often at higher doses than for depression), or both; ERP fits a patient who prefers to avoid medication. ERP has the patient confront feared stimuli while refraining from compulsions so the anxiety habituates. Cognitive processing therapy is a trauma-focused therapy for PTSD, and habit reversal training targets tics and body-focused repetitive behaviors such as trichotillomania. Benzodiazepines do not treat OCD, and psychodynamic therapy lacks evidence for it.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["ocd","cbt","exposure-response-prevention","anxiety-disorders"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553162/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-015","prompt":"A 30-year-old woman was in a highway collision 2 weeks ago in which another driver was killed. Since then she has had intrusive memories, nightmares, avoidance of driving, feeling detached from her surroundings, hypervigilance, and an exaggerated startle response that interfere with her work. Which diagnosis is most appropriate?","choices":["Acute stress disorder","Posttraumatic stress disorder","Adjustment disorder","Generalized anxiety disorder","Panic disorder"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Acute stress disorder and PTSD share the same trauma exposure and symptom types (intrusion, negative mood, dissociation, avoidance, arousal), and they are distinguished by timing. ASD lasts from 3 days to 1 month after the trauma, while PTSD requires symptoms lasting more than 1 month. At 2 weeks she meets criteria for ASD; if symptoms persist beyond a month, the diagnosis becomes PTSD. Adjustment disorder applies when the stressor need not be traumatic and full ASD or PTSD criteria are not met.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["trauma-related-disorders","acute-stress-disorder","ptsd","dsm-5-tr-criteria"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Acute Stress Disorder","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560815/"}]},{"id":"med-psych-016","prompt":"A 25-year-old woman is evaluated after superficially cutting her forearms following a breakup. Since adolescence she has had intense, unstable relationships in which she alternates between idealizing and devaluing partners, frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, chronic feelings of emptiness, and mood shifts lasting hours. She has no sustained manic or depressive episodes. Which treatment has the strongest evidence for her condition?","choices":["Lithium maintenance therapy","Dialectical behavior therapy","Exposure and response prevention","Long-term scheduled alprazolam","Antipsychotic monotherapy"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This pattern is borderline personality disorder: unstable relationships and self-image, splitting, fear of abandonment, impulsivity, recurrent self-harm, emptiness, and affective instability lasting hours rather than the days to weeks of bipolar mood episodes. Structured psychotherapy is the primary treatment, and dialectical behavior therapy has the strongest evidence for reducing self-harm and suicidal behavior. No medication is FDA-approved for BPD; drugs are used only for specific comorbid symptoms. Benzodiazepines can increase disinhibition and misuse, and lithium is a mood stabilizer for bipolar disorder with risk in overdose.","subject":"psychiatry","tags":["personality-disorders","borderline-personality-disorder","dbt","self-harm"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Borderline Personality Disorder","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430883/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-001","prompt":"A 26-year-old woman at 30 weeks' gestation reports mild breathlessness with exertion but is otherwise well. Respiratory rate is 16/min and SpO2 is 99% on room air; the lungs are clear. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.44, PaCO2 30 mm Hg, PaO2 104 mm Hg, and HCO3− 20 mEq/L. Which physiologic change of pregnancy primarily accounts for her low PaCO2?","choices":["Increased respiratory rate from uterine pressure","Reduced functional residual capacity","Progesterone-driven increase in tidal volume","Primary renal bicarbonate wasting","Hypoxemic drive from dilutional anemia"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Progesterone sensitizes the respiratory center to CO2 and raises minute ventilation by 30%–50%, almost entirely through a larger tidal volume; the respiratory rate changes little. The result is a chronic respiratory alkalosis with PaCO2 around 28–32 mm Hg and a compensatory fall in bicarbonate, which also creates a CO2 gradient favoring fetal gas exchange. FRC does fall as the uterus elevates the diaphragm, but that does not lower PaCO2. The low bicarbonate is renal compensation, not the primary process, and dilutional anemia does not cause hypoxemia; her PaO2 is actually high.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["pregnancy-physiology","acid-base","respiratory-physiology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Physiology, Pregnancy","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559304/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-002","prompt":"A 29-year-old woman with polycystic ovary syndrome (oligomenorrhea, hirsutism, and polycystic ovarian morphology) has been trying to conceive for 14 months. Her partner's semen analysis and her hysterosalpingogram are normal, and her BMI is 31 kg/m2. Her physician recommends the oral drug that current guidelines favor as first-line for ovulation induction in this condition. This drug works primarily by which mechanism?","choices":["Estrogen receptor antagonism in the hypothalamus","Activation of hepatic AMP-activated protein kinase","Pulsatile stimulation of pituitary GnRH receptors","Aromatase inhibition that lowers estrogen feedback","Direct stimulation of ovarian FSH receptors"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Letrozole is now preferred over clomiphene for ovulation induction in PCOS (ACOG, ASRM, and the international PCOS guideline) because it yields higher ovulation and live-birth rates. As an aromatase inhibitor, it lowers estrogen production, reducing negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary so that FSH rises and stimulates follicle development. Clomiphene achieves a similar FSH rise by blocking hypothalamic estrogen receptors, which is the classic but now second-line approach. Metformin (AMPK activation) improves insulin resistance but is a weak ovulation-induction agent alone, and pulsatile GnRH or injectable gonadotropins are not oral first-line therapy.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["pcos","infertility","letrozole","ovulation-induction"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Aromatase Inhibitors","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557856/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-003","prompt":"A 24-year-old man notices a painless, firm enlargement of his right testis over 2 months. Scrotal ultrasound shows a 3-cm heterogeneous intratesticular mass. Serum α-fetoprotein is 860 ng/mL (normal <10), β-hCG is 9 mIU/mL (mildly elevated), and LDH is mildly elevated. Which histologic component is this tumor most likely to contain?","choices":["Pure classic seminoma","Choriocarcinoma elements","Leydig cell tumor","Testicular lymphoma","Yolk sac tumor elements"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"A markedly elevated AFP indicates a nonseminomatous germ cell tumor with yolk sac (endodermal sinus) elements, which produce AFP and show Schiller-Duval bodies on histology. Pure seminoma never raises AFP; if AFP is elevated, the tumor is managed as nonseminomatous even if the pathology looks like seminoma. Seminomas and choriocarcinoma can raise β-hCG, and choriocarcinoma typically produces very high hCG levels with early hematogenous spread rather than AFP. Leydig cell tumors produce androgens or estrogens, and testicular lymphoma occurs mostly in men over 60.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["testicular-tumors","germ-cell-tumors","tumor-markers"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Testicular Cancer","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563159/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-004","prompt":"A 37-year-old woman, G2P1, at 16 weeks' gestation chooses second-trimester maternal serum (quad) screening. Results show low α-fetoprotein, low unconjugated estriol, elevated hCG, and elevated inhibin A. Ultrasound confirms the dating and a single fetus. Which condition is this pattern most suggestive of?","choices":["Trisomy 21","Trisomy 18","Open neural tube defect","Abdominal wall defect","Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The quad screen pattern for trisomy 21 is low AFP and low estriol with high hCG and high inhibin A. Trisomy 18 lowers AFP, estriol, and hCG, with inhibin A generally normal. Open neural tube defects and abdominal wall defects (gastroschisis, omphalocele) cause an elevated AFP. Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome, a cholesterol synthesis defect, produces a very low estriol. Screening results are probabilities only; a positive screen is followed by counseling and offered cell-free DNA screening or diagnostic testing (CVS or amniocentesis).","subject":"reproductive","tags":["prenatal-screening","quad-screen","aneuploidy"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Prenatal Genetic Screening","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557702/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-005","prompt":"A 31-year-old primigravida at 33 weeks' gestation has BP of 158/104 mm Hg on two readings 4 hours apart, a urine protein-to-creatinine ratio of 0.6, and a new headache. Her BP was normal before 20 weeks. Platelets and liver enzymes are normal. Which process most likely initiated her condition?","choices":["Placental overproduction of placental growth factor","Abnormal remodeling of uterine spiral arteries","Immune complex deposition in the glomeruli","Aldosterone-mediated sodium and water retention","Antiphospholipid antibody–mediated thrombosis"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Preeclampsia begins with incomplete trophoblastic invasion and remodeling of the maternal spiral arteries, leaving narrow, high-resistance vessels and an ischemic placenta. The stressed placenta releases antiangiogenic factors, especially soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1 (sFlt-1) and soluble endoglin, which cause widespread maternal endothelial dysfunction, hypertension, and proteinuria (glomerular endotheliosis). Placental growth factor is decreased, not increased, which is why a high sFlt-1/PlGF ratio is used as a biomarker. Antiphospholipid syndrome raises preeclampsia risk but is not the underlying mechanism in a typical patient, and immune complex deposition describes lupus nephritis.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["preeclampsia","placental-pathology","hypertensive-disorders-of-pregnancy"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Preeclampsia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK570611/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-006","prompt":"A 16-year-old girl is evaluated for primary amenorrhea. She is 172 cm tall with Tanner 5 breast development but sparse pubic and axillary hair. Pelvic examination shows a short, blind-ending vagina, ultrasound shows no uterus, and there are bilateral inguinal masses. Karyotype is 46,XY, and serum testosterone is in the normal adult male range. Which defect is most likely?","choices":["5α-reductase type 2 deficiency","Failure of Müllerian duct development","Androgen receptor dysfunction","Gonadal dysgenesis with streak gonads","Deficient anti-Müllerian hormone action"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome results from a nonfunctional androgen receptor in a 46,XY individual with testes. Sertoli cells make anti-Müllerian hormone, so the uterus and upper vagina are absent, while the testes produce testosterone that cannot act; it is aromatized to estrogen, producing breast development, with scant pubic and axillary hair. Müllerian agenesis (MRKH) also causes absent uterus and amenorrhea but occurs in 46,XX females with normal pubic hair and ovaries. 5α-reductase deficiency causes ambiguous genitalia with virilization at puberty, and Swyer syndrome (XY gonadal dysgenesis) has a uterus and absent breast development because streak gonads make neither hormone.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["amenorrhea","androgen-insensitivity","disorders-of-sex-development"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542206/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-007","prompt":"A boy is born at 39 weeks weighing 4,600 g. His mother had gestational diabetes with fasting glucose values often above 110 mg/dL despite treatment during the third trimester. At 1 hour of life he is jittery, and plasma glucose is 26 mg/dL. Which mechanism best explains his hypoglycemia?","choices":["Transplacental passage of maternal insulin","Immature hepatic gluconeogenic enzymes","Depleted glycogen from growth restriction","Fetal β-cell hyperplasia, hyperinsulinemia","Neonatal cortisol deficiency"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Maternal glucose crosses the placenta freely, but maternal insulin does not. Chronic fetal hyperglycemia stimulates fetal β-cell hyperplasia and hyperinsulinemia, which promotes macrosomia (insulin acts as a fetal growth hormone). After the cord is clamped, the glucose supply stops abruptly while insulin levels stay high, causing hypoglycemia in the first hours of life. Limited glycogen stores explain hypoglycemia in growth-restricted or preterm infants, not a macrosomic term infant, and immature gluconeogenesis contributes in prematurity.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["gestational-diabetes","neonatal-hypoglycemia","infant-of-diabetic-mother"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Neonatal Hypoglycemia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537105/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-008","prompt":"A 28-year-old woman, G2P1, has 2 days of mild left pelvic pain and light vaginal bleeding; her last menstrual period was 6½ weeks ago. BP is 118/74 mm Hg and pulse 78/min; the abdomen is soft with mild left adnexal tenderness. Serum β-hCG is 2,600 mIU/mL. Transvaginal ultrasound shows no intrauterine pregnancy, a 2.3-cm left adnexal mass without fetal cardiac activity, and no free fluid. Hemoglobin, creatinine, and liver enzymes are normal. She is not breastfeeding and can reliably return for follow-up. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Laparoscopic salpingectomy","Repeat β-hCG in 48 hours","Dilation and curettage","Expectant management","Intramuscular methotrexate"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"An empty uterus with an extrauterine adnexal mass and an hCG of 2,600 mIU/mL indicates ectopic pregnancy. She is hemodynamically stable with an unruptured mass under about 3.5 cm, no embryonic cardiac activity, an hCG well below 5,000 mIU/mL, normal blood counts and renal and hepatic function, and reliable follow-up, so methotrexate is appropriate; hCG is then followed until it is undetectable. Surgery (salpingectomy or salpingostomy) is indicated for hemodynamic instability, rupture, or contraindications to methotrexate. Serial hCG is used for a pregnancy of unknown location, not when ultrasound already shows an adnexal ectopic, and expectant management is reserved for very low, falling hCG levels.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["ectopic-pregnancy","methotrexate","early-pregnancy"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Ectopic Pregnancy","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539860/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-009","prompt":"A 27-year-old primigravida at 35 weeks' gestation has BP 166/112 mm Hg on repeat measurement, a severe headache unrelieved by acetaminophen, and 2+ proteinuria. Platelets are 168,000/mm3, AST 32 U/L, and creatinine 0.8 mg/dL. The fetal heart rate tracing is category I, and the fetus is vertex. IV labetalol and magnesium sulfate are started. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Delivery after maternal stabilization","Expectant management until 37 weeks","Betamethasone, then delivery in 48 hours","Immediate cesarean delivery","IV furosemide and repeat labs in 6 hours"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Preeclampsia with severe features (BP ≥160/110 mm Hg and a persistent cerebral symptom) at or beyond 34 weeks is managed by delivery once the mother is stabilized with antihypertensives and magnesium sulfate for seizure prophylaxis. Expectant management to 37 weeks applies only to preeclampsia without severe features. Delaying delivery 48 hours for corticosteroids is considered only before 34 weeks in stable patients, and late-preterm steroids should not delay indicated delivery. Severe preeclampsia is not by itself an indication for cesarean, so induction of labor is reasonable with a reassuring tracing and vertex presentation.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["preeclampsia","magnesium-sulfate","obstetric-management","acog"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Preeclampsia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK570611/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-010","prompt":"A 32-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma and chronic hypertension delivers vaginally at 39 weeks. Fifteen minutes later she has brisk bleeding, with an estimated blood loss of 1,100 mL. The uterus remains soft and boggy despite bimanual massage and an oxytocin infusion; the placenta is intact, and there are no lacerations. BP is 150/96 mm Hg. Which additional uterotonic is most appropriate?","choices":["Methylergonovine","Misoprostol","Carboprost tromethamine","Dinoprostone","Terbutaline"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Uterine atony is the most common cause of postpartum hemorrhage; after massage and oxytocin, second-line uterotonics are chosen around contraindications. Methylergonovine, an ergot alkaloid, causes vasoconstriction and is contraindicated in hypertension and preeclampsia. Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α) causes bronchoconstriction and is contraindicated in asthma. Misoprostol (a PGE1 analog) has neither restriction, so it is the best option here; tranexamic acid should also be given early as an antifibrinolytic adjunct. Dinoprostone is used for cervical ripening, and terbutaline relaxes the uterus.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["postpartum-hemorrhage","uterine-atony","uterotonics"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Postpartum Hemorrhage","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499988/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-011","prompt":"A 66-year-old woman asks whether she can stop cervical cancer screening. At age 47 she was treated with a loop electrosurgical excision procedure (LEEP) for CIN 2; since then, all of her screening tests, most recently HPV-cytology co-testing at age 64, have been negative. She has a cervix, is not immunocompromised, and had no in utero DES exposure. Which recommendation is most appropriate?","choices":["Stop now, since she is over 65 with negative tests","Continue screening until age 70, as for all women","Continue screening until 25 years after treatment","Continue annual cytology for the rest of her life","Stop unless she has a new sexual partner"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Women older than 65 may stop screening only after adequate negative prior screening and no history of CIN 2 or higher; the USPSTF recommendation explicitly excludes women with prior high-grade lesions. ASCCP and ACS guidance call for continued routine surveillance for at least 25 years after treatment of CIN 2+, even past age 65, so she should continue until about age 72. Stopping now applies the standard age-65 rule to the wrong patient. There is no universal age-70 rule or need for lifelong annual cytology, and a new sexual partner does not change stopping criteria.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["cervical-cancer-screening","uspstf","cin","preventive-care"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"USPSTF Recommendation: Cervical Cancer: Screening","url":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org/uspstf/recommendation/cervical-cancer-screening"}]},{"id":"med-repro-012","prompt":"A 27-year-old woman finds a lump in her right breast. Examination shows a 2-cm smooth, firm, mobile, nontender mass in the upper outer quadrant with no skin changes, nipple discharge, or axillary lymphadenopathy. She has no family history of breast or ovarian cancer. Which is the most appropriate initial imaging study?","choices":["Diagnostic mammography","Contrast-enhanced breast MRI","Screening mammography","Targeted breast ultrasound","Diagnostic mammography with ultrasound"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"For a palpable breast mass in a woman younger than 30, targeted ultrasound is the initial imaging study (ACR Appropriateness Criteria); dense breast tissue limits mammography in young women, and ultrasound distinguishes cysts from solid masses. This presentation suggests a fibroadenoma, and ultrasound features guide whether core biopsy or follow-up is needed. Diagnostic mammography, with or without ultrasound, is the initial study at age 40 or older (and an option at 30–39). Screening mammography is never appropriate for evaluating a symptomatic finding, and MRI is not a first-line test for a palpable mass.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["breast-mass","breast-imaging","fibroadenoma"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: New Palpable Breast Mass","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560757/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-013","prompt":"A 14-year-old boy wakes with sudden, severe left scrotal pain and vomits twice. He arrives 3 hours after onset. The left testis is high-riding with a horizontal lie and is diffusely tender, and the left cremasteric reflex is absent. There is no fever or dysuria, and urinalysis is normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Scrotal Doppler ultrasonography","Ceftriaxone and doxycycline","Manual detorsion and discharge home","Scrotal elevation and NSAIDs","Immediate surgical exploration"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Sudden severe testicular pain with nausea and vomiting, a high-riding horizontally oriented testis, and an absent cremasteric reflex make testicular torsion highly likely (a high TWIST score). When the clinical diagnosis is clear, imaging should not delay surgery, because salvage rates fall sharply after about 6 hours; exploration includes detorsion and bilateral orchiopexy. Doppler ultrasound is used when the diagnosis is uncertain. Manual detorsion may be attempted while arranging surgery, but orchiopexy is still required. Antibiotics and scrotal elevation treat epididymitis, which develops gradually and usually has pyuria.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["testicular-torsion","acute-scrotum","urologic-emergency"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Testicular Torsion","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448199/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-014","prompt":"A 21-year-old woman has 4 days of lower abdominal pain and dyspareunia. Temperature is 38.1°C. She has cervical motion tenderness, uterine and bilateral adnexal tenderness, and mucopurulent cervical discharge. Pregnancy test is negative, and ultrasound shows no tubo-ovarian abscess. She is tolerating oral intake and appears well. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["IM ceftriaxone; doxycycline and metronidazole for 14 days","IM ceftriaxone; azithromycin 1 g orally once","IM ceftriaxone; doxycycline for 7 days","Oral cefixime; doxycycline and metronidazole for 14 days","Oral metronidazole and doxycycline for 14 days"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"She meets clinical criteria for pelvic inflammatory disease and can be treated as an outpatient. The 2021 CDC regimen is ceftriaxone 500 mg IM once plus doxycycline 100 mg twice daily and metronidazole 500 mg twice daily, each for 14 days, covering gonorrhea, chlamydia, and the anaerobes and BV-associated organisms of polymicrobial upper-tract infection. A single dose of azithromycin or a 7-day doxycycline course is treatment for uncomplicated cervicitis, not PID. Oral cephalosporins such as cefixime are not recommended for PID, and an oral regimen without a cephalosporin leaves gonorrhea untreated.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["pid","sexually-transmitted-infections","cdc-guidelines"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Pelvic Inflammatory Disease","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499959/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-015","prompt":"A 24-year-old woman requests contraception. She has migraine headaches that are preceded by 20 minutes of flickering zigzag lines in her vision. She does not smoke, BP is 116/72 mm Hg, and she has no other medical problems. Which method is contraindicated for her?","choices":["Levonorgestrel intrauterine device","Combined estrogen-progestin pill","Copper intrauterine device","Etonogestrel subdermal implant","Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Migraine with aura is a US MEC category 4 (unacceptable risk) condition for combined hormonal contraception at any age because estrogen further increases the risk of ischemic stroke. All estrogen-containing methods, including the pill, patch, and ring, should be avoided. Progestin-only methods (levonorgestrel IUD, etonogestrel implant, DMPA, progestin-only pills) and the copper IUD do not carry this stroke risk and are acceptable. DMPA's reversible bone density loss is a counseling point, not a contraindication in this patient.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["contraception","us-mec","migraine-with-aura"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Contraception","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536949/"}]},{"id":"med-repro-016","prompt":"A 58-year-old man with no urinary symptoms asks whether he should be screened for prostate cancer. He has no family history of prostate cancer, is not of African ancestry, and is in good health. According to the USPSTF recommendation, which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Order a PSA test every year starting now","Advise against PSA screening at his age","Shared decision about PSA-based screening","Perform a digital rectal exam instead of PSA","Check PSA only if urinary symptoms develop"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The USPSTF gives PSA-based screening a grade C recommendation for men aged 55 to 69: the decision should be individual, after the clinician discusses the small potential mortality benefit and the harms of false positives, biopsy complications, and overdiagnosis and overtreatment (incontinence, erectile dysfunction). Routine annual screening without shared decision-making is not recommended, and the USPSTF recommends against screening men 70 and older (grade D), not men his age. Digital rectal examination is not recommended as a screening test, and urinary symptoms usually reflect BPH rather than early prostate cancer.","subject":"reproductive","tags":["prostate-cancer","psa-screening","uspstf","shared-decision-making"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"USPSTF Recommendation: Prostate Cancer: Screening","url":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org/uspstf/recommendation/prostate-cancer-screening"}]},{"id":"med-msk-001","prompt":"A 13-year-old boy stepped on a nail that went through the rubber sole of his athletic shoe 3 weeks ago. He now has worsening heel pain and swelling and difficulty bearing weight; temperature is 38.0°C. MRI shows osteomyelitis of the calcaneus. A bone biopsy culture grows an aerobic, oxidase-positive, non-lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod. Which organism is most likely?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Salmonella enterica","Pasteurella multocida","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Eikenella corrodens"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Puncture wounds through the foam or rubber of athletic shoes classically inoculate Pseudomonas aeruginosa, an aerobic, oxidase-positive, non-lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod, causing osteomyelitis or osteochondritis of the foot bones. Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause of osteomyelitis overall, but it is a gram-positive coccus. Salmonella is oxidase-negative and is associated with osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease. Pasteurella multocida follows cat or dog bites, and Eikenella corrodens follows human bites or clenched-fist injuries.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["osteomyelitis","pseudomonas","microbiology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Osteomyelitis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532250/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-002","prompt":"A 56-year-old man with a kidney transplant takes azathioprine, tacrolimus, and prednisone. After his third gout flare in a year, a urate-lowering drug is started. Four weeks later he has fatigue and fever; WBC count is 1,600/mm3, hemoglobin 8.1 g/dL, and platelets 52,000/mm3. He has had no diarrhea, myalgias, or weakness. The new drug most likely caused these findings by which mechanism?","choices":["Inhibition of URAT1 urate reabsorption","Oxidation of uric acid to allantoin","Inhibition of thiopurine methyltransferase","Inhibition of hepatic CYP3A4 metabolism","Inhibition of xanthine oxidase"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Azathioprine is converted to 6-mercaptopurine, which is inactivated partly by xanthine oxidase. Allopurinol (or febuxostat) inhibits xanthine oxidase, so 6-MP and its cytotoxic thioguanine nucleotides accumulate, causing severe myelosuppression; if a xanthine oxidase inhibitor is essential, the azathioprine dose must be cut substantially (often to about 25%) or the drug changed. TPMT is the other inactivating enzyme, and inherited TPMT deficiency also causes azathioprine toxicity, but no urate-lowering drug inhibits TPMT. Probenecid (URAT1 inhibition) and pegloticase (urate oxidation) do not interact with azathioprine this way, and CYP3A4 inhibition would raise tacrolimus levels, causing nephrotoxicity rather than pancytopenia.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["gout","allopurinol","azathioprine","drug-interactions"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Allopurinol","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499942/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-003","prompt":"A 23-year-old woman has 2 months of fatigue, painful swollen hand joints, a photosensitive malar rash, and new leg swelling. BP is 148/92 mm Hg. Urinalysis shows 3+ protein, red blood cell casts, and dysmorphic red cells. Anti-double-stranded DNA antibodies are elevated, and C3 and C4 levels are low. Renal biopsy shows subendothelial immune deposits. Which mechanism is primarily responsible for her kidney injury?","choices":["Immune complex deposition (type III)","Antibodies to type IV collagen (type II)","IgE-mediated mast cell activation (type I)","T cell–mediated granuloma formation (type IV)","ANCA-mediated neutrophil activation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Lupus nephritis is a type III hypersensitivity reaction: immune complexes, often containing anti-dsDNA antibodies, deposit in the glomeruli and activate complement, which is why C3 and C4 are low and anti-dsDNA titers track nephritis activity. Subendothelial deposits are typical of diffuse proliferative (class IV) lupus nephritis, the most common and severe form. Anti-GBM disease is a type II reaction with linear IgG deposition and normal complement. ANCA-associated vasculitis is pauci-immune, with few or no deposits.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["sle","lupus-nephritis","hypersensitivity"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Lupus Nephritis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499817/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-004","prompt":"An 8-year-old boy falls onto his outstretched hand from monkey bars. Radiographs show a displaced extension-type supracondylar humerus fracture. The radial pulse is present. He cannot flex the interphalangeal joint of the thumb or the distal interphalangeal joint of the index finger, so he cannot make an 'OK' sign, but sensation throughout the hand is intact. Which nerve is most likely injured?","choices":["Posterior interosseous nerve","Anterior interosseous nerve","Ulnar nerve at the elbow","Median nerve at the wrist","Radial nerve proper"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The anterior interosseous nerve, a purely motor branch of the median nerve, supplies flexor pollicis longus, the lateral half of flexor digitorum profundus (index and middle fingers), and pronator quadratus, and it is the nerve most often injured in extension-type supracondylar fractures. Loss of thumb IP and index DIP flexion prevents an 'OK' sign, and sensation is spared because the nerve has no cutaneous branch. A median nerve injury at the wrist would cause thenar weakness and sensory loss over the radial palm and fingers. The posterior interosseous and radial nerves control finger and wrist extension, and the ulnar nerve controls most intrinsic hand muscles.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["upper-limb-nerve-injury","supracondylar-fracture","pediatric-orthopedics","anatomy"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Anterior Interosseous Syndrome","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK525956/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-005","prompt":"A 12-year-old boy has 6 weeks of worsening right thigh pain, low-grade fevers, and a tender mass. ESR is 64 mm/h, and blood cultures are negative. Radiographs show a permeative lytic lesion in the femoral diaphysis with multilayered periosteal new bone and an adjacent soft-tissue mass. Biopsy shows sheets of uniform small round cells with scant glycogen-rich cytoplasm and no osteoid; the cells are negative for myogenin, CD45, and TdT. Which genetic alteration is most likely?","choices":["PAX3-FOXO1 fusion from t(2;13)","SS18-SSX fusion from t(X;18)","EWSR1-FLI1 fusion from t(11;22)","Biallelic loss of the RB1 tumor suppressor","MYC-IGH fusion from t(8;14)"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Ewing sarcoma typically arises in the diaphysis of long bones or the pelvis in children and adolescents, often with fever and an elevated ESR that can mimic osteomyelitis, and it produces a permeative lesion with layered ('onion-skin') periosteal new bone and a soft-tissue mass. Histology shows uniform small round blue cells with glycogen-rich cytoplasm and strong membranous CD99 staining, and about 85% carry the t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1 fusion. Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma (PAX3-FOXO1) and Burkitt lymphoma (MYC-IGH) are also small round blue cell tumors, but rhabdomyosarcoma is myogenin-positive and lymphomas express CD45, so the negative stains exclude them. Synovial sarcoma, with t(X;18), is usually a spindle cell soft-tissue tumor near large joints, and RB1 loss predisposes to osteosarcoma, which is metaphyseal, produces osteoid, and shows a sunburst pattern with a Codman triangle.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["bone-tumors","ewing-sarcoma","molecular-pathology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Ewing Sarcoma","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559183/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-006","prompt":"A 67-year-old woman has a DXA T-score of −2.8 at the femoral neck. She is prescribed a once-weekly tablet and told to take it on an empty stomach with a full glass of plain water and to remain upright for at least 30 minutes before eating. Which is the mechanism of action of this drug?","choices":["Monoclonal antibody that neutralizes RANKL","Intermittent activation of osteoblast PTH1 receptors","Selective estrogen receptor agonism in bone tissue","Inhibition of farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase","Monoclonal antibody that neutralizes sclerostin"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Alendronate and risedronate are nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates that bind hydroxyapatite and, when taken up by osteoclasts, inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase, impairing protein prenylation and causing osteoclast dysfunction and apoptosis. Poor oral absorption and the risk of pill esophagitis explain the dosing instructions. Denosumab (anti-RANKL) and romosozumab (anti-sclerostin) are injectable antibodies, teriparatide and abaloparatide are daily injections that activate PTH1 receptors intermittently, and raloxifene is a daily SERM without these administration rules.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["osteoporosis","bisphosphonates","pharmacology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Alendronate","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526073/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-007","prompt":"A 27-year-old man has 2 years of low back pain and stiffness that is worst in the morning, lasts over an hour, and improves with exercise. Last year he had an episode of a painful red eye with photophobia. Examination shows reduced lumbar flexion on the Schober test and tenderness over both sacroiliac joints. Pelvic radiographs show bilateral sacroiliitis. Which cardiac complication is most characteristic of this disease?","choices":["Mitral valve prolapse","Libman-Sacks endocarditis","Constrictive pericarditis","Coronary artery aneurysms","Aortic regurgitation"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Ankylosing spondylitis, an HLA-B27–associated spondyloarthritis, causes inflammatory back pain, sacroiliitis, and anterior uveitis. Its characteristic cardiac involvement is aortitis of the aortic root with aortic regurgitation, along with conduction disease such as AV block. Libman-Sacks (nonbacterial verrucous) endocarditis is a feature of SLE, mitral valve prolapse is associated with Marfan and Ehlers-Danlos syndromes, and coronary artery aneurysms occur in Kawasaki disease. Pericarditis is more typical of SLE and rheumatoid arthritis.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["spondyloarthropathy","ankylosing-spondylitis","hla-b27","extra-articular"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Ankylosing Spondylitis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470173/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-008","prompt":"A 71-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73 m2), heart failure, and a past bleeding duodenal ulcer has acute pain, swelling, and redness of the right first metatarsophalangeal joint. Arthrocentesis shows needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals, and the Gram stain and culture are negative. He takes no urate-lowering therapy. Which is the most appropriate treatment for this flare?","choices":["A short course of oral prednisone","Indomethacin three times daily","Full-dose allopurinol as monotherapy","Probenecid twice daily","Pegloticase infusion"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Options for an acute gout flare are NSAIDs, colchicine, or glucocorticoids, chosen by comorbidities. NSAIDs such as indomethacin are contraindicated by his advanced CKD, heart failure, and prior GI bleeding, so a short course of oral glucocorticoids (or an intra-articular injection for a single joint) is appropriate. Urate-lowering therapy can be started during a flare under ACR guidance, but only with flare treatment and at a low starting dose in CKD (allopurinol 50 mg/day or less), and it does not treat the acute attack. Probenecid is ineffective with low GFR, and pegloticase is reserved for refractory chronic tophaceous gout.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["gout","acute-flare","ckd","acr-guidelines"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Gout","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK546606/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-009","prompt":"A 22-year-old woman has 4 days of fever, migrating pain in her wrists and knees, and pain along the back of her right hand. Examination shows tenosynovitis of the right wrist extensor tendons and several painless pustules on erythematous bases on her forearms and shins. She has a new sexual partner. Pregnancy test is negative, and nucleic acid amplification testing for chlamydia is negative. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM as a single dose","Ceftriaxone 1 g IV every 24 hours","Doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily","Vancomycin IV every 12 hours","Prednisone and naproxen"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The triad of tenosynovitis, migratory polyarthralgia, and painless vesiculopustular skin lesions in a sexually active young adult indicates disseminated gonococcal infection. CDC recommends ceftriaxone 1 g IV or IM every 24 hours, typically with hospitalization, continued until 24–48 hours after improvement and completing at least 7 days; doxycycline is added if chlamydia has not been excluded. A single 500-mg IM dose treats only uncomplicated mucosal gonorrhea. Doxycycline alone does not reliably cover gonorrhea, vancomycin targets gram-positive organisms, and anti-inflammatory therapy would suit reactive arthritis but leaves the infection untreated.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["septic-arthritis","disseminated-gonococcal-infection","sexually-transmitted-infections"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Gonococcal Arthritis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470439/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-010","prompt":"A 74-year-old woman has 3 weeks of new left temporal headaches, scalp tenderness when brushing her hair, and jaw pain when chewing. Yesterday she had a 5-minute episode of blurred vision in her left eye that resolved. ESR is 96 mm/h and CRP 8.4 mg/dL. The left temporal artery is tender with a reduced pulse. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Temporal artery biopsy before any steroids","Prednisone 15 mg daily with slow taper","High-dose glucocorticoids immediately","Urgent MRI of the brain and orbits","Low-dose aspirin and observation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Giant cell arteritis with transient visual loss threatens permanent blindness from arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy, so high-dose glucocorticoids (often IV methylprednisolone pulses when vision is involved) should start immediately. Temporal artery biopsy or vascular ultrasound should be done soon, ideally within 1–2 weeks, and remains diagnostic for days to weeks after steroids begin, so testing must not delay treatment. Prednisone 12.5–25 mg daily is the dose for isolated polymyalgia rheumatica and is inadequate for GCA. Tocilizumab may be added later as a steroid-sparing agent.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["giant-cell-arteritis","polymyalgia-rheumatica","vasculitis","glucocorticoids"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Giant Cell Arteritis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459376/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-011","prompt":"A 72-year-old woman has a DXA T-score of −2.9 at the lumbar spine and no prior fractures. She has a distal esophageal stricture that requires periodic dilation and a history of an unprovoked deep venous thrombosis. Creatinine clearance is 64 mL/min, serum calcium and 25-hydroxyvitamin D are normal, and her dental health is good. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral alendronate once weekly","Raloxifene once daily","Calcitonin nasal spray daily","Yearly IV zoledronic acid","Estrogen-progestin therapy"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"She has osteoporosis that warrants pharmacotherapy, and bisphosphonates are first-line. An esophageal stricture is a contraindication to oral bisphosphonates because of their esophageal toxicity and the need for passage into the stomach, so yearly IV zoledronic acid is preferred; her creatinine clearance is above the 35 mL/min threshold. Raloxifene and estrogen therapy both increase venous thromboembolism risk and are contraindicated after a DVT. Calcitonin is a weak antiresorptive with minimal fracture benefit and is no longer recommended for osteoporosis treatment.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["osteoporosis","bisphosphonates","zoledronic-acid","contraindications"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Zoledronate","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585053/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-012","prompt":"A 24-year-old man underwent intramedullary nailing of a closed tibial shaft fracture 10 hours ago. He now reports severe, worsening leg pain despite IV opioids and numbness between his first and second toes. The anterior leg is tense, and passive plantar flexion of the toes causes severe pain. The dorsalis pedis pulse is palpable. Diastolic BP is 70 mm Hg, and the anterior compartment pressure is 52 mm Hg. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Elevate the leg well above heart level","More opioids and reassess in 4 hours","CT angiography of the leg","Repeat pressure measurement in 2 hours","Emergency four-compartment fasciotomy"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Pain out of proportion that worsens with passive stretch, paresthesias in the deep peroneal nerve distribution, and a tense compartment after a tibial fracture indicate acute compartment syndrome. A delta pressure (diastolic BP minus compartment pressure) under 30 mm Hg, here 18 mm Hg, confirms it, and emergency fasciotomy is required to prevent muscle and nerve necrosis. Palpable pulses do not exclude compartment syndrome, because compartment pressure rarely exceeds arterial pressure, so CT angiography is not needed. Elevating the limb above the heart lowers perfusion pressure (keep it at heart level), and delays for analgesia or repeat measurements risk irreversible injury.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["compartment-syndrome","orthopedic-emergency","tibial-fracture"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Acute Compartment Syndrome","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448124/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-013","prompt":"A 12-year-old boy with a BMI at the 98th percentile has had 4 weeks of left knee pain and a limp. He can bear weight. The knee examination is normal; when the left hip is flexed, the thigh rotates externally, and internal rotation is limited. AP and frog-leg lateral radiographs of the pelvis show posterior and medial displacement of the left femoral epiphysis relative to the metaphysis. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["In situ screw fixation of the left hip","Closed reduction and spica casting","Abduction bracing and observation","Crutches, NSAIDs, and observation","Physical therapy for hip strengthening"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis is a displacement of the femoral epiphysis through the physis, typically in obese adolescents, and often presents with referred knee pain and obligatory external rotation with hip flexion. Treatment is prompt non-weight-bearing and surgical in situ fixation with a single cannulated screw to prevent further slipping. Forceful reduction increases the risk of avascular necrosis, so the slip is pinned where it lies. Bracing and observation are approaches used in Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, and conservative care allows progression to an unstable slip.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["pediatric-orthopedics","scfe","hip-disorders"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538302/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-014","prompt":"A girl born at 39 weeks by planned cesarean delivery for frank breech presentation is examined at 2 days of age. Ortolani and Barlow maneuvers are negative, hip abduction is full and symmetric, and thigh skin folds are symmetric. There is no family history of hip dysplasia. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Hip ultrasonography before hospital discharge","Hip ultrasonography at about 6 weeks of age","Pelvic radiograph at about 6 weeks of age","Pavlik harness for 6 weeks as prophylaxis","No imaging if later hip exams are normal"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Breech presentation in the third trimester is a major risk factor for developmental dysplasia of the hip, and the AAP recommends imaging for breech girls even with a normal examination. Ultrasound is preferred at about 6 weeks of age, because physiologic laxity in the first weeks produces false-positive findings, or a pelvic radiograph can be obtained after about 4 months once the femoral heads ossify. Radiographs are unreliable at 6 weeks because the femoral head is still cartilaginous. A Pavlik harness treats a dislocatable or dysplastic hip, not a normal exam, and relying on exam alone can miss dysplasia in a high-risk infant.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["pediatric-orthopedics","ddh","screening"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563157/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-015","prompt":"A 68-year-old woman has years of hand pain that worsens with use during the day and morning stiffness lasting about 15 minutes. Examination shows hard, bony enlargements of the distal and proximal interphalangeal joints without warmth; the metacarpophalangeal joints and wrists are spared. ESR is 14 mm/h. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Psoriatic arthritis","Osteoarthritis","Chronic tophaceous gout","Calcium pyrophosphate arthropathy"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Osteoarthritis causes use-related pain, brief morning stiffness (under 30 minutes), and hard bony enlargements of the DIP joints (Heberden nodes) and PIP joints (Bouchard nodes), typically sparing the MCPs and wrists, with normal inflammatory markers. Rheumatoid arthritis causes prolonged morning stiffness and soft, warm synovitis of the MCPs, PIPs, and wrists while sparing the DIPs. Psoriatic arthritis can involve the DIPs but is inflammatory, with dactylitis and nail changes. Calcium pyrophosphate arthropathy characteristically affects the second and third MCPs and wrists.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["osteoarthritis","rheumatoid-arthritis","hand-arthritis"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Osteoarthritis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482326/"}]},{"id":"med-msk-016","prompt":"A 41-year-old man has 6 months of pain and swelling in several fingers and toes. His left third toe is diffusely swollen. Several fingernails show pitting and onycholysis, and he has scaly plaques behind his ears and in the gluteal cleft. Hand radiographs show erosions at the distal interphalangeal joints with adjacent fluffy periostitis and a 'pencil-in-cup' deformity. Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies are negative. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Reactive arthritis","Erosive osteoarthritis","Psoriatic arthritis","Chronic tophaceous gout"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Psoriatic arthritis is a seronegative spondyloarthritis marked by DIP involvement, dactylitis (sausage digits), enthesitis, and nail pitting and onycholysis, often with psoriasis in hidden areas such as the scalp, ears, and gluteal cleft. Radiographs show erosions with adjacent new bone formation and pencil-in-cup deformities. Reactive arthritis also causes dactylitis but follows a genitourinary or GI infection and usually affects the large joints of the legs. Erosive osteoarthritis produces central 'gull-wing' erosions without periostitis, and rheumatoid arthritis spares the DIPs and is usually seropositive.","subject":"musculoskeletal","tags":["psoriatic-arthritis","spondyloarthropathy","dermatology"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Psoriatic Arthritis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547710/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-001","prompt":"A 58-year-old woman with long-standing, active rheumatoid arthritis reports fatigue. Hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL, MCV 84 fL, serum iron 38 μg/dL (low), total iron-binding capacity 210 μg/dL (low), ferritin 340 ng/mL, and the reticulocyte index is low. Stool testing for occult blood is negative, and creatinine is normal. Which mechanism best explains her anemia?","choices":["Reduced renal erythropoietin synthesis","Lead inhibition of ferrochelatase","Chronic occult gastrointestinal iron loss","Impaired β-globin chain synthesis","Hepcidin-induced ferroportin degradation"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"In anemia of inflammation (chronic disease), IL-6 induces hepatic hepcidin, which binds ferroportin and causes its internalization and degradation. Iron is trapped in macrophages and enterocytes, so serum iron falls while ferritin, an acute-phase reactant and marker of stores, is normal or high, and TIBC is low. Iron deficiency from GI loss would show low ferritin and high TIBC. Reduced erythropoietin drives the anemia of CKD, but her creatinine is normal. Lead poisoning and β-thalassemia trait typically cause microcytosis with basophilic stippling or target cells.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["anemia","anemia-of-chronic-disease","iron-metabolism","hepcidin"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"NIDDK: Anemia of Inflammation or Chronic Disease","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/blood-diseases/anemia-inflammation-chronic-disease"}]},{"id":"med-heme-002","prompt":"A 24-year-old man develops fatigue, dark urine, and back pain 2 days after eating a large meal of fava beans. Hemoglobin is 8.9 g/dL (14.6 g/dL a month ago), indirect bilirubin 3.4 mg/dL, LDH 680 U/L, and haptoglobin undetectable. The direct antiglobulin test is negative. Peripheral smear shows bite cells, and a supravital stain shows Heinz bodies. Which mechanism best explains his hemolysis?","choices":["Insufficient NADPH to keep glutathione reduced","ATP depletion from a glycolytic enzyme defect","Deficient spectrin or ankyrin in the membrane","Complement lysis from absent GPI anchors","Drug- or food-induced IgG coating of red cells"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"G6PD catalyzes the first step of the pentose phosphate pathway, the red cell's only source of NADPH. Without enough NADPH, glutathione cannot be kept reduced, so oxidant stress (fava beans, infections, or drugs such as primaquine, dapsone, and rasburicase) denatures hemoglobin into Heinz bodies, which splenic macrophages remove, leaving bite cells. Pyruvate kinase deficiency causes chronic hemolysis from ATP depletion without Heinz bodies. Hereditary spherocytosis involves spectrin or ankyrin defects with spherocytes, PNH reflects complement lysis due to absent GPI-anchored CD55 and CD59, and a negative DAT excludes immune-mediated hemolysis.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["hemolytic-anemia","g6pd-deficiency","biochemistry"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase Deficiency","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470315/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-003","prompt":"A 19-year-old woman has had heavy menstrual bleeding since menarche, frequent nosebleeds, and prolonged bleeding after wisdom tooth extraction. Platelet count is 260,000/mm3, PT is normal, and aPTT is mildly prolonged at 39 seconds. Von Willebrand factor antigen and activity are both reduced to about 35%, and factor VIII activity is 40%. Before a dental procedure, she receives an intranasal drug that raises her vWF and factor VIII levels within an hour. This drug acts by which mechanism?","choices":["Inhibiting conversion of plasminogen to plasmin","Releasing vWF from endothelial Weibel-Palade bodies","Increasing hepatic transcription of the vWF gene","Stimulating thrombopoietin receptors on megakaryocytes","Replacing vWF with a plasma-derived concentrate"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is type 1 von Willebrand disease, a partial quantitative deficiency causing mucocutaneous bleeding; because vWF carries and stabilizes factor VIII, factor VIII and the aPTT may also be affected. Desmopressin (DDAVP), a vasopressin V2 receptor agonist, releases stored vWF (and factor VIII) from endothelial Weibel-Palade bodies, raising levels within about an hour. Vascular endothelium, not the liver, is the main source of vWF. Tranexamic acid inhibits plasminogen activation and is a useful adjunct for mucosal bleeding, and vWF concentrates are given IV for type 3 disease or DDAVP nonresponders.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["von-willebrand-disease","desmopressin","coagulation"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Von Willebrand Disease","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459222/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-004","prompt":"A 64-year-old man is on day 7 after coronary artery bypass surgery, during which he received unfractionated heparin; he has since received subcutaneous heparin for prophylaxis. His platelet count was 158,000/mm3 on day 1, rose to 262,000/mm3 on day 4, and is 96,000/mm3 today. He has new left calf swelling, and duplex ultrasonography shows a popliteal vein thrombosis. Which mechanism best explains these findings?","choices":["Nonimmune heparin-induced platelet aggregation","Autoantibodies to platelet glycoprotein IIb/IIIa","IgG against platelet factor 4–heparin complexes","Autoantibodies that inhibit ADAMTS13 protease","Platelet consumption by systemic coagulation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (type II) is caused by IgG antibodies against complexes of platelet factor 4 and heparin; the immune complexes activate platelets through FcγRIIa receptors, causing thrombocytopenia and a paradoxical prothrombotic state. A drop of more than 50% from the postoperative peak 5–10 days after heparin exposure, with new thrombosis, is classic; the early fall on day 1 reflects bypass hemodilution and consumption. Nonimmune heparin-associated thrombocytopenia (type I) occurs within the first 2 days, is mild, and is not thrombotic. Management is stopping all heparin and starting a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban or bivalirudin.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["heparin-induced-thrombocytopenia","thrombocytopenia","immunohematology"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482330/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-005","prompt":"A 41-year-old woman has 1 week of fatigue, gum bleeding, and large bruises. WBC count is 2,100/mm3, hemoglobin 8.4 g/dL, platelets 21,000/mm3, PT 19 seconds, aPTT 44 seconds, and fibrinogen 90 mg/dL. Bone marrow shows sheets of abnormal promyelocytes with bilobed nuclei, and some cells contain bundles of Auer rods. Which genetic abnormality is most likely?","choices":["t(8;21) RUNX1-RUNX1T1","inv(16) CBFB-MYH11","t(9;22) BCR-ABL1","t(15;17) PML-RARA","t(14;18) IGH-BCL2"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Acute promyelocytic leukemia (a subtype of AML) is defined by t(15;17), which fuses PML with the retinoic acid receptor α gene and blocks myeloid differentiation at the promyelocyte stage. Promyelocyte granules release procoagulant and fibrinolytic factors, causing DIC with low fibrinogen, which is a leading cause of early death. All-trans retinoic acid with arsenic trioxide induces differentiation and should be started as soon as APL is suspected. t(8;21) and inv(16) are core-binding-factor AMLs that can also show Auer rods but lack the DIC-prone hypergranular promyelocytes, and t(9;22) and t(14;18) define CML and follicular lymphoma.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["leukemia","acute-promyelocytic-leukemia","dic","cytogenetics"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Acute Myeloid Leukemia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507875/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-006","prompt":"A 67-year-old man has 3 months of back pain, fatigue, constipation, and increased thirst. Serum calcium is 12.4 mg/dL, creatinine 2.0 mg/dL, total protein 9.8 g/dL, albumin 3.2 g/dL, and hemoglobin 9.2 g/dL. PTH is suppressed. Skull radiographs show multiple punched-out lytic lesions. Which mechanism best explains his hypercalcemia?","choices":["Tumor secretion of PTH-related peptide","Tumor 1α-hydroxylation of vitamin D","Autonomous parathyroid hormone secretion","Increased renal tubular calcium reabsorption","Osteoclast activation via tumor-driven RANKL"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"The large protein gap, anemia, kidney injury, and punched-out lesions indicate multiple myeloma. Myeloma cells and the marrow microenvironment increase RANKL and decrease osteoprotegerin, and cytokines such as MIP-1α and IL-6 further activate osteoclasts, while osteoblast suppression (via DKK1) prevents repair. The result is purely lytic lesions (bone scans are often negative) and local osteolytic hypercalcemia. PTHrP secretion is typical of squamous cell carcinomas, and 1α-hydroxylase activity explains hypercalcemia in lymphomas and granulomatous disease. Suppressed PTH excludes primary hyperparathyroidism.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["multiple-myeloma","hypercalcemia-of-malignancy","plasma-cell-disorders"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Multiple Myeloma","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534764/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-007","prompt":"A 26-year-old man is being treated for classic Hodgkin lymphoma with doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, and dacarbazine, with filgrastim support. After his fifth cycle he has progressive exertional dyspnea and a dry cough. He is afebrile, with fine bibasilar crackles. Chest CT shows bilateral subpleural reticular opacities, and DLCO is 48% of predicted. Echocardiography shows a normal ejection fraction. Which drug is most likely responsible?","choices":["Bleomycin","Doxorubicin","Vinblastine","Dacarbazine","Filgrastim"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Bleomycin generates oxygen free radicals that cause DNA strand breaks, and the lungs and skin are vulnerable because they have little bleomycin hydrolase, the enzyme that inactivates it. Bleomycin pneumonitis progressing to fibrosis presents with dyspnea, dry cough, crackles, subpleural reticular opacities, and a falling DLCO; risk rises with cumulative dose, age, renal impairment, and high inspired oxygen. G-CSF may modestly potentiate lung injury but is not the primary cause. Doxorubicin causes dose-dependent dilated cardiomyopathy (his EF is normal), vinblastine mainly causes myelosuppression, and dacarbazine causes nausea and myelosuppression.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["chemotherapy-toxicity","bleomycin","hodgkin-lymphoma"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Bleomycin","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555895/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-008","prompt":"A 15-year-old boy with sickle cell disease (HbSS) is admitted for a vaso-occlusive pain crisis. On day 2 he develops a fever of 38.6°C, chest pain, tachypnea, and an SpO2 of 90% on room air. Chest radiograph shows a new right lower lobe infiltrate. Hemoglobin is 6.9 g/dL (baseline 8.6 g/dL). He is given supplemental oxygen, ceftriaxone, azithromycin, analgesia, and incentive spirometry, and over the next 2 hours his breathing is stable without further deterioration. Which is the most appropriate additional intervention?","choices":["Urgent red cell exchange transfusion","Simple red blood cell transfusion","Start hydroxyurea at full dose","IV fluids at twice maintenance rate","Systemic dexamethasone"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Acute chest syndrome (new infiltrate plus fever or respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease) is treated with oxygen, antibiotics covering atypical organisms, analgesia, incentive spirometry, and transfusion. When hemoglobin has fallen below baseline and the illness is moderate and stable, simple transfusion improves oxygen-carrying capacity and dilutes HbS. Exchange transfusion is reserved for severe or rapidly progressing ACS (worsening hypoxemia, multilobar disease, or a higher hemoglobin that limits simple transfusion because of hyperviscosity). Hydroxyurea prevents future episodes but does not treat an acute one, aggressive IV fluids can cause pulmonary edema, and steroids are associated with rebound pain crises.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["sickle-cell-disease","acute-chest-syndrome","transfusion"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Acute Chest Syndrome","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441872/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-009","prompt":"A 36-year-old woman has 3 days of fatigue, headache, and intermittent confusion. Temperature is 37.9°C. Hemoglobin is 7.8 g/dL, platelets 14,000/mm3, LDH 1,450 U/L, haptoglobin undetectable, and creatinine 1.3 mg/dL. PT and aPTT are normal, and the direct antiglobulin test is negative. The peripheral smear shows numerous schistocytes. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Platelet transfusion","Intravenous immune globulin","Therapeutic plasma exchange","Eculizumab infusion","Hemodialysis and supportive care"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia and severe thrombocytopenia with neurologic symptoms and normal coagulation studies indicate thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, caused by severe ADAMTS13 deficiency (usually an autoantibody). It is fatal without prompt treatment, so plasma exchange should start urgently, along with glucocorticoids and often caplacizumab, without waiting for the ADAMTS13 result. Platelet transfusion may worsen microvascular thrombosis and is avoided unless there is life-threatening bleeding. IVIG treats immune thrombocytopenia, which lacks schistocytes. Eculizumab treats complement-mediated atypical HUS, and supportive care alone is the approach for Shiga toxin–associated HUS.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["ttp","thrombotic-microangiopathy","plasma-exchange"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430721/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-010","prompt":"An 81-year-old man with atrial fibrillation falls and strikes his head. He takes dabigatran and his last dose was 3 hours ago. He is drowsy, and CT shows an acute subdural hematoma with midline shift. Creatinine is 1.2 mg/dL. Which agent should be given to reverse his anticoagulant?","choices":["Andexanet alfa","Vitamin K and fresh frozen plasma","Protamine sulfate","Idarucizumab","Desmopressin"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Dabigatran is a direct thrombin inhibitor, and its specific reversal agent is idarucizumab, a humanized monoclonal antibody fragment that binds dabigatran with very high affinity and reverses its effect within minutes; hemodialysis can also remove dabigatran when idarucizumab is unavailable. Andexanet alfa is a decoy factor Xa that reverses factor Xa inhibitors such as apixaban and rivaroxaban. Vitamin K with 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (preferred over FFP) reverses warfarin, and protamine reverses heparin.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["anticoagulation","dabigatran","anticoagulant-reversal","intracranial-hemorrhage"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Anticoagulation Safety","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519025/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-011","prompt":"A 58-year-old woman undergoing liver resection receives 2 units of fresh frozen plasma. Within 2 hours of the transfusion she develops dyspnea, an SpO2 of 84% on 4 L of oxygen, a temperature of 38.3°C, and a BP of 88/54 mm Hg. Jugular venous pressure is not elevated, and chest radiograph shows new bilateral infiltrates. BNP is 60 pg/mL, and echocardiography shows normal ventricular function. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Transfusion-associated circulatory overload","Anaphylactic transfusion reaction","Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction","Febrile nonhemolytic transfusion reaction","Transfusion-related acute lung injury"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"TRALI is acute noncardiogenic pulmonary edema within 6 hours of transfusion, often with fever and hypotension, caused largely by donor anti-HLA or anti-HNA antibodies (especially in plasma-rich products from multiparous female donors) that activate recipient neutrophils in the pulmonary vasculature. Treatment is supportive. TACO also causes hypoxemia and infiltrates, but with hypertension, elevated JVP, high BNP, and a response to diuretics. Anaphylaxis (for example, from anti-IgA antibodies in IgA deficiency) causes bronchospasm, urticaria, and angioedema, while acute hemolytic reactions cause hemoglobinuria, flank pain, and DIC.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["transfusion-reactions","trali","blood-products"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Transfusion-Related Acute Lung Injury","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507846/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-012","prompt":"A 19-year-old man has a rapidly enlarging abdominal mass, and biopsy shows Burkitt lymphoma. Before chemotherapy, labs show WBC count 14,000/mm3, LDH 2,900 U/L (upper limit 250), uric acid 10.4 mg/dL, potassium 4.8 mEq/L, phosphorus 4.9 mg/dL, and creatinine 1.5 mg/dL (baseline 0.9 mg/dL). G6PD activity is normal. Aggressive IV hydration is started. Which additional measure is most appropriate before chemotherapy?","choices":["Rasburicase","Allopurinol","Urine alkalinization with sodium bicarbonate","Febuxostat","Prophylactic hemodialysis"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Burkitt lymphoma with a high LDH, preexisting hyperuricemia, and acute kidney injury puts him at high risk for tumor lysis syndrome. High-risk patients should receive aggressive hydration plus rasburicase, a recombinant urate oxidase that converts existing uric acid to soluble allantoin and lowers levels within hours. Allopurinol and febuxostat only block new uric acid production and are appropriate for intermediate risk. Urine alkalinization is no longer recommended because it promotes calcium phosphate precipitation. Rasburicase is contraindicated in G6PD deficiency (it generates hydrogen peroxide and causes hemolysis), which is why G6PD status was checked.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["tumor-lysis-syndrome","oncologic-emergency","rasburicase","burkitt-lymphoma"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Tumor Lysis Syndrome","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK518985/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-013","prompt":"A 71-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer has 2 weeks of worsening midthoracic back pain that is worse when lying down, and since this morning he has had difficulty walking. Examination shows bilateral leg weakness (4−/5), brisk knee reflexes, and decreased sensation below the umbilicus. He has no bowel or bladder symptoms. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["MRI of the thoracic spine before any treatment","IV dexamethasone, then MRI of the entire spine","Plain radiographs of the thoracic spine","Whole-body radionuclide bone scan","Emergent radiation without imaging"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"New back pain with motor weakness and a sensory level in a patient with cancer is malignant spinal cord compression until proven otherwise. High-dose dexamethasone should be given immediately to reduce vasogenic edema, followed by urgent MRI of the entire spine, because about one-third of patients have multiple levels of epidural disease; definitive therapy is radiation and/or surgical decompression. Imaging only the thoracic spine misses other compressive lesions, and plain films and bone scans cannot show the epidural space or the cord. Radiation should be planned from MRI findings rather than given blindly.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["oncologic-emergency","spinal-cord-compression","metastatic-cancer"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Spinal Cord Compression","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557604/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-014","prompt":"A 63-year-old woman smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily from age 20 to age 49 and has not smoked since. She has no respiratory symptoms and is in good health. According to the USPSTF, which recommendation for lung cancer screening is most appropriate?","choices":["No screening, since she quit over 10 years ago","Annual chest radiograph","Annual low-dose chest CT","No screening, since she has under 30 pack-years","Sputum cytology every year"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The 2021 USPSTF recommendation is annual low-dose CT for adults aged 50 to 80 with at least a 20 pack-year history who currently smoke or quit within the past 15 years. She has 29 pack-years and quit 14 years ago, so she qualifies. The older criteria (age 55 to 80, 30 pack-years) were lowered in 2021 to include more women and Black adults who develop lung cancer with lighter exposure. Screening stops once a person has not smoked for 15 years or develops a health problem that limits life expectancy or the ability to undergo curative surgery, and chest radiography and sputum cytology do not reduce lung cancer mortality.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["cancer-screening","lung-cancer","uspstf"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"USPSTF Recommendation: Lung Cancer: Screening","url":"https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org/uspstf/recommendation/lung-cancer-screening"}]},{"id":"med-heme-015","prompt":"A 24-year-old man of Greek descent has mild anemia found on routine labs. Hemoglobin is 11.8 g/dL, MCV 63 fL, RBC count 6.1 million/mm3, RDW normal, and ferritin 120 ng/mL. The peripheral smear shows target cells. He feels well and has no history of bleeding. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":["Iron deficiency anemia","Anemia of chronic disease","Sideroblastic anemia","β-Thalassemia minor","Hemoglobin H disease"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Marked microcytosis with only mild anemia, a high RBC count (Mentzer index MCV/RBC of about 10, under 13), a normal RDW, normal ferritin, and target cells point to thalassemia trait; in a person of Mediterranean descent, β-thalassemia minor is most likely and is confirmed by an elevated HbA2 on electrophoresis. Iron deficiency causes a low RBC count, a high RDW, and low ferritin, and iron should not be given to thalassemia carriers with normal stores. Anemia of chronic disease is usually normocytic or mildly microcytic with an inflammatory cause. Hemoglobin H disease (3-gene α deletion) causes moderate hemolytic anemia with splenomegaly, not a mild incidental finding.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["anemia","thalassemia","microcytic-anemia"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Beta Thalassemia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK531481/"}]},{"id":"med-heme-016","prompt":"A 66-year-old woman has 4 months of fatigue, tingling in her feet, and unsteadiness. She has taken metformin for 12 years. Hemoglobin is 9.6 g/dL, MCV 114 fL, and the smear shows hypersegmented neutrophils. Serum vitamin B12 is 240 pg/mL (borderline low). Which laboratory finding would best confirm vitamin B12 deficiency rather than folate deficiency?","choices":["Elevated total homocysteine","Low red blood cell folate","Elevated indirect bilirubin","Low reticulocyte count","Elevated methylmalonic acid"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Vitamin B12 is a cofactor for two enzymes: methionine synthase (which also requires folate) and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (which does not). Homocysteine therefore rises in both B12 and folate deficiency, whereas methylmalonic acid rises only in B12 deficiency, making it the best confirmatory test when B12 levels are borderline. Ineffective erythropoiesis raises indirect bilirubin and LDH and lowers the reticulocyte count in both deficiencies. Long-term metformin use reduces B12 absorption, and her neurologic symptoms also fit B12, not folate, deficiency; giving folate alone could correct the anemia while the neuropathy progresses.","subject":"hematologyOncology","tags":["anemia","vitamin-b12","macrocytic-anemia","metformin"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"StatPearls: Vitamin B12 Deficiency","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441923/"}]},{"id":"med-id-001","prompt":"A 74-year-old man develops fever 5 days after transurethral resection of the prostate. Blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in pairs and short chains. The isolate is catalase-negative and nonhemolytic, hydrolyzes esculin in the presence of 40% bile, grows in 6.5% NaCl broth, and is resistant to ceftriaxone. Which organism is most likely?","choices":["Streptococcus gallolyticus","Streptococcus agalactiae","Viridans group streptococci","Staphylococcus saprophyticus","Enterococcus faecalis"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Bile-esculin hydrolysis marks group D organisms, and only enterococci also grow in 6.5% NaCl; enterococci are intrinsically resistant to cephalosporins because of low-affinity penicillin-binding proteins, and genitourinary instrumentation is a classic portal of entry. Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis) is also bile-esculin positive but fails to grow in 6.5% NaCl, is usually cephalosporin-susceptible, and its bacteremia prompts colonoscopy for colon cancer. Staphylococcus saprophyticus causes urinary infections but is catalase-positive and grows in clusters. Streptococcus agalactiae is beta-hemolytic and CAMP-positive.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["gram-positive-cocci","enterococcus","lab-identification"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Enterococcus Infections (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK567759/"}]},{"id":"med-id-002","prompt":"A 6-year-old unvaccinated boy has fever, sore throat, and marked neck swelling. A gray membrane covers his tonsils and bleeds when scraped. Ten days later he develops second-degree AV block. Culture on tellurite agar grows black colonies. The toxin responsible for his cardiac complication acts by which mechanism?","choices":["ADP-ribosylation of the Gs alpha subunit","ADP-ribosylation of the Gi alpha subunit","Removal of an adenine from 28S rRNA","ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2","Cleavage of SNARE proteins at synapses"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae carries a phage-encoded exotoxin that ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2, halting protein synthesis in myocardium, nerves, and pharyngeal epithelium; this explains the pseudomembrane, myocarditis with conduction block, and later neuropathy. ADP-ribosylation of Gs (permanently activating adenylyl cyclase) is the cholera and heat-labile E. coli toxin mechanism, while ADP-ribosylation of Gi is pertussis toxin. Shiga toxin removes an adenine from 28S rRNA of the 60S subunit, and botulinum and tetanus toxins cleave SNARE proteins.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["exotoxins","corynebacterium-diphtheriae","virulence-factors"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Diphtheria (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560911/"}]},{"id":"med-id-003","prompt":"A high-school wrestler has a recurrent furuncle. The Staphylococcus aureus isolate is resistant to oxacillin, cefazolin, and amoxicillin-clavulanate but susceptible to vancomycin and doxycycline. Adding a beta-lactamase inhibitor in the lab does not restore oxacillin activity. Which mechanism best explains this resistance?","choices":["Altered penicillin-binding protein with low beta-lactam affinity","Plasmid beta-lactamase that hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring","Peptidoglycan precursors ending in D-Ala-D-Lac","Methylation of 23S ribosomal RNA at the binding site","Loss of outer membrane porins limiting drug entry"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"MRSA carries mecA (on SCCmec), which encodes PBP2a, a transpeptidase with low affinity for nearly all beta-lactams, so oxacillin, cefazolin, and beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor combinations all fail. Penicillinase production explains penicillin resistance in most S. aureus, but oxacillin is stable to it and a beta-lactamase inhibitor would restore activity. D-Ala-D-Lac substitution is the vanA mechanism of vancomycin resistance in enterococci, and 23S rRNA methylation (erm) causes macrolide-lincosamide resistance. Porin loss is a gram-negative mechanism; S. aureus has no outer membrane.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["antibiotic-resistance","mrsa","beta-lactams"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococcus aureus (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482221/"}]},{"id":"med-id-004","prompt":"A 38-year-old man with untreated HIV infection (CD4 count 42/mm3) has 2 weeks of headache and low-grade fever. Lumbar puncture shows an opening pressure of 34 cm H2O. CSF microscopy shows yeasts surrounded by wide clear halos with narrow-based budding, and the capsule stains red with mucicarmine. Which organism is most likely?","choices":["Histoplasma capsulatum","Blastomyces dermatitidis","Candida albicans","Cryptococcus neoformans","Coccidioides immitis"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A heavily encapsulated yeast with narrow-based budding that stains with India ink and mucicarmine, causing subacute meningitis with high opening pressure at CD4 counts below 100/mm3, is Cryptococcus neoformans; CSF cryptococcal antigen confirms it. Histoplasma capsulatum appears as small intracellular yeasts within macrophages and usually causes disseminated or pulmonary disease. Blastomyces shows broad-based budding with a thick refractile wall, and Coccidioides forms spherules filled with endospores. Candida forms pseudohyphae and rarely causes meningitis in adults.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["fungi","cryptococcus","hiv-opportunistic-infections","meningitis"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cryptococcal Meningitis (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK525986/"}]},{"id":"med-id-005","prompt":"A 29-year-old woman spent 6 months volunteering in rural Honduras. Seven months after returning, she develops fevers every 48 hours. A blood smear shows enlarged red cells with Schüffner stippling and ameboid trophozoites. Chloroquine clears the parasitemia. Which additional drug is needed to eradicate the liver forms responsible for relapse?","choices":["Atovaquone-proguanil","Artemether-lumefantrine","Mefloquine","Primaquine","Doxycycline"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Plasmodium vivax (and P. ovale) form dormant liver hypnozoites that cause relapse months later; enlarged red cells with Schüffner dots indicate P. vivax. Radical cure requires an 8-aminoquinoline such as primaquine (or tafenoquine), and G6PD activity must be confirmed first because these drugs can cause severe hemolysis. Atovaquone-proguanil kills developing liver schizonts during the initial infection (causal prophylaxis) but does not eradicate dormant hypnozoites. Artemether-lumefantrine and mefloquine act only on blood-stage parasites.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["parasites","malaria","plasmodium-vivax","antimalarials"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Relapsing Malaria (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538333/"}]},{"id":"med-id-006","prompt":"A 31-year-old man was evaluated for jaundice 3 months ago and now feels better. Laboratory studies today show ALT 85 U/L, HBsAg negative, anti-HBs negative, total anti-HBc positive, and IgM anti-HBc positive. Which interpretation is most likely?","choices":["Chronic hepatitis B infection","Resolved infection with immunity","Window period of acute infection","Immunity from prior vaccination","Inactive chronic carrier state"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In the window period, HBsAg has been cleared but anti-HBs is not yet detectable, so IgM anti-HBc is the only marker of recent infection. Chronic infection (including the inactive carrier state) requires persistent HBsAg for more than 6 months, which is absent here. Resolved infection would show anti-HBs with IgG (not IgM) anti-HBc, and vaccination produces anti-HBs alone without any anti-HBc because the vaccine contains only surface antigen.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["hepatitis-b","serology","viral-hepatitis"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Hepatitis B (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK555945/"}]},{"id":"med-id-007","prompt":"A 45-year-old man started 4-drug therapy for pulmonary tuberculosis 2 months ago. He now reports blurred vision and can no longer distinguish red from green on a color plate test. Which mechanism of action belongs to the drug most likely responsible?","choices":["Inhibition of mycolic acid synthesis","Inhibition of arabinosyl transferase","Inhibition of DNA-dependent RNA polymerase","Disruption of mycobacterial membrane energetics","Binding the 30S ribosomal subunit"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Dose-related optic neuritis with loss of red-green color discrimination is the signature toxicity of ethambutol, which inhibits arabinosyl transferase and blocks arabinogalactan synthesis in the mycobacterial cell wall. Isoniazid inhibits mycolic acid synthesis (via InhA) and mainly causes hepatitis and pyridoxine-responsive peripheral neuropathy. Rifampin inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase and causes orange body fluids and CYP induction, while pyrazinamide disrupts membrane energetics and causes hepatotoxicity and hyperuricemia. Streptomycin, which binds the 30S subunit, causes ototoxicity rather than optic neuritis.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["tuberculosis","ripe-therapy","drug-toxicity","antimycobacterials"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Ethambutol (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559050/"}]},{"id":"med-id-008","prompt":"A patient with Escherichia coli bacteremia develops fever, hypotension, and disseminated intravascular coagulation. Which bacterial component binds the TLR4/MD-2 complex on host macrophages to trigger this response?","choices":["O-antigen of lipopolysaccharide","Lipoteichoic acid of the cell wall","Peptidoglycan muramyl dipeptide","Flagellin of the bacterial flagellum","Lipid A of lipopolysaccharide"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Endotoxin is the lipopolysaccharide of the gram-negative outer membrane, and its toxic moiety is lipid A, which signals through TLR4/MD-2 (with CD14) to release TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 and drive fever, shock, and DIC. The O-antigen is the variable polysaccharide used for serotyping but is not responsible for toxicity. Lipoteichoic acid is a gram-positive component sensed by TLR2, muramyl dipeptide is sensed by NOD2, and flagellin is sensed by TLR5.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["endotoxin","gram-negative-bacteria","innate-immunity","sepsis"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Biochemistry, Lipopolysaccharide (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554414/"}]},{"id":"med-id-009","prompt":"A healthy 56-year-old woman had an episode of herpes zoster 2 years ago that resolved fully. She asks whether she still needs a shingles vaccine. Which recommendation is most appropriate?","choices":["No vaccine, because her prior episode confers lasting immunity","Check varicella IgG first and vaccinate only if it is negative","Wait until age 60, then give one dose of recombinant vaccine","One dose of live attenuated zoster vaccine now","Two doses of recombinant zoster vaccine, 2–6 months apart"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"CDC recommends 2 doses of recombinant zoster vaccine (RZV) 2–6 months apart for all immunocompetent adults aged 50 years or older, and for immunocompromised adults aged 19 or older. A prior episode of shingles does not provide reliable protection against recurrence, so these patients are still vaccinated once the acute episode has resolved. Serologic screening for varicella immunity is not recommended before RZV. The live attenuated zoster vaccine is no longer available in the United States.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["vaccines","adult-immunization","herpes-zoster"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Shingles Vaccine Recommendations (CDC)","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/shingles/hcp/vaccine-considerations/index.html"}]},{"id":"med-id-010","prompt":"A 66-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 55 mL/min) has 3 days of fever, productive cough, and right lower lobe consolidation. He is alert; respiratory rate 20/min, BP 132/78 mm Hg, BUN 16 mg/dL. He has had no recent antibiotics or hospitalization and no MRSA or Pseudomonas risk factors. He will be treated as an outpatient. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Amoxicillin-clavulanate alone for 7 days","Azithromycin alone for 5 days","Doxycycline alone for 7 days","High-dose amoxicillin alone for 7 days","Amoxicillin-clavulanate plus azithromycin"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"His CURB-65 score is 1 (age 65 or older only), supporting outpatient care, but diabetes and chronic kidney disease are comorbidities. ATS/IDSA guidance for outpatients with comorbidities is a beta-lactam (amoxicillin-clavulanate or a cephalosporin) plus a macrolide or doxycycline, or a respiratory fluoroquinolone alone. Amoxicillin, doxycycline, or (where pneumococcal macrolide resistance is under 25%) a macrolide alone is reserved for healthy adults without comorbidities. Amoxicillin-clavulanate alone omits coverage of atypical organisms.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["community-acquired-pneumonia","empiric-therapy","outpatient-antibiotics"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Community-Acquired Pneumonia (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430749/"}]},{"id":"med-id-011","prompt":"A 68-year-old woman has fever, headache, neck stiffness, and confusion. Head CT is normal. CSF shows WBC 2,400/mm3 (90% neutrophils), glucose 22 mg/dL, and protein 240 mg/dL; Gram stain is pending. Dexamethasone is given. Which empiric antimicrobial regimen should accompany it?","choices":["Vancomycin, ceftriaxone, and ampicillin","Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone","Ampicillin, gentamicin, and cefotaxime","Ceftriaxone plus ampicillin","Vancomycin, cefepime, and metronidazole"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"For community-acquired bacterial meningitis in adults older than 50, empiric therapy must cover Streptococcus pneumoniae (including penicillin-resistant strains), Neisseria meningitidis, and Listeria monocytogenes: vancomycin plus a third-generation cephalosporin plus ampicillin. Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone is the regimen for immunocompetent patients aged 2–50 years but misses Listeria, which is intrinsically resistant to cephalosporins. Ceftriaxone plus ampicillin lacks coverage for cephalosporin-resistant pneumococci, and ampicillin-gentamicin-cefotaxime is a neonatal regimen. Vancomycin plus cefepime suits post-neurosurgical meningitis, and metronidazole adds no Listeria activity.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["bacterial-meningitis","empiric-therapy","listeria"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Bacterial Meningitis (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470351/"}]},{"id":"med-id-012","prompt":"A 24-year-old man requests HIV testing 3 weeks after condomless sex with a new partner. He has fever, pharyngitis, and a diffuse rash. A fourth-generation HIV-1/2 antigen/antibody immunoassay is reactive. The HIV-1/HIV-2 antibody differentiation immunoassay is negative for both. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Confirm with an HIV-1 Western blot","Order an HIV-1 nucleic acid test","Report the initial result as a false positive","Repeat the antigen/antibody test in 3 months","Obtain a CD4 count and resistance genotype"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In the CDC laboratory algorithm, a reactive antigen/antibody screen followed by a negative or indeterminate differentiation assay is resolved with an HIV-1 nucleic acid test. A positive NAT here indicates acute HIV-1 infection: p24 antigen is detectable before antibodies, which fits his acute retroviral syndrome. The Western blot was removed from the algorithm because it is less sensitive than newer assays in early infection. Calling the result a false positive or waiting 3 months would miss a highly infectious stage, and CD4 count and genotype come after diagnosis is confirmed.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["hiv","diagnostic-algorithm","acute-hiv"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"HIV Testing and Screening (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482145/"}]},{"id":"med-id-013","prompt":"A 41-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with HIV. CD4 count is 40/mm3 and Toxoplasma IgG is positive. She has no symptoms of opportunistic infection, and disseminated mycobacterial infection has been excluded. She starts antiretroviral therapy today and has no drug allergies. Which prophylactic regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole plus weekly azithromycin","Daily dapsone monotherapy","Atovaquone plus weekly azithromycin","Pyrimethamine, sulfadiazine, and leucovorin","Daily trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole alone"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Double-strength trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole daily provides prophylaxis against both Pneumocystis (CD4 count below 200/mm3) and Toxoplasma reactivation (CD4 count below 100/mm3 with positive IgG). Current DHHS opportunistic infection guidelines no longer recommend primary MAC prophylaxis with azithromycin for patients who start ART immediately, which removes the older CD4-below-50 add-on. Dapsone alone covers Pneumocystis but not Toxoplasma (it needs pyrimethamine and leucovorin) and is reserved for sulfa intolerance. Pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine-leucovorin is a treatment regimen for active toxoplasmic encephalitis.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["hiv","opportunistic-infection-prophylaxis","pneumocystis","toxoplasmosis"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Pneumocystis jirovecii Pneumonia (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482370/"}]},{"id":"med-id-014","prompt":"A 72-year-old woman on day 6 of cefepime for pneumonia develops profuse diarrhea, abdominal distension, and absent bowel sounds. BP is 84/50 mm Hg despite fluids, WBC 31,000/mm3, and lactate 4.1 mmol/L. Stool testing is positive for Clostridioides difficile toxin. CT shows colonic wall thickening and ileus without perforation. Along with surgical consultation, which antimicrobial regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["High-dose enteral vancomycin plus IV metronidazole","Oral vancomycin 125 mg four times daily alone","Oral fidaxomicin 200 mg twice daily alone","IV vancomycin plus oral metronidazole","Oral metronidazole 500 mg three times daily"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hypotension, shock, ileus, or megacolon define fulminant C. difficile infection, treated with vancomycin 500 mg four times daily by mouth or tube (plus rectal vancomycin when ileus limits delivery) together with IV metronidazole, which reaches the colon through biliary and transmural excretion. Standard-dose oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin is first-line for nonfulminant episodes but is inadequate here, and fidaxomicin has not been studied in fulminant disease. IV vancomycin does not enter the colonic lumen, and oral metronidazole is no longer recommended for anything beyond a nonsevere initial episode when other agents are unavailable.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["clostridioides-difficile","fulminant-colitis","antibiotic-associated-diarrhea"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Clostridioides difficile Infection (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431054/"}]},{"id":"med-id-015","prompt":"A 34-year-old nurse born in the Philippines has a positive interferon-gamma release assay on employment screening. She has no cough, fever, night sweats, or weight loss, and her HIV test is negative. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin rifampin daily for 4 months","Start four-drug RIPE therapy","Collect three sputum samples for AFB","Obtain a chest radiograph","Repeat testing with a tuberculin skin test"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Every positive TB infection test (IGRA or tuberculin skin test) must be followed by symptom review and a chest radiograph to exclude active disease before latent TB treatment is started. Starting rifampin for 4 months is a preferred latent regimen, but giving it before active TB is excluded risks rifampin monotherapy of active disease and acquired resistance. Four-drug therapy and sputum AFB testing are for patients with symptoms or an abnormal radiograph. A confirmatory tuberculin skin test is unnecessary; IGRA is preferred in BCG-vaccinated people.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["tuberculosis","latent-tuberculosis","screening"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Latent Tuberculosis (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK599527/"}]},{"id":"med-id-016","prompt":"A 27-year-old man has a 4-cm fluctuant, tender abscess on his thigh with 2 cm of surrounding erythema. Temperature is 38.4°C and heart rate 104/min. The abscess is incised and drained, and purulent material is sent for culture. Community MRSA rates are high. Which oral antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Cephalexin 500 mg four times daily","Dicloxacillin 500 mg four times daily","TMP-SMX double strength twice daily","Amoxicillin-clavulanate twice daily","Penicillin V 500 mg four times daily"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Incision and drainage is the primary treatment for a skin abscess, and IDSA advises adding an antibiotic active against MRSA when systemic signs (fever, tachycardia) are present; options include trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, or clindamycin. Cephalexin and dicloxacillin cover streptococci and methicillin-susceptible S. aureus and are appropriate for nonpurulent cellulitis, but not for MRSA. Amoxicillin-clavulanate and penicillin V likewise have no MRSA activity because PBP2a resists beta-lactams.","subject":"infectiousDisease","tags":["skin-soft-tissue-infection","mrsa","abscess","empiric-therapy"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococcus aureus (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482221/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-001","prompt":"A 52-year-old man receives an animal-derived antivenom after a rattlesnake bite. Nine days later he develops fever, an urticarial rash, polyarthralgia, and mild proteinuria. Serum C3 and C4 levels are low. Which mechanism underlies this reaction?","choices":["IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation","IgG binding to fixed tissue antigens","Deposition of antigen-antibody complexes","Cytokine release by sensitized T cells","Antibodies stimulating a cell receptor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Serum sickness is a type III hypersensitivity reaction: about 1–2 weeks after exposure to foreign proteins, newly formed antibodies combine with circulating antigen, and the complexes deposit in vessels, joints, and glomeruli and consume complement (low C3 and C4). Type I reactions are IgE-mediated and occur within minutes. Type II reactions involve antibody against cell-bound or tissue antigens (or receptor-stimulating antibodies, as in Graves disease), and type IV reactions are mediated by T cells over 48–72 hours without complement consumption.","subject":"immunology","tags":["hypersensitivity","type-iii-hypersensitivity","serum-sickness"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Serum Sickness (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538312/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-002","prompt":"A 14-month-old boy has had 2 episodes of pneumococcal pneumonia and recurrent otitis media since age 7 months. His tonsils are barely visible. Serum IgG, IgA, and IgM are all markedly low; flow cytometry shows almost no CD19+ cells, and T-cell numbers are normal. A maternal uncle died of infection in childhood. Which gene product is most likely defective?","choices":["CD40 ligand","Adenosine deaminase","Common gamma chain (IL-2RG)","TACI receptor","Bruton tyrosine kinase"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"X-linked agammaglobulinemia results from mutations in Bruton tyrosine kinase, which blocks pre-B-cell maturation, so mature B cells, all immunoglobulin classes, and lymphoid tissue such as tonsils are absent; infections start after maternal IgG wanes at about 6 months. CD40 ligand deficiency (hyper-IgM syndrome) leaves B cells present with normal or high IgM. Adenosine deaminase and common gamma chain defects cause SCID with absent or nonfunctional T cells. TACI variants are associated with common variable immunodeficiency, which has normal B-cell numbers and later onset.","subject":"immunology","tags":["primary-immunodeficiency","x-linked-agammaglobulinemia","b-cells"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"X-Linked Agammaglobulinemia (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549865/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-003","prompt":"A 4-year-old boy has had a Serratia liver abscess, Staphylococcus aureus lymphadenitis, and now Aspergillus pneumonia. His neutrophil count is normal, and his neutrophils migrate to sites of infection and ingest bacteria normally. On flow cytometry with dihydrorhodamine 123, his neutrophils show no increase in fluorescence after phorbol ester stimulation; his healthy mother's neutrophils show two distinct populations, one that becomes fluorescent and one that does not. Which protein is most likely deficient?","choices":["Myeloperoxidase","Beta-2 integrin (CD18)","Lysosomal trafficking regulator","Interferon-gamma receptor","NADPH oxidase"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Recurrent infections with catalase-positive organisms such as S. aureus, Serratia, Burkholderia, and Aspergillus indicate chronic granulomatous disease, a defect of the phagocyte NADPH oxidase (most often X-linked CYBB, encoding gp91phox). Dihydrorhodamine is oxidized to fluorescent rhodamine by the respiratory burst, so absent fluorescence after stimulation means no superoxide is being generated, and the mother's two neutrophil populations reflect random X inactivation in a carrier. Myeloperoxidase acts downstream, converting hydrogen peroxide to hypochlorous acid; its deficiency is autosomal recessive and usually mild, with occasional Candida infections, and it would not produce a mosaic pattern in the mother. CD18 deficiency (leukocyte adhesion deficiency) impairs neutrophil migration, with delayed umbilical cord separation and absent pus. LYST mutations cause Chédiak-Higashi syndrome with giant granules and partial albinism, and IFN-gamma receptor defects cause mycobacterial infections.","subject":"immunology","tags":["phagocyte-defects","chronic-granulomatous-disease","primary-immunodeficiency"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Chronic Granulomatous Disease (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493171/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-004","prompt":"A newborn has a seizure on day 2 of life. Serum calcium is 6.4 mg/dL with a low PTH level. Echocardiography shows truncus arteriosus, and the chest radiograph lacks a thymic shadow. Abnormal development of which embryologic structures explains these findings?","choices":["Third and fourth pharyngeal pouches","Third and fourth pharyngeal clefts","First and second pharyngeal arches","Fourth and sixth pharyngeal arches","First pharyngeal pouch and cleft"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge) syndrome disrupts the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches, which form the thymus (third), inferior parathyroids (third), and superior parathyroids (fourth), producing T-cell deficiency and hypocalcemia; abnormal neural crest migration causes the conotruncal defects. Pharyngeal clefts are ectodermal and form only the external auditory meatus (first cleft). The fourth and sixth arches give rise to laryngeal cartilages and the aortic arch and pulmonary artery derivatives, not the thymus or parathyroids. The first pouch forms the middle ear cavity and eustachian tube.","subject":"immunology","tags":["digeorge-syndrome","22q11-deletion","embryology","t-cell-deficiency"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"DiGeorge Syndrome (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549798/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-005","prompt":"A 19-year-old college student has his second episode of Neisseria meningitidis bacteremia in 3 years. He has had no other unusual infections. Total hemolytic complement activity (CH50) is undetectable, and C3 and C4 levels are normal. Deficiency of which component is most likely?","choices":["C1 esterase inhibitor","Complement component C2","Properdin (alternative pathway)","Decay-accelerating factor (CD55)","Terminal components C5–C9"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Recurrent Neisseria infections are the hallmark of terminal complement (C5–C9) deficiency because the membrane attack complex is essential for lysing these organisms; any terminal deficiency abolishes CH50. Properdin deficiency also predisposes to Neisseria, but it affects only the alternative pathway, so CH50 is normal and AH50 is low. C2 deficiency, the most common classical pathway defect, is associated mainly with SLE-like disease and encapsulated bacterial infections. C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency causes hereditary angioedema, and CD55 deficiency causes complement-mediated hemolysis as in PNH.","subject":"immunology","tags":["complement-deficiency","neisseria","membrane-attack-complex"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Complement Deficiency (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557581/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-006","prompt":"Fibroblasts infected with a herpesvirus degrade viral proteins into short peptides in the cytosol normally. However, a viral protein binds a heterodimeric, ATP-dependent transporter in the endoplasmic reticulum membrane and stops these peptides from entering the ER lumen. This immune-evasion strategy most directly impairs which process?","choices":["Presentation of endocytosed peptides on MHC II to CD4+ T cells","Presentation of cytosolic peptides on MHC I to CD8+ T cells","Removal of the invariant chain from MHC II in endosomes","Killing of cells lacking surface MHC I by NK cells","Proteasomal cleavage of viral proteins into peptides"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The ATP-dependent heterodimer in the ER membrane is the transporter associated with antigen processing (TAP1/TAP2). Viral proteins in the cytosol are degraded by the proteasome, and TAP pumps the resulting peptides into the endoplasmic reticulum, where they load onto MHC class I for recognition by CD8+ cytotoxic T cells; blocking TAP prevents this presentation, and empty MHC I molecules are retained in the ER. Proteasomal cleavage occurs upstream of TAP and is intact here. MHC II loading takes place in endosomes after invariant chain degradation and does not use TAP. Reduced surface MHC I actually makes cells more susceptible to NK-cell killing ('missing self'), which is why many viruses also express decoy MHC-like molecules.","subject":"immunology","tags":["mhc-class-i","antigen-presentation","cd8-t-cells","immune-evasion"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Physiology, MHC Class I (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556022/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-007","prompt":"Ten weeks after a deceased-donor kidney transplant, a 47-year-old woman has a rising serum creatinine. Her tacrolimus trough level is in the target range. Biopsy shows interstitial lymphocytes invading tubular epithelium (tubulitis) and intimal arteritis; C4d staining is negative and donor-specific antibodies are absent. Which mechanism is primarily responsible?","choices":["Recipient T cells responding to donor MHC","Preformed recipient antibodies to donor HLA","Donor T cells attacking recipient tissues","Calcineurin inhibitor arteriolar toxicity","Fibrosis from chronic alloantibody injury"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Acute cellular rejection, typically weeks to months after transplantation, is driven by recipient CD4+ and CD8+ T cells sensitized to donor MHC antigens, producing tubulitis and, in more severe cases, intimal arteritis. Preformed antibodies cause hyperacute rejection within minutes, and antibody-mediated rejection shows C4d deposition with donor-specific antibodies. Donor T cells attacking the host is graft-versus-host disease, which is seen mainly after bone marrow transplants. Calcineurin inhibitor toxicity shows arteriolar hyalinosis and is unlikely with a therapeutic trough, and chronic rejection develops over months to years with fibrosis.","subject":"immunology","tags":["transplant-rejection","acute-cellular-rejection","t-cells"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Acute Renal Transplantation Rejection (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553074/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-008","prompt":"Minutes after eating shrimp, a 30-year-old woman weighing 68 kg has diffuse urticaria, wheezing, throat tightness, and a blood pressure of 82/48 mm Hg. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Epinephrine 0.5 mg IM into the anterolateral thigh","Epinephrine 0.5 mg subcutaneously into the deltoid","Epinephrine 1 mg IV push over one minute","Diphenhydramine 50 mg IV plus famotidine IV","Methylprednisolone 125 mg IV with IV fluids"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Anaphylaxis is treated first with intramuscular epinephrine (1 mg/mL concentration, 0.01 mg/kg up to 0.5 mg in adults) into the anterolateral thigh, repeated every 5–15 minutes as needed, with IV fluids for hypotension. Intramuscular thigh injection produces faster and higher peak levels than subcutaneous injection. A 1-mg IV bolus is the cardiac-arrest dose and can precipitate arrhythmias or myocardial ischemia in a patient with a pulse; IV epinephrine for refractory shock is given as a titrated infusion. Antihistamines and glucocorticoids are adjuncts that do not reverse airway obstruction or shock.","subject":"immunology","tags":["anaphylaxis","epinephrine","emergency-management"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Anaphylaxis (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482124/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-009","prompt":"A 22-year-old woman has had recurrent episodes of nonpitting swelling of the face and hands and attacks of abdominal pain, each lasting 2–3 days, without urticaria. Her father had similar episodes. She now has lip and tongue swelling without stridor. C4 is low, C1 inhibitor level is low, and C1q is normal. She takes no medications. Which therapy is most likely to be effective for this attack?","choices":["Intramuscular epinephrine","Subcutaneous icatibant","IV diphenhydramine and famotidine","Long-term oral danazol","IV methylprednisolone"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Hereditary angioedema type I (autosomal dominant C1 inhibitor deficiency with low C4 and normal C1q) is mediated by bradykinin, not histamine. Acute attacks respond to icatibant (a bradykinin B2 receptor antagonist), C1 inhibitor concentrate, or ecallantide (a kallikrein inhibitor). Epinephrine, antihistamines, and glucocorticoids—the mainstays of histaminergic angioedema—are ineffective in this condition. Danazol is an older long-term prophylactic agent and does not treat an acute attack. A normal C1q distinguishes hereditary disease from acquired C1 inhibitor deficiency.","subject":"immunology","tags":["hereditary-angioedema","c1-inhibitor","bradykinin"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Hereditary Angioedema (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482266/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-010","prompt":"A 29-year-old man has had 4 episodes of pneumonia and chronic sinusitis over 3 years, plus Giardia diarrhea. IgG is 210 mg/dL with low IgA and IgM, he has no protective titers after tetanus and pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccination, and circulating B-cell numbers are normal. Which long-term therapy is most appropriate?","choices":["Prophylactic azithromycin alone","Allogeneic stem cell transplantation","Repeat pneumococcal conjugate vaccination","Daily trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole alone","Lifelong immune globulin replacement"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Common variable immunodeficiency presents in adolescence or adulthood with low IgG plus low IgA and/or IgM, poor vaccine responses, and normal B-cell numbers that fail to differentiate into plasma cells. Standard therapy is lifelong IV or subcutaneous immune globulin replacement, which reduces pneumonia and bronchiectasis. Prophylactic antibiotics may be added for persistent infections but do not replace antibody. Vaccine boosters are ineffective because the defect is in antibody production, and stem cell transplantation is reserved for severe combined defects rather than CVID.","subject":"immunology","tags":["common-variable-immunodeficiency","immunoglobulin-replacement","primary-immunodeficiency"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Common Variable Immunodeficiency (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK549787/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-011","prompt":"A 58-year-old man with a kidney transplant has taken azathioprine, tacrolimus, and prednisone for 6 years with stable blood counts. Three weeks after starting allopurinol for gout, he has WBC 1,100/mm3, hemoglobin 7.9 g/dL, and platelets 38,000/mm3. Which mechanism best explains this?","choices":["Unmasked inherited TPMT deficiency","Tacrolimus accumulation from CYP3A4 inhibition","Allopurinol-induced DRESS syndrome","Parvovirus B19 infection of marrow precursors","Xanthine oxidase inhibition raising 6-MP levels"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Azathioprine is converted to 6-mercaptopurine, which is inactivated by xanthine oxidase and thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT); allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase, shunting 6-MP toward cytotoxic thioguanine nucleotides and causing severe myelosuppression unless the azathioprine dose is cut by about 75%. Inherited TPMT deficiency would have caused cytopenias within weeks of starting azathioprine, not after 6 stable years. Allopurinol does not meaningfully inhibit CYP3A4, and tacrolimus toxicity presents with nephrotoxicity, tremor, and hyperkalemia rather than pancytopenia. Parvovirus B19 typically causes isolated red cell aplasia, and DRESS features rash, fever, and eosinophilia.","subject":"immunology","tags":["immunosuppressants","azathioprine","drug-interactions","transplant"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Azathioprine (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542190/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-012","prompt":"A 26-year-old woman with moderate-to-severe Crohn disease is about to begin infliximab. Along with hepatitis B serologies, which test is required before the first infusion?","choices":["JC polyomavirus antibody titer","Interferon-gamma release assay","Thiopurine methyltransferase activity","HLA-B*57:01 allele genotyping","Anti-dsDNA antibody titer"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"TNF-alpha is essential for maintaining granulomas, so infliximab and other TNF inhibitors can reactivate latent tuberculosis; screening with an IGRA (or tuberculin skin test), and treating latent infection if positive, is required before therapy. JC virus antibody testing is used before natalizumab, an anti-alpha-4 integrin antibody associated with progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy. TPMT activity is checked before azathioprine or 6-mercaptopurine, and HLA-B*57:01 before abacavir. Anti-dsDNA is not a prerequisite, although TNF inhibitors can induce lupus-like autoantibodies.","subject":"immunology","tags":["biologics","tnf-inhibitors","latent-tuberculosis","screening"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Infliximab (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500021/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-013","prompt":"A 67-year-old man who has taken hydralazine for resistant hypertension for 1 year develops fever, arthralgias, and a left pleural effusion. Kidney function and urinalysis are normal, and he has no rash or neurologic findings. ANA is positive. Which additional antibody is most likely present?","choices":["Anti-histone","Anti-double-stranded DNA","Anti-Smith","Anti-centromere","Anti-Ro/SSA"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Drug-induced lupus from hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid, or minocycline typically produces arthralgias, fever, and serositis with a positive ANA and anti-histone antibodies, while renal and CNS involvement are rare; it resolves after the drug is stopped. Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith antibodies are specific for idiopathic SLE and are usually absent in classic drug-induced disease (TNF inhibitor–induced lupus is an exception for anti-dsDNA). Anti-centromere antibodies suggest limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis, and anti-Ro/SSA suggests Sjögren syndrome, subacute cutaneous lupus, or neonatal lupus risk.","subject":"immunology","tags":["autoantibodies","drug-induced-lupus","hydralazine"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441889/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-014","prompt":"A 35-year-old woman with refractory immune thrombocytopenia is scheduled for elective splenectomy in 6 weeks. She has never received pneumococcal, meningococcal, or Haemophilus influenzae type b vaccines. When should these vaccines ideally be given?","choices":["At least 2 weeks before surgery","Within 24 hours after surgery","At least 2 weeks after surgery","At induction of anesthesia","Six months after surgery"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"For elective splenectomy, vaccines against encapsulated organisms (pneumococcal, MenACWY, MenB, and Hib) should be completed at least 14 days before surgery, when an intact spleen can mount the best antibody response. If vaccination is not possible beforehand, it is given 14 days or more after surgery rather than immediately, because early postoperative responses are weaker. Delaying 6 months leaves her unprotected during the highest-risk period for overwhelming postsplenectomy infection.","subject":"immunology","tags":["asplenia","vaccines","encapsulated-organisms","splenectomy"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Asplenia (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538171/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-015","prompt":"A naive CD4+ T cell recognizes its peptide–MHC II complex on a dendritic cell, but the dendritic cell's B7 molecules (CD80/CD86) are blocked by abatacept. Without this second signal, the T cell becomes anergic. Which T-cell receptor normally receives this costimulatory signal?","choices":["CTLA-4","CD40 ligand","PD-1","CD28","LFA-1"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Naive T-cell activation requires signal 1 (TCR binding peptide–MHC) and signal 2 (CD28 on the T cell binding B7-1/B7-2 on the antigen-presenting cell); signal 1 alone induces anergy. Abatacept, a CTLA-4–Ig fusion protein, binds B7 and blocks CD28 costimulation. CTLA-4 also binds B7, with higher affinity, but delivers an inhibitory signal, as does PD-1 binding PD-L1. CD40 ligand on helper T cells signals to B cells and macrophages, and LFA-1 mediates adhesion to ICAM-1.","subject":"immunology","tags":["t-cell-activation","costimulation","abatacept"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Histology, T-Cell Lymphocyte (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535433/"}]},{"id":"med-immuno-016","prompt":"The Haemophilus influenzae type b vaccine links the capsular polysaccharide to a carrier protein. Why does this conjugation make the vaccine effective in infants younger than 2 years?","choices":["It allows polysaccharide presentation on MHC I to CD8 T cells","It crosslinks B-cell receptors more strongly than free sugar","It activates complement to enhance capsular opsonization","It recruits helper T cells for class switching and memory","It directly stimulates marginal zone B cells in the spleen"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Pure polysaccharides are T-independent antigens that infants respond to poorly, and they produce little class switching or memory. In a conjugate vaccine, B cells specific for the polysaccharide internalize the conjugate and present carrier-protein peptides on MHC II to CD4+ helper T cells, which then drive class switching, affinity maturation, and memory against the polysaccharide. Stronger B-cell receptor crosslinking is how polysaccharides trigger T-independent responses, and marginal zone B cells, which mediate those responses, are immature in infants. Carbohydrates are not presented on MHC I.","subject":"immunology","tags":["vaccine-immunology","conjugate-vaccines","t-dependent-response"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Haemophilus influenzae Type b Vaccine (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553112/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-001","prompt":"A drug has a volume of distribution of 2 L/kg and an oral bioavailability of 70%. A 70-kg patient needs to reach a target plasma concentration of 4 mg/L promptly. What oral loading dose is required?","choices":["392 mg","560 mg","800 mg","1,120 mg","1,600 mg"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Loading dose = (target concentration × Vd) / F. Vd = 2 L/kg × 70 kg = 140 L, so the dose that must reach the circulation is 4 mg/L × 140 L = 560 mg, and the oral dose is 560 mg / 0.7 = 800 mg. Choosing 560 mg omits the correction for bioavailability (it would be correct only for an IV dose), and 392 mg results from multiplying by F instead of dividing. Clearance and half-life do not enter the loading-dose calculation; they determine the maintenance dose.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["pharmacokinetics","loading-dose","bioavailability","calculation"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Loading Dose (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557418/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-002","prompt":"A drug given by continuous IV infusion without a loading dose has a volume of distribution of 40 L and a clearance of 4 L/h and follows first-order kinetics. Approximately how long after the infusion starts will the plasma concentration reach about 94% of its steady-state value?","choices":["About 7 hours","About 10 hours","About 28 hours","About 40 hours","About 70 hours"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Half-life = 0.693 × Vd / CL = 0.693 × 40 L / 4 L/h ≈ 6.9 hours. With first-order kinetics, an infusion reaches 50%, 75%, 87.5%, and 93.75% of steady state after 1, 2, 3, and 4 half-lives, so about 94% takes 4 × 6.9 ≈ 28 hours. The 7-hour answer is a single half-life (50%), and 40 hours comes from omitting the 0.693 factor (Vd/CL = 10 hours × 4). The time to steady state depends only on half-life, not on the infusion rate.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["pharmacokinetics","half-life","steady-state","calculation"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Elimination Half-Life of Drugs (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554498/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-003","prompt":"A 40-year-old man taking phenytoin 300 mg/day has a trough level of 12 mcg/mL. After his dose is increased to 400 mg/day, the level rises to 32 mcg/mL and he develops nystagmus and ataxia. At these concentrations, hepatic metabolism of the drug is saturated. Which statement about the drug at these concentrations is correct?","choices":["Its half-life stays constant regardless of dose","Its elimination rate is proportional to concentration","Its half-life lengthens as concentration rises","A constant fraction is eliminated each hour","Steady state is reached in 4–5 half-lives"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When metabolizing enzymes are saturated (Michaelis-Menten behavior, as with phenytoin, ethanol, and high-dose aspirin), the drug is eliminated by zero-order kinetics: a constant amount per unit time, so the apparent half-life lengthens as concentration rises and small dose increases cause disproportionate jumps in level. A constant half-life, elimination proportional to concentration, and removal of a constant fraction per hour all describe first-order kinetics. The 4–5 half-life rule for reaching steady state also applies only to first-order drugs.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["pharmacokinetics","zero-order-kinetics","phenytoin"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Physiology, Zero- and First-Order Kinetics (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499866/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-004","prompt":"In an isolated tissue preparation, an agonist produces a maximal response of 100% with an EC50 of 10 nM. Drug X alone produces no response. In the presence of drug X, the agonist still reaches a 100% maximal response, but its EC50 rises to 80 nM; higher concentrations of X shift the curve farther to the right. How is drug X best classified?","choices":["Noncompetitive antagonist","Partial agonist","Inverse agonist","Competitive antagonist","Irreversible antagonist"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A competitive (surmountable) antagonist binds reversibly to the agonist's site, shifting the dose-response curve to the right (lower potency) without reducing the maximal response, because enough agonist can outcompete it. Noncompetitive and irreversible antagonists reduce the maximal response (lower efficacy), and adding agonist cannot overcome them. A partial agonist and an inverse agonist each produce a response of their own (a submaximal response or a reduction in constitutive activity), whereas drug X has no effect when given alone.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["pharmacodynamics","receptor-antagonists","dose-response-curves"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Pharmacodynamics (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507791/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-005","prompt":"In an anesthetized animal, an IV bolus of drug Y raises mean arterial pressure. After pretreatment with phentolamine, the same dose of drug Y lowers mean arterial pressure and increases heart rate. Which drug is Y most likely to be?","choices":["Norepinephrine","Phenylephrine","Isoproterenol","Vasopressin","Epinephrine"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Epinephrine activates alpha-1, beta-1, and beta-2 receptors; normally alpha-1 vasoconstriction dominates and pressure rises, but after an alpha blocker the unopposed beta-2 vasodilation lowers pressure ('epinephrine reversal') while beta-1 stimulation raises heart rate. Norepinephrine has little beta-2 activity, so after alpha blockade its pressor effect is lost but pressure does not fall. Phenylephrine's pressor effect is simply abolished, and vasopressin acts on V1 receptors, so phentolamine does not block it. Isoproterenol lowers mean pressure even without pretreatment.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["autonomic-pharmacology","sympathomimetics","alpha-blockers"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Adrenergic Drugs (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534230/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-006","prompt":"A 24-year-old woman taking a combined oral contraceptive becomes pregnant despite reporting perfect adherence. Two months earlier, another clinic had started her on a new medication. Which medication is most likely to have caused the contraceptive failure?","choices":["Isoniazid","Clarithromycin","Rifampin","Fluconazole","Ciprofloxacin"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Rifampin is a potent inducer of CYP3A4 (and UGT enzymes), accelerating metabolism of ethinyl estradiol and progestins enough to cause contraceptive failure; a backup or nonhormonal method is required during and for 28 days after use. Clarithromycin and fluconazole inhibit CYP3A4, and ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2, so they raise rather than lower levels of affected drugs. Isoniazid is also an enzyme inhibitor (notably raising phenytoin levels). Other inducers to remember include carbamazepine, phenytoin, St. John's wort, and chronic alcohol use.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["cyp450","drug-interactions","enzyme-induction","contraception"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Rifampin (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557488/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-007","prompt":"A 33-year-old man with newly diagnosed HIV infection starts a single-tablet regimen of dolutegravir, abacavir, and lamivudine. Ten days later he has fever, a diffuse maculopapular rash, nausea, abdominal pain, and dyspnea, and the symptoms intensify within hours of each dose. He has no mucosal lesions. Which pretreatment test would most likely have identified his risk for this reaction?","choices":["HLA-B*15:02","HLA-B*58:01","TPMT activity","HLA-B*57:01","CYP2C19 genotype"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Fever, rash, and GI and respiratory symptoms that begin within the first 6 weeks of therapy and worsen with each dose indicate abacavir hypersensitivity, which can be fatal on rechallenge; abacavir is the component of this regimen classically responsible. The reaction is strongly associated with HLA-B*57:01, so screening is required before abacavir is started, and the drug must not be given to carriers. HLA-B*15:02 predicts carbamazepine-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome, particularly in people of Southeast Asian ancestry, and HLA-B*58:01 predicts severe allopurinol reactions. TPMT activity guides thiopurine dosing, and CYP2C19 loss-of-function alleles reduce the activation of clopidogrel.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["pharmacogenomics","drug-hypersensitivity","antiretrovirals"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Abacavir (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537117/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-008","prompt":"A 19-year-old woman is found confused by her roommate next to an empty bottle of extra-strength acetaminophen; the time of ingestion is unknown. Serum acetaminophen is 32 mcg/mL, AST 640 U/L, ALT 710 U/L, and INR 1.6. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start intravenous acetylcysteine immediately","Plot the level on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram","Repeat the acetaminophen level in 4 hours","Give activated charcoal and observe","Start fomepizole and arrange hemodialysis"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Rumack-Matthew nomogram applies only to a single acute ingestion with a known time between 4 and 24 hours. With an unknown time of ingestion, any detectable acetaminophen level or elevated aminotransferases is an indication for N-acetylcysteine, which replenishes glutathione and benefits patients even after hepatic injury has begun. Waiting for a repeat level delays an antidote whose benefit depends on time. Activated charcoal alone is inadequate, and fomepizole with hemodialysis is the core therapy for toxic alcohols (considered only as an adjunct in massive acetaminophen ingestions).","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["toxicology","acetaminophen","n-acetylcysteine"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Acetaminophen Toxicity (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441917/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-009","prompt":"A 46-year-old farmworker has vomiting, diarrhea, profuse sweating, bronchorrhea, miosis, bradycardia, and muscle fasciculations with weakness after spraying crops. After decontamination and airway support, which drug combination is most appropriate?","choices":["Physostigmine and atropine","Atropine and pralidoxime","Pralidoxime and naloxone","Neostigmine and glycopyrrolate","Atropine and flumazenil"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Organophosphates irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase, causing muscarinic (secretions, bronchorrhea, bradycardia, miosis) and nicotinic (fasciculations, paralysis) toxicity. Atropine, titrated to drying of bronchial secretions, blocks muscarinic effects, and pralidoxime regenerates acetylcholinesterase before 'aging' occurs, reversing nicotinic weakness that atropine cannot treat. Physostigmine and neostigmine are cholinesterase inhibitors that would worsen toxicity. Naloxone and flumazenil reverse opioids and benzodiazepines; pinpoint pupils here reflect cholinergic excess, not opioids.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["toxicology","organophosphates","cholinergic-crisis","antidotes"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Organophosphate Toxicity (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470430/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-010","prompt":"A 35-year-old woman ingested an unknown quantity of amitriptyline. She is drowsy, with dry flushed skin, dilated pupils, heart rate 128/min, and BP 92/58 mm Hg. ECG shows a QRS duration of 136 ms and a prominent terminal R wave in lead aVR. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["IV physostigmine","IV amiodarone","IV flumazenil","IV procainamide","IV sodium bicarbonate"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Tricyclic antidepressants block fast cardiac sodium channels, widening the QRS complex and causing ventricular arrhythmias and hypotension; a QRS over 100 ms or a terminal R wave in aVR is an indication for IV sodium bicarbonate. Bicarbonate supplies sodium to overcome the channel blockade and alkalemia that reduces drug binding. Physostigmine is contraindicated because it can precipitate bradycardia and asystole in TCA poisoning. Procainamide and amiodarone prolong conduction or repolarization and are avoided, and flumazenil can provoke seizures in mixed ingestions.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["toxicology","tricyclic-antidepressants","sodium-channel-blockade"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Tricyclic Antidepressant Toxicity (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430931/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-011","prompt":"An 81-year-old woman taking digoxin for atrial fibrillation has nausea, confusion, and yellow-tinged vision 2 weeks after starting amiodarone. Heart rate is 38/min with a junctional rhythm; potassium is 6.1 mEq/L, and the digoxin level is 4.2 ng/mL. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Urgent hemodialysis","Digoxin immune Fab","IV calcium gluconate","IV lidocaine bolus","Transcutaneous pacing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Life-threatening digoxin toxicity—symptomatic bradyarrhythmia, ventricular arrhythmia, end-organ dysfunction, or significant hyperkalemia—is treated with digoxin immune Fab, which binds digoxin and rapidly reverses toxicity and hyperkalemia. Amiodarone raised her level by inhibiting P-glycoprotein–mediated elimination. Hemodialysis does not remove digoxin because of its large volume of distribution. Hyperkalemia reflects Na+/K+-ATPase inhibition, and Fab corrects it more effectively than calcium (once thought dangerous in digoxin toxicity). Pacing and lidocaine are temporizing options when Fab is unavailable.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["toxicology","digoxin","drug-interactions","antidotes"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cardiac Glycoside and Digoxin Toxicity (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459165/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-012","prompt":"A 45-year-old man is found confused. Laboratory studies: Na 140, Cl 102, HCO3 8 mEq/L; glucose 90 mg/dL; BUN 14 mg/dL; creatinine 2.6 mg/dL; calcium 7.2 mg/dL; measured serum osmolality 330 mOsm/kg; serum ethanol undetectable. Visual acuity and funduscopic examination are normal. Which ingestion is most likely?","choices":["Methanol","Ethylene glycol","Isopropyl alcohol","Propylene glycol","Salicylate"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The anion gap is 140 − (102 + 8) = 30, and the calculated osmolality is 2(140) + 90/18 + 14/2.8 = 290, giving an osmolal gap of 40: an anion-gap acidosis with a large osmolal gap points to a toxic alcohol. Acute kidney injury and hypocalcemia (calcium chelated into oxalate crystals) indicate ethylene glycol, which is treated with fomepizole and often hemodialysis. Methanol produces the same lab pattern but characteristically injures the optic nerve and retina. Isopropyl alcohol raises the osmolal gap without an anion-gap acidosis (ketosis without acidosis), and salicylate toxicity causes an anion-gap acidosis (usually with respiratory alkalosis) without an osmolal gap this large. Propylene glycol toxicity, usually from prolonged IV lorazepam infusions, causes lactic acidosis.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["toxicology","toxic-alcohols","anion-gap","osmolal-gap"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Ethylene Glycol Toxicity (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537009/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-013","prompt":"A 34-year-old woman ingested a large amount of aspirin 6 hours ago. She has tinnitus, vomiting, and tachypnea but is alert and oriented. ABG: pH 7.46, PaCO2 24 mm Hg, HCO3 16 mEq/L. Salicylate level is 58 mg/dL, creatinine 0.8 mg/dL, and the chest radiograph is clear. After activated charcoal and IV fluids, which intervention is most appropriate next?","choices":["Emergent hemodialysis","Intubation with standard ventilation","Oral acetazolamide","IV acetylcysteine","IV sodium bicarbonate infusion"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"She has a mixed primary respiratory alkalosis and anion-gap metabolic acidosis typical of salicylate toxicity. IV sodium bicarbonate alkalinizes the serum (limiting CNS entry of the drug) and the urine (trapping ionized salicylate for excretion), with potassium repletion as needed. Hemodialysis is reserved for altered mental status, pulmonary edema, kidney injury, refractory acidosis, or very high levels (above about 90–100 mg/dL), none of which is present. Intubation is hazardous because loss of her compensatory hyperventilation worsens acidemia, and acetazolamide alkalinizes urine but worsens systemic acidosis.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["toxicology","salicylates","acid-base","urinary-alkalinization"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Salicylates Toxicity (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499879/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-014","prompt":"A 76-year-old man taking warfarin for atrial fibrillation presents with acute left hemiparesis; CT shows an intracerebral hemorrhage. INR is 3.4. In addition to holding warfarin, which reversal strategy is most appropriate?","choices":["Fresh frozen plasma without vitamin K","Oral vitamin K 2.5 mg alone","Idarucizumab plus IV vitamin K","4-factor PCC plus IV vitamin K","Andexanet alfa plus IV vitamin K"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Life-threatening bleeding on warfarin requires rapid replacement of factors II, VII, IX, and X with 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate, plus 5–10 mg of IV vitamin K so that the liver resumes factor synthesis once the PCC wears off. Fresh frozen plasma corrects the INR more slowly and with large volumes, and its effect fades without vitamin K. Oral vitamin K alone suits a supratherapeutic INR without bleeding. Idarucizumab reverses dabigatran, and andexanet alfa reverses the factor Xa inhibitors apixaban and rivaroxaban.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["anticoagulation","warfarin-reversal","intracranial-hemorrhage"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Prothrombin Complex Concentrate (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539716/"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-015","prompt":"A new oral anticoagulant is tested in 3,200 patients with atrial fibrillation, who are randomized to the new drug or to warfarin with stroke as the primary outcome, to support an application for FDA approval. Which clinical trial phase does this study represent?","choices":["Phase I","Phase II","Phase IV","Preclinical","Phase III"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Phase III trials are large (hundreds to thousands of patients) randomized studies comparing a new drug with standard therapy or placebo to confirm efficacy and monitor adverse effects, and they form the main basis for FDA approval. Phase I trials test safety, pharmacokinetics, and dosing in small numbers of healthy volunteers or patients. Phase II trials study efficacy and optimal dosing in a few hundred patients with the disease, and Phase IV is postmarketing surveillance after approval.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["drug-development","clinical-trial-phases"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"The Drug Development Process, Step 3: Clinical Research (FDA)","url":"https://www.fda.gov/patients/drug-development-process/step-3-clinical-research"}]},{"id":"med-pharm-016","prompt":"In clinical studies, drug A has a median effective dose (ED50) of 4 mg and a median toxic dose (TD50) of 400 mg. Drug B has an ED50 of 0.5 mg and a TD50 of 5 mg. Which statement is correct?","choices":["Drug B has the wider therapeutic index","Drug A is more potent than drug B","Drug A has the wider therapeutic index","Both drugs have the same therapeutic index","Drug B is safer since it needs a lower dose"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Therapeutic index = TD50 / ED50: drug A is 400/4 = 100 and drug B is 5/0.5 = 10, so drug A has the wider margin of safety. Drug B is more potent (lower ED50), but potency reflects the dose needed for an effect, not safety. A narrow therapeutic index, as with drug B (and with drugs such as warfarin, digoxin, and lithium), calls for closer monitoring.","subject":"pharmacology","tags":["pharmacodynamics","therapeutic-index","potency"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Pharmacodynamics (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507791/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-001","prompt":"A 5-month-old girl has a protuberant abdomen, a doll-like face, and seizures when feedings are delayed. After a 3-hour fast, glucose is 32 mg/dL and lactate 6.8 mmol/L, and uric acid and triglycerides are elevated. Glucagon does not raise her blood glucose. The liver is markedly enlarged, and muscle tone and CK are normal. Which enzyme is most likely deficient?","choices":["Glycogen debranching enzyme","Glucose-6-phosphatase","Hepatic glycogen phosphorylase","Lysosomal acid alpha-glucosidase","Muscle glycogen phosphorylase"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Von Gierke disease (GSD type Ia) results from glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency, which blocks the final step of both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, causing severe fasting hypoglycemia unresponsive to glucagon. Accumulated glucose-6-phosphate is shunted into lactate, uric acid, and triglycerides. Debranching enzyme deficiency (Cori disease) causes milder hypoglycemia with normal lactate and often myopathy with elevated CK, and hepatic phosphorylase deficiency (Hers disease) is milder still. Acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency (Pompe disease) causes cardiomegaly and hypotonia without hypoglycemia, and muscle phosphorylase deficiency (McArdle disease) causes exercise intolerance.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["glycogen-storage-disease","von-gierke","metabolism"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Glycogen Storage Disease Type I (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534196/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-002","prompt":"A 9-month-old boy of Ashkenazi Jewish descent is losing motor milestones and has an exaggerated startle response to noise. Funduscopy shows bilateral cherry-red macular spots. There is no hepatosplenomegaly. Which substance accumulates in his neurons?","choices":["GM2 ganglioside","Sphingomyelin","Glucocerebroside","Galactocerebroside","Ceramide trihexoside"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Tay-Sachs disease is autosomal recessive hexosaminidase A deficiency causing GM2 ganglioside accumulation, with neurodegeneration, hyperacusis, and a cherry-red macula but no organomegaly. Niemann-Pick disease (sphingomyelinase deficiency, sphingomyelin accumulation) also shows a cherry-red spot but has prominent hepatosplenomegaly. Glucocerebroside accumulates in Gaucher disease (hepatosplenomegaly, bone crises), galactocerebroside in Krabbe disease (peripheral neuropathy, optic atrophy, globoid cells), and ceramide trihexoside in Fabry disease.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["lysosomal-storage-disease","tay-sachs","sphingolipidoses"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Tay-Sachs Disease (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564432/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-003","prompt":"A 3-day-old boy becomes lethargic and tachypneic after breastfeeding is established. Plasma ammonia is 410 µmol/L and blood pH is 7.47. Plasma citrulline is very low, and urinary orotic acid is markedly elevated. Which enzyme is most likely deficient?","choices":["Ornithine transcarbamylase","Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I","Argininosuccinate synthetase","Uridine monophosphate synthase","N-acetylglutamate synthase"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ornithine transcarbamylase (OTC) deficiency, the most common urea cycle disorder and X-linked, causes hyperammonemia with respiratory alkalosis and low citrulline; accumulated carbamoyl phosphate spills into pyrimidine synthesis, producing orotic aciduria. Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I and N-acetylglutamate synthase deficiencies also cause hyperammonemia with low citrulline, but orotic acid is normal or low because carbamoyl phosphate is not made. Argininosuccinate synthetase deficiency (citrullinemia) has markedly elevated citrulline. UMP synthase deficiency causes orotic aciduria with megaloblastic anemia but no hyperammonemia.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["urea-cycle-disorders","hyperammonemia","orotic-aciduria"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Ornithine Transcarbamylase Deficiency (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537257/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-004","prompt":"During an overnight fast, rising glucagon activates protein kinase A in hepatocytes. Which change most directly shifts the liver from glycolysis toward gluconeogenesis?","choices":["Increased fructose-2,6-bisphosphate levels","Dephosphorylation of pyruvate kinase","Decreased fructose-2,6-bisphosphate levels","Lower acetyl-CoA activating pyruvate carboxylase","Increased hepatic glucokinase activity"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"PKA phosphorylates the bifunctional enzyme PFK-2/FBPase-2, activating its phosphatase activity, so fructose-2,6-bisphosphate falls; this removes activation of PFK-1 and relieves inhibition of fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, favoring gluconeogenesis. PKA also phosphorylates (inactivating) hepatic pyruvate kinase, not dephosphorylates it. During fasting, fatty acid oxidation raises acetyl-CoA, which activates pyruvate carboxylase, so falling acetyl-CoA is backwards. Glucokinase activity rises in the fed state under insulin.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["metabolic-regulation","gluconeogenesis","glycolysis","fructose-2-6-bisphosphate"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Biochemistry, Gluconeogenesis (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544346/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-005","prompt":"A 2-year-old boy had severe neonatal hypotonia and poor feeding and now has hyperphagia, small hands, and hypogonadism. His mother was 41 years old at delivery. DNA methylation analysis at 15q11-q13 shows only the maternal pattern. A SNP chromosomal microarray shows normal copy number across chromosome 15 but long regions of homozygosity confined to that chromosome. Which mechanism explains his disorder?","choices":["Paternal uniparental disomy","Maternal uniparental disomy","Deletion on maternal chromosome 15","Maternal UBE3A loss of function","Paternal imprinting center defect"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Prader-Willi syndrome results from loss of paternally expressed genes at 15q11-q13: about 70% from a paternal deletion, about 25% from maternal uniparental disomy, and a few percent from imprinting center defects. A maternal-only methylation pattern confirms the diagnosis but not the mechanism. Normal copy number excludes a deletion, and long runs of homozygosity limited to chromosome 15 show that both homologs came from one parent, here the mother; maternal UPD usually follows trisomy rescue after maternal meiotic nondisjunction, which is why it is associated with advanced maternal age. Paternal UPD, a maternal deletion, and maternal UBE3A loss of function cause Angelman syndrome, not this phenotype or methylation pattern. A paternal imprinting center defect also gives maternal-only methylation without a large deletion, but inheritance is biparental, so chromosome-wide runs of homozygosity are absent.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["genomic-imprinting","prader-willi-syndrome","uniparental-disomy"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Prader-Willi Syndrome (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553161/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-006","prompt":"A newborn has severe hypotonia, respiratory insufficiency requiring ventilation, and bilateral clubfoot. His 28-year-old mother has mild facial weakness and early cataracts and cannot release her grip quickly after a handshake. Which genetic abnormality is most likely in the infant?","choices":["CGG repeat expansion in FMR1","CAG repeat expansion in HTT","CTG repeat expansion in DMPK","GAA repeat expansion in FXN","SMN1 exon 7 homozygous deletion"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The mother's grip myotonia, cataracts, and facial weakness indicate myotonic dystrophy type 1, an autosomal dominant CTG expansion in the 3′ untranslated region of DMPK. CTG repeats expand most dramatically through maternal transmission (anticipation), producing congenital myotonic dystrophy with neonatal hypotonia, respiratory failure, and clubfoot. Spinal muscular atrophy (SMN1 deletion) also causes neonatal hypotonia, but it is autosomal recessive and would not explain the mother's findings. CGG (fragile X), CAG (Huntington), and GAA (Friedreich ataxia) expansions do not cause this presentation.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["trinucleotide-repeats","myotonic-dystrophy","anticipation"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Congenital Myotonic Dystrophy (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560518/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-007","prompt":"A 41-year-old man has a right-sided colon adenocarcinoma; colonoscopy shows only 2 other adenomas. His mother had endometrial cancer at age 48, and a maternal uncle had colon cancer at age 44. The tumor is mucinous with dense tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes, and PCR of tumor DNA shows that several mononucleotide repeat tracts differ in length from those in his normal mucosa. Which DNA repair process is defective?","choices":["Base excision repair","Mismatch repair","Nucleotide excision repair","Homologous recombination repair","Nonhomologous end joining"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Lynch syndrome (hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer) is autosomal dominant loss of a mismatch repair gene (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, or PMS2). Unrepaired replication slippage changes the length of short repeat tracts in tumor DNA relative to normal tissue (microsatellite instability) and leads to early right-sided colon cancer with few polyps, often mucinous with tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes, and to endometrial cancer in relatives. Base excision repair defects (biallelic MUTYH) cause MUTYH-associated polyposis with dozens to hundreds of adenomas and are microsatellite-stable. Nucleotide excision repair defects cause xeroderma pigmentosum, homologous recombination defects (BRCA1/2) predispose mainly to breast, ovarian, prostate, and pancreatic cancer, and nonhomologous end joining defects cause radiosensitive immunodeficiency rather than hereditary colon cancer.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["dna-repair","lynch-syndrome","microsatellite-instability"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Hereditary Nonpolyposis Colon Cancer (Lynch Syndrome) (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564511/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-008","prompt":"A 26-year-old woman with classic phenylketonuria stopped her phenylalanine-restricted diet in adolescence; her current phenylalanine level is 18 mg/dL. She plans to conceive within the year. Her partner does not carry a PKU variant. Which counseling is most accurate?","choices":["Resume strict dietary control before conception to prevent fetal harm","No treatment is needed because the fetus will only be a heterozygous carrier","Begin dietary restriction once a pregnancy test is positive","Start a tyrosine-restricted diet to protect fetal brain development","Delay dietary changes until the third trimester, when the brain matures"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Maternal hyperphenylalaninemia is teratogenic regardless of fetal genotype, causing microcephaly, intellectual disability, congenital heart defects, and growth restriction (maternal PKU syndrome). Phenylalanine should be lowered to about 2–6 mg/dL (120–360 µmol/L) at least 3 months before conception and kept there throughout pregnancy, because organogenesis occurs before most pregnancies are recognized. A heterozygous fetus is still exposed to high maternal levels across the placenta. Tyrosine becomes an essential amino acid in PKU and is supplemented, not restricted.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["phenylketonuria","maternal-pku","preconception-counseling"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Phenylketonuria (PKU) (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535378/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-009","prompt":"A 52-year-old man with alcohol use disorder is brought in confused and ataxic, with horizontal nystagmus and bilateral lateral rectus palsies. Fingerstick glucose is 58 mg/dL. Which is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":["IV dextrose bolus, then oral thiamine","High-dose IV thiamine plus dextrose","Oral thiamine 100 mg daily","IM vitamin B12 with oral folate","Brain MRI before starting therapy"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia in a malnourished patient indicate Wernicke encephalopathy, a thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency affecting thiamine-dependent enzymes such as pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and transketolase. High-dose parenteral thiamine is given immediately, before or together with glucose, because a glucose load without thiamine can precipitate or worsen the encephalopathy; hypoglycemia should not go untreated while waiting. Oral thiamine is poorly absorbed in alcohol use disorder and is inadequate for acute disease. Wernicke encephalopathy is a clinical diagnosis, and imaging must not delay treatment.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["vitamin-deficiency","thiamine","wernicke-encephalopathy"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470344/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-010","prompt":"A healthy 30-year-old woman's brother has cystic fibrosis; her parents are unaffected. Her partner has no family history of CF and comes from a population in which CF affects 1 in 2,500 births. Before any carrier testing, what is the probability that their first child will have CF?","choices":["1 in 100","1 in 150","1 in 200","1 in 300","1 in 400"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Because she is unaffected and both parents are carriers, her chance of being a carrier is 2/3 (of the 3 unaffected genotypes, 2 are heterozygous). For her partner, q² = 1/2,500, so q = 1/50 and the carrier frequency 2pq ≈ 2 × 1/50 = 1/25. The child's risk is 2/3 × 1/25 × 1/4 = 1/150. Using 1/2 as her carrier risk gives 1/200, and using q (1/50) instead of 2pq for the partner gives 1/300.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["hardy-weinberg","autosomal-recessive","genetic-counseling","cystic-fibrosis","calculation"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cystic Fibrosis (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493206/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-011","prompt":"A 36-year-old woman at 17 weeks' gestation has a second-trimester quadruple screen with low AFP, low hCG, low unconjugated estriol, and normal inhibin A. Which condition does this pattern most strongly suggest?","choices":["Trisomy 21","Open neural tube defect","Twin gestation","Turner syndrome","Trisomy 18"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Trisomy 18 classically lowers AFP, hCG, and estriol, with normal inhibin A. Trisomy 21 lowers AFP and estriol but raises hCG and inhibin A. Open neural tube defects and multiple gestation raise maternal serum AFP rather than lowering it. A screen-positive result should be followed by counseling and an offer of diagnostic testing (amniocentesis with karyotype or microarray), with detailed ultrasound; cell-free DNA is a more accurate screening option but is not diagnostic.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["prenatal-screening","aneuploidy","trisomy-18"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Trisomy 18 (Edwards Syndrome) (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK570597/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-012","prompt":"A 49-year-old man has fatigue, arthralgias of the second and third MCP joints, and an ALT of 78 U/L. Transferrin saturation is 68%, ferritin 1,250 ng/mL, and he is homozygous for HFE C282Y. Hemoglobin is 15.1 g/dL. Liver biopsy shows iron deposition without cirrhosis. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral deferasirox chelation","Serial therapeutic phlebotomy","Subcutaneous deferoxamine infusions","Low-iron diet without phlebotomy","Liver transplantation evaluation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Hereditary hemochromatosis (autosomal recessive HFE C282Y homozygosity) causes increased intestinal iron absorption from low hepcidin. Weekly phlebotomy of about 500 mL until ferritin falls to about 50–100 ng/mL, followed by maintenance, is first-line because it is effective, inexpensive, and well tolerated when hemoglobin is normal. Chelators such as deferoxamine and deferasirox are reserved for patients who cannot undergo phlebotomy, such as those with anemia or transfusional iron overload. Dietary changes are adjunctive only, and transplantation is reserved for decompensated cirrhosis or hepatocellular carcinoma.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["hereditary-hemochromatosis","iron-overload","hfe"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Hemochromatosis (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430862/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-013","prompt":"A 14-year-old boy with tall stature, long fingers, and mild intellectual disability has inferonasal dislocation of both lenses. He recently had a deep vein thrombosis. Plasma total homocysteine and methionine are both markedly elevated. Which is the most appropriate initial therapy?","choices":["High-dose pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","Losartan to slow aortic root dilation","Dietary methionine supplementation","Hydroxocobalamin injections alone","Lifelong warfarin as sole therapy"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Homocystinuria from cystathionine beta-synthase (CBS) deficiency causes a marfanoid habitus with downward lens dislocation, intellectual disability, and thrombosis, with high homocysteine and high methionine. CBS requires pyridoxal phosphate, and about half of patients respond to high-dose pyridoxine; treatment also includes methionine restriction, betaine, and folate and B12 supplementation. Losartan targets aortic dilation in Marfan syndrome, which causes upward lens dislocation and normal homocysteine. Cobalamin alone treats remethylation defects, in which methionine is low rather than high, and anticoagulation does not correct the underlying metabolic defect.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["amino-acid-metabolism","homocystinuria","ectopia-lentis"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Homocystinuria (MedlinePlus Genetics, NIH)","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/condition/homocystinuria/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-014","prompt":"A 4-year-old boy has speech delay, hand flapping, poor eye contact, large ears, and a long face. His maternal grandfather developed an intention tremor and ataxia in his 60s. Which test is most appropriate to confirm the suspected diagnosis?","choices":["Chromosomal microarray analysis","Standard G-banded karyotype","PCR for FMR1 CGG repeat size","Whole-exome sequencing panel","FISH for a 15q11-q13 deletion"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Fragile X syndrome is caused by a CGG expansion of more than 200 repeats in the FMR1 5′ untranslated region with hypermethylation; the grandfather's late-onset tremor and ataxia suggest fragile X–associated tremor/ataxia syndrome from a premutation (55–200 repeats). Diagnosis uses PCR to size the repeat, with Southern blot or methylation PCR to assess large expansions and methylation status. Chromosomal microarray and exome sequencing cannot detect repeat expansions, and the cytogenetic fragile site seen on karyotype is insensitive and no longer used for diagnosis. FISH for 15q11-q13 evaluates Prader-Willi and Angelman deletions.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["fragile-x-syndrome","trinucleotide-repeats","genetic-testing","lab-techniques"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Fragile X Syndrome (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459243/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-015","prompt":"Investigators use CRISPR-Cas9 with a single guide RNA targeting exon 2 of a gene, without supplying a repair template, to knock out the gene in cultured cells. Which cellular process most often produces the inactivating mutations?","choices":["Homology-directed repair","Mismatch repair","Nonhomologous end joining","Nucleotide excision repair","Base excision repair"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The guide RNA directs Cas9 to the target sequence (next to a PAM site), where it makes a double-strand break. Without a template, the cell repairs the break by error-prone nonhomologous end joining, which introduces small insertions or deletions that cause frameshifts and premature stop codons, knocking out the gene. Homology-directed repair needs a supplied donor template and is used for precise edits or knock-ins. Mismatch, nucleotide excision, and base excision repair act on single-strand lesions, not double-strand breaks.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["crispr-cas9","gene-editing","dna-repair","lab-techniques"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"What are genome editing and CRISPR-Cas9? (MedlinePlus Genetics, NIH)","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/understanding/genomicresearch/genomeediting/"}]},{"id":"med-biochem-016","prompt":"A man with hemophilia A and a woman who is not a carrier and has no family history of bleeding disorders plan to have children. Which statement about their children is correct?","choices":["All sons will be affected","Half of daughters will be carriers","Half of sons will be affected","All daughters will be carriers","All children will be unaffected carriers"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In X-linked recessive inheritance, an affected father passes his X chromosome (carrying the F8 variant) to every daughter and his Y chromosome to every son. All daughters are therefore obligate heterozygous carriers, and all sons are unaffected and noncarriers because their X comes from the noncarrier mother. Male-to-male transmission does not occur in X-linked disorders. A 50% risk for sons applies when the mother, not the father, is the carrier.","subject":"biochemistryGenetics","tags":["inheritance-patterns","x-linked-recessive","hemophilia"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Genetics, X-Linked Inheritance (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557383/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-001","prompt":"A new rapid assay is evaluated in 1,000 adults against a gold-standard test. Of the 100 adults with the disease, 90 test positive. Of the 900 adults without the disease, 855 test negative. What is the positive predictive value of the assay?","choices":["33%","67%","90%","95%","99%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Build the 2×2 table: true positives 90, false negatives 10, true negatives 855, false positives 900 − 855 = 45. PPV = TP / (TP + FP) = 90 / 135 ≈ 67%. Sensitivity is 90/100 = 90% and specificity 855/900 = 95%; these describe test performance in people whose disease status is known, not the probability of disease after a positive result. NPV = 855/865 ≈ 99%, and 33% (45/135) is the proportion of positive results that are false.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["diagnostic-testing","positive-predictive-value","2x2-table","calculation"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Diagnostic Testing Accuracy: Sensitivity, Specificity, Predictive Values and Likelihood Ratios (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557491/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-002","prompt":"A physical examination finding has a sensitivity of 80% and a specificity of 90% for appendicitis. A physician estimates a 20% pretest probability of appendicitis in a patient who turns out to have the finding. What is the approximate post-test probability?","choices":["33%","50%","67%","80%","89%"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"LR+ = sensitivity / (1 − specificity) = 0.80 / 0.10 = 8. Pretest odds = 0.20 / 0.80 = 0.25, post-test odds = 0.25 × 8 = 2, and post-test probability = 2 / (1 + 2) ≈ 67%. 89% is the result if the pretest probability were 50% (odds 1 × 8 = 8, probability 8/9), and 80% simply restates the sensitivity. Likelihood ratios must be applied to odds, not directly to probabilities.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["likelihood-ratios","bayes-theorem","post-test-probability","calculation"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Diagnostic Testing Accuracy: Sensitivity, Specificity, Predictive Values and Likelihood Ratios (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557491/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-003","prompt":"Investigators identify 150 adults with mesothelioma and 300 adults without it from the same hospitals, then review occupational records for prior asbestos exposure. Exposure is found in 90 cases and 60 controls. Which measure of association, with its value, is appropriate?","choices":["Relative risk of 3.0","Odds ratio of 3.0","Relative risk of 6.0","Odds ratio of 6.0","Attributable risk of 0.40"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A case-control study samples on outcome, so disease incidence cannot be calculated and the odds ratio is the appropriate measure. OR = (a × d) / (b × c) = (90 × 240) / (60 × 60) = 21,600 / 3,600 = 6.0, where 240 is the number of unexposed controls (300 − 60). Dividing the proportion of cases exposed (60%) by the proportion of controls exposed (20%) gives 3.0, which is not a relative risk; relative and attributable risks require a cohort design that follows exposed and unexposed groups forward to measure incidence.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["case-control-study","odds-ratio","study-design","calculation"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Odds Ratio (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431098/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-004","prompt":"A new screening CT detects pancreatic cancer an average of 2 years before symptoms would appear. Median survival from diagnosis is 3.5 years in screened patients vs 1.5 years in unscreened patients, but age-specific pancreatic cancer mortality is identical in both groups. Which bias best explains the apparent survival benefit?","choices":["Lead-time bias","Length-time bias","Selection bias","Overdiagnosis bias","Recall bias"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Lead-time bias occurs when earlier detection moves the date of diagnosis forward without delaying death, so survival measured from diagnosis lengthens by roughly the lead time (2 years here) while mortality is unchanged. Comparing mortality rates rather than survival from diagnosis avoids it. Length-time bias arises because screening preferentially detects slow-growing, less aggressive tumors with long preclinical phases. Overdiagnosis refers to detecting cancers that would never have caused symptoms, and recall bias affects self-reported exposures in case-control studies.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["bias","lead-time-bias","screening"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Study Bias (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574513/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-005","prompt":"A randomized trial of 60 patients finds that a new antihypertensive lowers stroke incidence from 8% to 5%, with p = 0.41, and the authors conclude the drug has no effect. A later trial of 12,000 patients finds a similar effect size with p < 0.001. The first trial's conclusion most likely reflects which problem?","choices":["Type I error from multiple comparisons","Confounding by baseline imbalance","Regression to the mean","Type II error from inadequate power","Lead-time bias in event detection"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A type II error (beta) is failing to reject the null hypothesis when a true difference exists; its probability rises when the sample is small relative to the effect size and event rate, so power (1 − beta) is low. With 60 patients and a 3-percentage-point difference, the first trial was badly underpowered, and its nonsignificant result does not show 'no effect.' A type I error (alpha) is a false-positive finding, the opposite problem. Baseline confounding is possible in small trials but would not explain a consistent effect size that becomes significant with a larger sample.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["type-ii-error","statistical-power","sample-size"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Type I and Type II Errors and Statistical Power (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557530/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-006","prompt":"In a 3-year randomized trial, myocardial infarction occurred in 12% of the control group and in 8% of patients receiving a new drug. Which set of values is correct for the drug?","choices":["ARR 4%, RRR 50%, NNT 25","ARR 33%, RRR 4%, NNT 3","ARR 4%, RRR 33%, NNT 3","ARR 4%, RRR 33%, NNT 25","ARR 8%, RRR 67%, NNT 13"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Absolute risk reduction = 12% − 8% = 4%. Relative risk reduction = ARR / control event rate = 4/12 ≈ 33% (equivalently 1 − RR, where RR = 8/12 = 0.67). NNT = 1 / ARR = 1 / 0.04 = 25 patients treated for 3 years to prevent one MI. An RRR of 50% comes from dividing by the treatment-group rate instead of the control rate, and an NNT of 3 comes from inverting the RRR rather than the ARR. Note the duration: NNT is meaningful only for the stated time frame.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["absolute-risk-reduction","relative-risk-reduction","number-needed-to-treat","calculation"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Relative Risk (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430824/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-007","prompt":"In a randomized trial of supervised exercise vs usual care for depression, 30% of the exercise group stopped attending sessions, mostly because their mood worsened. The investigators analyze only participants who completed the exercise protocol and report a large benefit. Compared with analyzing all participants in their originally assigned groups, this approach most likely does what?","choices":["Overestimates benefit by undoing randomization","Underestimates benefit by diluting the effect","Reduces confounding by excluding nonadherent patients","Preserves randomization while increasing power","Converts the trial into a crossover design"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Per-protocol analysis drops participants who did not adhere; because dropout here was linked to worsening mood, the remaining exercise group is selected for better prognosis, which breaks the balance created by randomization and inflates the apparent benefit. Intention-to-treat analysis keeps everyone in the group to which they were randomized, preserving comparability and giving a conservative estimate of real-world effectiveness. Dilution toward the null is the characteristic effect of ITT, not of per-protocol analysis, and excluding nonadherent patients introduces rather than removes confounding.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["intention-to-treat","per-protocol-analysis","randomized-controlled-trial"],"focus":["step1"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Epidemiology of Study Design (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470342/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-008","prompt":"A trial tests whether oral antibiotics are noninferior to IV antibiotics for osteomyelitis, using a prespecified noninferiority margin of 10 percentage points for treatment failure. Treatment failure occurred in 14% of the oral group and 12% of the IV group: risk difference 2 percentage points (95% CI −3 to 7). Which conclusion is correct?","choices":["Oral therapy is noninferior to IV therapy","Oral therapy is inferior because failures were higher","The trial is inconclusive because the CI includes 0","Oral therapy is superior to IV therapy","Noninferiority requires a p-value under 0.05 for superiority"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Noninferiority is shown when the upper bound of the confidence interval for the excess failure rate (here 7 points) lies below the prespecified margin (10 points), which it does. A CI that includes 0 means superiority is not shown, but that is not the question a noninferiority trial asks. The 2-point excess in observed failures is a point estimate within the acceptable margin, not evidence of inferiority. Noninferiority trials should be analyzed both by intention-to-treat and per-protocol, because ITT dilution can bias toward a false finding of noninferiority.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["non-inferiority-trial","confidence-intervals","evidence-based-medicine"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Non-Inferiority Clinical Trials to Establish Effectiveness, Guidance for Industry (FDA)","url":"https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/non-inferiority-clinical-trials"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-009","prompt":"A 64-year-old man with schizophrenia, stable on medication, has a gangrenous toe, and amputation is recommended. He refuses. He accurately describes his condition and the risks of refusal, including sepsis and death, discusses alternatives, and consistently explains that he values keeping his body intact over living longer. Which action is most appropriate?","choices":["Request a court order to authorize the amputation","Ask his sister to consent as his surrogate decision-maker","Proceed, because psychiatric illness negates capacity","Delay all treatment until a psychiatrist evaluates him","Respect his informed refusal and keep offering care"],"correctIndex":4,"explanation":"Decision-making capacity is decision-specific and is shown when a patient can communicate a choice, understand the relevant information, appreciate how it applies to his situation, and reason consistently with his values—all of which he demonstrates. A psychiatric diagnosis does not by itself remove capacity, so a capacitated patient's refusal must be respected even when it may lead to death, while alternatives such as antibiotics and wound care continue to be offered. Surrogates and courts become relevant only when a patient lacks capacity. Capacity can be assessed by any physician, and withholding all treatment pending psychiatry would harm him.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["decision-making-capacity","informed-refusal","autonomy"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Competency and Capacity (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-010","prompt":"A 15-year-old girl who came to the clinic alone tests positive for Chlamydia trachomatis. She asks that her parents not be told. State law allows minors to consent to their own STI care and does not require parental notification. Which action is most appropriate?","choices":["Treat her, but notify her parents because she is under 16","Obtain parental consent before giving any antibiotics","Treat her confidentially and report the case to public health","Treat her and skip the public health report to protect privacy","Refer her to family court for an emancipation ruling"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"All US states allow minors to consent to diagnosis and treatment of sexually transmitted infections, so she can be treated without parental consent or notification, and her confidentiality should be preserved. Chlamydia is a nationally notifiable disease, however, so reporting to the health department is legally required regardless of age; public health reporting is a recognized exception to confidentiality and is not the same as telling her parents. Emancipation is unnecessary because STI care is a specific statutory exception for minors. Clinicians should also offer expedited partner therapy where permitted, counseling, and retesting in about 3 months.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["minors","confidentiality","reportable-diseases","adolescent-health"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines, 2021: Adolescents (CDC)","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/std/treatment-guidelines/adolescents.htm"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-011","prompt":"During a psychotherapy session, a 32-year-old man says he plans to shoot his former business partner, names him, and says he bought a rifle last week. He declines voluntary admission. In addition to evaluating him for involuntary hospitalization, what action is most appropriate?","choices":["Keep the threat confidential because he has not yet acted","Take reasonable steps to warn the partner and notify police","Document the statement and reassess him at the next visit","Warn the partner only if the patient gives permission","Report the threat to the state medical licensing board"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under the Tarasoff principle, adopted by most states as a duty to warn or protect, a clinician whose patient poses a serious threat of violence to an identifiable person must take reasonable steps to protect that person, such as warning the intended victim, notifying law enforcement, and/or hospitalizing the patient. A specific named target, a stated plan, and access to a weapon clearly meet this threshold. Confidentiality yields to the duty to protect, so neither the patient's permission nor an actual act of violence is required. Delaying until the next visit leaves the partner at risk, and the licensing board has no protective role.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["confidentiality","duty-to-warn","tarasoff"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Duty to Warn (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542236/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-012","prompt":"A 79-year-old woman with advanced dementia develops aspiration pneumonia and lacks decision-making capacity. Five years ago, she designated her niece as durable power of attorney for health care. Her two adult sons want her intubated; the niece reports that the patient repeatedly said she would never want a breathing tube. Whose decision should guide her care?","choices":["The sons, because they are her next of kin","The sons, because preserving life takes priority","The attending, because family members disagree","The niece, applying the patient's known wishes","A court-appointed guardian, given the conflict"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A health care agent named in a durable power of attorney takes precedence over the default surrogate hierarchy (spouse, then adult children, then parents, and so on), so the niece is the legal decision-maker. Surrogates use substituted judgment, deciding as the patient would have, and here the patient's stated wishes are known. Next-of-kin status does not override a valid designation, and a general preference for preserving life does not override the patient's own values. Ethics consultation can help with family conflict, but court involvement is a last resort and is not needed when a valid agent exists.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["surrogate-decision-making","advance-directives","health-care-proxy"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Advance Directives (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459133/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-013","prompt":"A hospitalized 58-year-old man received a double dose of enoxaparin because of an order-entry error. He has had no bleeding, and his hemoglobin is stable. He is alert and asks why a nurse is checking on him so often. What is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Say it is routine monitoring, since no harm has occurred","Disclose the error only if bleeding later develops","Ask risk management to speak with him instead of you","Explain the error, apologize, and describe the monitoring plan","Tell him the nurse caused it and file an incident report"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Patients should be told promptly and honestly about errors that reach them, including those that have not (yet) caused harm, especially when they change care, as the extra monitoring does here. Disclosure includes what happened, an apology, what is being done to protect the patient, and how recurrence will be prevented, followed by reporting through the institution's safety system. Waiting until harm occurs or describing the monitoring as routine is deceptive. Risk management can support but should not replace the treating physician's disclosure, and blaming an individual is inaccurate here and undermines a systems approach to safety.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["medical-errors","disclosure","patient-safety"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Disclosure of Errors (AHRQ Patient Safety Network)","url":"https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/disclosure-errors"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-014","prompt":"A 27-year-old woman at 38 weeks' gestation arrives at a private hospital's emergency department with regular contractions every 3 minutes; her cervix is 6 cm dilated. She is uninsured, and an administrator asks that she be transferred to the county hospital 40 minutes away. Under EMTALA, what is required?","choices":["Transfer after documenting reassuring fetal heart tones","Transfer once the receiving hospital agrees to accept","Stabilize her, which in active labor means delivery","Complete a financial screen before further evaluation","Transfer her by ambulance with a nurse in attendance"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"EMTALA requires any Medicare-participating hospital with an emergency department to provide a medical screening examination and stabilizing treatment regardless of ability to pay. For a woman in active labor, 'stabilized' means delivery of the infant and placenta, so transfer is permitted only if the patient requests it after being informed or a physician certifies that the medical benefits outweigh the risks—never for financial reasons. Reassuring fetal heart tones, acceptance by the receiving hospital, and appropriate transport are necessary conditions for a lawful transfer but are not sufficient on their own. Delaying screening or treatment to ask about insurance is prohibited.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["emtala","active-labor","patient-transfer"],"focus":["step2ck"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"EMTALA and Patient Transfers (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557812/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-015","prompt":"A rapid strep test with fixed sensitivity and specificity was validated in a pediatric clinic where 30% of children with sore throat have streptococcal pharyngitis. It is now used in an adult clinic where the prevalence is 10%. Compared with the pediatric clinic, what change is expected in the adult clinic?","choices":["Higher PPV and lower NPV","Lower sensitivity and higher specificity","Lower PPV with no change in NPV","Lower PPV and higher NPV","No change in PPV or NPV"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Sensitivity and specificity are properties of the test and do not change with prevalence. Predictive values do: as prevalence falls, a larger share of positive results are false positives, so PPV decreases, and a larger share of negative results are true negatives, so NPV increases. This is why screening tests perform differently in low-prevalence populations than in the referral settings where they were validated.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["predictive-values","prevalence","diagnostic-testing"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Diagnostic Testing Accuracy: Sensitivity, Specificity, Predictive Values and Likelihood Ratios (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557491/"}]},{"id":"med-biostat-016","prompt":"A meta-analysis of 8 randomized trials comparing a drug with placebo for preventing hospital readmission reports a pooled risk ratio of 0.88 (95% CI 0.79–0.98). Five of the individual trials have confidence intervals that cross 1.0. Which interpretation is correct?","choices":["The pooled result is invalid because most trials were nonsignificant","The drug increases readmission risk by 12%","The pooled estimate shows a significant risk reduction","The drug prevents readmission in 88% of patients","Heterogeneity must be low because most trials crossed 1.0"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"For a ratio measure, the null value is 1.0; a pooled 95% CI of 0.79–0.98 excludes 1.0, so the combined estimate shows a statistically significant 12% relative reduction in readmission (1 − 0.88). Pooling individually underpowered trials is precisely what a meta-analysis is for, so nonsignificant individual trials do not invalidate the summary diamond. A risk ratio of 0.88 is a relative reduction, not the proportion of patients helped. Heterogeneity is judged from the consistency of the trial estimates (for example, the I² statistic), not from whether individual CIs cross 1.0.","subject":"biostatisticsEthics","tags":["meta-analysis","forest-plot","confidence-intervals"],"focus":["foundations"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Hypothesis Testing, P Values, Confidence Intervals, and Significance (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557421/"}]}]};
